window.QUESTION_BANK = {"chapters":[{"n":1,"title":"Six Sigma and Organizational Goals"},{"n":2,"title":"Lean Principles in the Organization"},{"n":3,"title":"Design for Six Sigma (DFSS) Methodologies"},{"n":4,"title":"Project Identification"},{"n":5,"title":"Voice of the Customer (VOC)"},{"n":6,"title":"Project Management Basics"},{"n":7,"title":"Management and Planning Tools"},{"n":8,"title":"Business Results for Projects"},{"n":9,"title":"Team Dynamics and Performance"},{"n":10,"title":"Process Analysis and Documentation"},{"n":11,"title":"Probability and Statistics"},{"n":12,"title":"Statistical Distributions"},{"n":13,"title":"Collecting and Summarizing Data"},{"n":14,"title":"Measurement System Analysis (MSA)"},{"n":15,"title":"Process and Performance Capability"},{"n":16,"title":"Exploratory Data Analysis"},{"n":17,"title":"Hypothesis Testing"},{"n":18,"title":"Additional Analysis Methods"},{"n":19,"title":"Design of Experiments (DOE)"},{"n":20,"title":"Implementation Planning"},{"n":21,"title":"Lean Tools"},{"n":22,"title":"Statistical Process Control (SPC)"},{"n":23,"title":"Sustain Improvements"},{"n":24,"title":"Total Productive Maintenance / Lean Tools for Process Control"}],"questions":[{"id":"c1q1","ch":1,"n":1,"q":"According to the chapter, what is described as the real power or true source of Six Sigma's effectiveness?","o":["The exclusive use of advanced statistical tools unavailable in other methods","The use of many proven elements of other methods in tandem with managerial focus","The training of a large number of Black Belts in the earliest stages of deployment","The replacement of all existing control plans and standard operating procedures"],"a":1,"x":["The text states the real power of Six Sigma is using many parts/elements of other proven methods in tandem with managerial focus. Distractor A baits the misconception that Six Sigma is purely statistical (the chapter explicitly says statistical calculations are only a small portion). C and D are inverted truths — the chapter warns against over-training Black Belts early and explicitly says Six Sigma should not replace existing procedures. Classic ASQ DNA: distractors are statements the chapter directly contradicts.","Reference: Lines 37–42 (\"The real power of Six Sigma is the use of many parts or elements of other methods…\"); contrast with lines 759–763."]},{"id":"c1q2","ch":1,"n":2,"q":"The chapter identifies the most common reason organizations fail when attempting to implement Six Sigma. Which is it?","o":["Insufficient use of outside consultants","Training Green Belts before Black Belts","Management's lack of commitment to real process improvement","Failure to purchase adequate statistical software"],"a":2,"x":["Directly stated: \"the most common failure is management's lack of commitment to real process improvement.\" B is a near-trap — the chapter discusses Green-Belt-first training as a remedy, not a failure cause. A reverses the consultant dynamic.","Reference: Lines 51–58."]},{"id":"c1q3","ch":1,"n":3,"q":"Which quality pioneer is credited with developing the cause-and-effect diagram and was instrumental in establishing quality circles in Japan?","o":["W. Edwards Deming","Kaoru Ishikawa","Joseph M. Juran","Armand Feigenbaum"],"a":1,"x":["Attribution item — pure \"Understand\" recognition. Ishikawa is credited with the cause-and-effect (fishbone) diagram and quality circles. The distractors are all real pioneers with real contributions (Deming = Fourteen Points; Juran = Pareto/trilogy; Feigenbaum = TQC/hidden plant), the signature ASQ trap of offering correct-people-wrong-contribution.","Reference: Lines 477–500."]},{"id":"c1q4","ch":1,"n":4,"q":"A Green Belt reads that \"any departure from the nominal or target value for a characteristic represents a loss to society.\" Which pioneer's teaching does this reflect?","o":["Philip Crosby","Walter Shewhart","Genichi Taguchi","Dorian Shainin"],"a":2,"x":["Verbatim Taguchi loss function language (\"loss to society\"). Distractors are pioneers with distinct contributions (Crosby = zero defects; Shewhart = control charts; Shainin = Red X), mimicking the attribution-swap pattern.","Reference: Lines 583–602."]},{"id":"c1q5","ch":1,"n":5,"q":"All of the following are credited to W. Edwards Deming in the chapter except:","o":["The \"Fourteen Points\"","The \"Seven Deadly Diseases\"","The system of profound knowledge","The Pareto principle"],"a":3,"x":["Negative-stem attribution trap — the single most repeated ASQ pattern in the bank (see sample Q77). The Pareto principle belongs to Juran, not Deming. A, B, C are all genuine Deming contributions, making them correct-and-therefore-wrong in a \"except\" stem.","Reference: Deming items at lines 378–447; Pareto principle attributed to Juran at line 533."]},{"id":"c1q6","ch":1,"n":6,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the origin of the plan-do-check-act (PDCA) cycle as presented in the chapter?","o":["Deming originated PDCA and Shewhart later revised it to PDSA","Shewhart originated the cycle and Deming later updated it to PDSA","Juran originated PDCA as part of the Juran trilogy","Ishikawa originated PDCA through the Union of Japanese Scientists and Engineers"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter states Shewhart originated PDCA (per Deming's own credit) and Deming later updated it to PDSA. Distractor A is the reversed-originator trap — the most tempting wrong answer because both names are correct, only the order is wrong. C and D mis-attribute to other pioneers.","Reference: Lines 566–572."]},{"id":"c1q7","ch":1,"n":7,"q":"The concept of the \"hidden\" plant — the idea that so much extra work is performed correcting mistakes that there is effectively a hidden plant within any factory — is attributed to which pioneer?","o":["Joseph M. Juran","Armand Feigenbaum","Subir Chowdhury","D. H. Stamatis"],"a":1,"x":["\"Hidden plant\" is a defining Feigenbaum contribution. Juran (A) is the strongest distractor because he is a contemporary giant, but his markers are Pareto/trilogy. C and D are weaker plausibles.","Reference: Lines 467–476."]},{"id":"c1q8","ch":1,"n":8,"q":"The Juran trilogy consists of three managerial processes. Which set correctly names them?","o":["Quality planning, quality control, quality improvement","Plan, do, study, act","Define, measure, analyze","Prevention, appraisal, failure"],"a":0,"x":["Quality planning, quality control, quality improvement — stated twice. B (PDSA) and C (DMAIC subset) are real frameworks from elsewhere in the chapter, the ASQ trick of using true-but-wrong-concept distractors. D is the cost-of-quality categories.","Reference: Lines 504–505 and 524."]},{"id":"c1q9","ch":1,"n":9,"q":"Per the chapter, the philosophical perspective of Six Sigma views all work as processes that can be defined, measured, analyzed, improved, and controlled. This is generally expressed by which concept?","o":["SIPOC","y = f(x)","DPMO","SMART"],"a":1,"x":["y = f(x) (\"control the inputs, you control the outputs\"). A (SIPOC) and C (DPMO) are legitimate chapter terms used as wrong-concept distractors — note sample Q75 tests this exact concept's boundaries.","Reference: Lines 260–264."]},{"id":"c1q10","ch":1,"n":10,"q":"In simple metric terms, the chapter states that Six Sigma quality performance means which of the following (accounting for a 1.5-sigma shift)?","o":["3.4 defects per thousand opportunities","6 defects per million opportunities","3.4 defects per million opportunities","1.5 defects per million opportunities"],"a":2,"x":["3.4 DPMO with the 1.5-sigma shift, stated verbatim. Distractor A swaps \"million\" for \"thousand\" (unit trap); B injects the number 6; D recycles the 1.5 figure — all quantitative near-miss distractors typical of ASQ.","Reference: Lines 279–281."]},{"id":"c1q11","ch":1,"n":11,"q":"A company develops up to four vision statements indicating where it should be in the next five years, then builds company goals and work plans around them, conducting periodic audits to monitor progress. This technique is called:","o":["Balanced scorecard","Hoshin planning","SWOT analysis","Benchmarking"],"a":1,"x":["Hoshin planning, described exactly. Note the chapter says \"up to four\" vision statements (sample bank Q90 says \"multiple\"/\"five years\" — be alert that the handbook wording is four). Distractors A, C, D are all real org-tools from this chapter, the wrong-concept pattern.","Reference: Lines 998–1005."]},{"id":"c1q12","ch":1,"n":12,"q":"Operating a system at less than its best — where changes may optimize individual processes but degrade the system as a whole — is referred to in the chapter as:","o":["Suboptimization","Reengineering","Continual improvement","Variation reduction"],"a":0,"x":["Suboptimization defined verbatim. Sample bank Q91 confirms ASQ tests this concept directly. Distractors are real chapter terms representing different concepts.","Reference: Lines 1032–1034."]},{"id":"c1q13","ch":1,"n":13,"q":"The balanced scorecard, as described in the chapter, includes financial and nonfinancial metrics across four perspectives. Which set correctly lists them?","o":["Suppliers, inputs, processes, outputs","Financial, customer, internal processes, employee learning and growth","Prevention, appraisal, internal failure, external failure","Strengths, weaknesses, opportunities, threats"],"a":1,"x":["Four balanced-scorecard perspectives stated explicitly. The trap is D (SWOT) and C (cost-of-quality categories) — both are genuine four-part frameworks from this same chapter, engineered to collide in recall. Sample Q87 tests this identically.","Reference: Lines 1291–1296."]},{"id":"c1q14","ch":1,"n":14,"q":"Regarding the three terms key process indicator, key performance indicator, and process performance indicator, what does the chapter conclude?","o":["They are precisely defined and clearly distinct from one another","It is hard to distinguish between the three terms","Only key performance indicator applies at the Green Belt level","They each measure a different one of the four balanced scorecard perspectives"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter openly admits \"It is hard to distinguish between these three terms.\" This is a comprehension item rewarding the reader who noticed the author's hedge. A is the intuitive-but-false answer (we assume terms are precise); C overstates the chapter's note that GBs typically operate at the \"key process indicator\" level; D fabricates a scorecard linkage.","Reference: Lines 1327–1335."]},{"id":"c1q15","ch":1,"n":15,"q":"A Green Belt is told that the SMART acronym for goal-setting \"most commonly\" stands for which of the following?","o":["Specific, Measurable, Attainable, Relevant, Time-bound","Strategic, Measurable, Achievable, Realistic, Timely","Specific, Manageable, Attainable, Reportable, Time-bound","Simple, Measurable, Actionable, Relevant, Tracked"],"a":0,"x":["Verbatim acronym expansion. Every distractor swaps one or two words (Strategic/Simple, Manageable, Realistic-vs-Relevant, Timely/Tracked) — the single-word-substitution distractor pattern that forces precise recall, exactly mirroring the sample bank's tight phrasing.","Reference: Lines 1238–1240."]},{"id":"c1q16","ch":1,"n":16,"q":"Six Sigma was first started at which company, then developed further into what we know today at which company?","o":["Started at General Electric, developed at Motorola","Started at Motorola, developed at General Electric","Started at Ford, developed at Motorola","Started at Western Electric, developed at General Electric"],"a":1,"x":["Verbatim: Six Sigma \"was first started at Motorola and was then developed more into what we know today at General Electric.\" Distractor A is the reversed-order trap (both companies correct, sequence wrong) — the highest-yield ASQ pattern. C and D inject Ford and Western Electric, both real entities elsewhere in the chapter (Ford SQI; Shewhart's Western Electric Hawthorne plant), making them seductive wrong-concept lures.","Reference: Lines 718–719."]},{"id":"c1q17","ch":1,"n":17,"q":"According to the chapter, which pioneer originated the zero defects concept?","o":["Philip Crosby","Armand Feigenbaum","Subir Chowdhury","Joseph M. Juran"],"a":0,"x":["Crosby \"originated the zero defects concept.\" Classic attribution item at the Understand level. Feigenbaum (TQC), Chowdhury (quality prophet/DFSS), and Juran (Pareto/trilogy) are correct-people-wrong-contribution distractors.","Reference: Lines 346–348."]},{"id":"c1q18","ch":1,"n":18,"q":"When the supplier and customer are added to the basic input-transformation-output process definition to help define the boundaries of what is to be studied, the resulting identification is called:","o":["DMAIC","SIPOC","PDCA","y = f(x)"],"a":1,"x":["SIPOC = suppliers, inputs, process, outputs, customers, used \"especially to help define the boundaries of what is to be studied.\" Distractors A, C, D are all genuine chapter acronyms representing different concepts — the wrong-concept distractor pattern.","Reference: Lines 724–727."]},{"id":"c1q19","ch":1,"n":19,"q":"In the DMAIC model as described, which phase is defined as \"monitor the system to sustain the gains\"?","o":["Measure","Analyze","Improve","Control"],"a":3,"x":["Control = \"Monitor the system to sustain the gains\" (verbatim). Each distractor is an adjacent DMAIC phase with its own definition (Measure=collect data; Analyze=study data; Improve=take action). ASQ exploits phase-confusion by offering all neighbors as plausible.","Reference: Lines 838–844."]},{"id":"c1q20","ch":1,"n":20,"q":"The chapter distinguishes Six Sigma from lean. Which statement correctly reflects that distinction?","o":["Six Sigma drives out waste while lean reduces process variation","Six Sigma reduces process variation while lean drives out waste and promotes work standardization","Both focus exclusively on reducing variation","Lean focuses on statistical control while Six Sigma focuses on value stream mapping"],"a":1,"x":["Six Sigma reduces variation; lean drives out waste and promotes standardization/value stream mapping. Distractor A is the swapped-attribute trap (correct verbs, wrong owners). C and D are direct contradictions of the text.","Reference: Lines 287–290."]},{"id":"c1q21","ch":1,"n":21,"q":"Figure 1.4 categorizes inputs to a process step. Which of the following is listed as an input category rather than an output?","o":["Products","Services","Measurement system","Customer satisfaction"],"a":2,"x":["Figure 1.4 lists the \"Six M's\" inputs — Man, Machine, Methods, Mother Nature, Management, Materials, Measurement system. Products and Services (A, B) are explicitly outputs. D is not a Figure 1.4 element at all. Sample bank Q33 and Q47 test this input/output boundary identically.","Reference: Lines 702–714."]},{"id":"c1q22","ch":1,"n":22,"q":"The cost-benefit analysis (cost of quality) classifies relevant expenses into four categories. Which set correctly names them?","o":["Prevention, appraisal, internal failure, external failure","Direct, indirect, fixed, variable","Planning, control, improvement, sustainment","Labor, materials, overhead, rework"],"a":0,"x":["The four cost-of-quality categories stated verbatim: prevention, appraisal, internal failure, external failure. Distractors borrow real accounting vocabulary (B, D) and the Juran-trilogy verbs (C) to create wrong-concept lures. Sample bank Q99 confirms ASQ tests this exact four-part set.","Reference: Lines 929–930."]},{"id":"c1q23","ch":1,"n":23,"q":"The chapter repeatedly references managers searching for a \"magic bullet.\" In context, this phrase most nearly describes:","o":["A validated statistical tool guaranteed to reduce variation","A single quick-fix solution managers hope will keep the organization in business","The DMAIC road map for continual improvement","The expected-profit formula used in project selection"],"a":1,"x":["\"Magic bullet\" is the single quick-fix managers hope will keep them in business; the chapter explicitly says the process \"is not meant to be a quick fix (magic bullet) approach.\" Distractor A baits the statistical-tool misconception; C and D substitute legitimate chapter constructs for the colloquial phrase.","Reference: Lines 109–110 and 737–740."]},{"id":"c1q24","ch":1,"n":24,"q":"Which of the following is identified in the chapter as one of the key business drivers used to present performance information to executives and staff?","o":["Lessons learned","Customer (customer performance)","Storyboards","Closure meetings"],"a":1,"x":["Customer (customer performance) heads the chapter's list of key drivers. The trap: A, C, D are all real chapter items but belong to project documentation/closure (lessons learned, storyboards, closure meetings), not the organizational drivers/metrics topic (I.A.3). ASQ deliberately pulls distractors from a neighboring subtopic to test whether you can place a term in the correct BoK box.","Reference: Lines 1266–1267 (drivers list); contrast closure/documentation terms elsewhere."]},{"id":"c2q1","ch":2,"n":1,"q":"Toyota is usually credited with creating the lean concept under the Toyota Production System. According to the chapter, from whom does Toyota credit having learned the process?","o":["W. Edwards Deming","The Ford Motor Company","General Motors at the NUMMI plant","Shigeo Shingo"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter states Toyota credits learning the process from Ford. Correct DNA: an attribution trap. Distractor C (NUMMI/GM) is the same chapter's famous example of TPS success — a plausible neighbor placed to catch a skimmer. D (Shingo) appears later for autonomation/SMED.","Reference: Lines 12–15 (\"they credit having learned the process from the Ford Motor Company\")."]},{"id":"c2q2","ch":2,"n":2,"q":"A green belt labels a process step \"value-added.\" Per the chapter's definition, which of the following is NOT one of the three criteria for a value-added step?","o":["The customer recognizes the value","It changes (transforms) the product","It is done right the first time","It minimizes the changeover time"],"a":3,"x":["The three criteria are customer recognizes value, transforms the product, done right first time. D (changeover time) is a SMED concept inserted as the \"correct definition of the wrong concept\" trap.","Reference: Lines 88–93."]},{"id":"c2q3","ch":2,"n":3,"q":"A gas furnace manufacturer must fire-test every furnace to comply with CSA requirements, and customers are willing to pay for the CSA listing. According to the chapter, how should this test step be classified?","o":["Non-value-added, because all inspection is waste","Value-added, because the customer is willing to pay for the listing","Excess processing (overprocessing)","A gray area that cannot be classified"],"a":1,"x":["Customer willingness to pay for the CSA listing makes the test value-added. Distractor A overgeneralizes (\"all inspection is waste\") — the exam loves absolutist distractors. C and D are plausible because the chapter does call inspection a gray area generally — but in this specific furnace case it is explicitly value-added.","Reference: Lines 135–138."]},{"id":"c2q4","ch":2,"n":4,"q":"Which lean tool is described as a workplace organization method, and is identified as \"one of the first tools to apply in the path to achieving lean enterprise organizations\"?","o":["Kanban","SMED","5S","Total Productive Maintenance"],"a":2,"x":["5S is explicitly named as among the first tools toward lean enterprise. All distractors are legitimate lean tools (neighbor trap), but only 5S carries the \"first tool\" designation in the text.","Reference: Lines 143, 153–154."]},{"id":"c2q5","ch":2,"n":5,"q":"During which step of 5S would a green belt develop checklists, standards, and work instructions to keep the work area in a clean and orderly condition?","o":["Sort","Set in order","Standardize","Sustain"],"a":2,"x":["Standardize = checklists, standards, work instructions. The high-frequency ASQ trap pits Standardize against Sustain (D); note Sustain is about maintaining effort, not creating documentation. This exact distinction appears repeatedly in the sample bank (Q53, Q66).","Reference: Lines 179–182."]},{"id":"c2q6","ch":2,"n":6,"q":"According to the John Casey \"5S Shakeup\" article cited in the chapter, after establishing visible scoreboards, where should a typical North American organization begin its 5S effort instead of starting with Sort?","o":["Shine","Set in order","Standardize","Sustain"],"a":2,"x":["Casey's secret #3 says start at step 4 (Standardize) so people know how to change their scores. A detail-recall trap; B (Set in order) is the literal step-2 distractor for the unwary.","Reference: Lines 197–199."]},{"id":"c2q7","ch":2,"n":7,"q":"Carlos is implementing kanban at his facility. Based on the chapter's guidance, which of the following fundamentals should already be in place BEFORE venturing into kanban?","o":["Hoshin Kanri, A3 reporting, and Gemba walks","5S, standard work, total productive maintenance, and variation reduction","DMADV, IDOV, and DFSS","SMED, heijunka, and andon"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter explicitly lists 5S, standard work, TPM, and variation reduction as prerequisites. Distractor D lists real lean tools but not the stated prerequisites — a \"right family, wrong members\" trap.","Reference: Lines 317–319."]},{"id":"c2q8","ch":2,"n":8,"q":"A production scheduler purposely produces in much smaller batches by sequencing (mixing) product variants within the same process, reducing lead times and lowering inventory. Which lean concept is being applied?","o":["Just-in-Time","Heijunka","Takt time","One-piece flow"],"a":1,"x":["Heijunka = leveling via small mixed batches. The trap: JIT (A) is the broader strategy and tempting, but \"sequencing/mixing variants in smaller batches\" is specifically Heijunka. Mirrors sample Q98 where Heijunka is distinguished from JIT.","Reference: Lines 260–263."]},{"id":"c2q9","ch":2,"n":9,"q":"Which concept aligns the goals of the company (strategy) with the plans of middle management (tactics) and the work performed on the operations floor (action)?","o":["Hoshin Kanri (policy deployment)","Heijunka","Andon","Standard work"],"a":0,"x":["Hoshin Kanri = policy deployment aligning strategy/tactics/action. Clean definitional item at the Apply/Understand boundary.","Reference: Lines 264–268."]},{"id":"c2q10","ch":2,"n":10,"q":"The chapter describes a concept rooted in \"why have a human do what a machine can do better,\" in which machines must be able to detect and correct their own operating problems. What is this concept called?","o":["Poka-yoke","Jidoka (autonomation)","Gemba","Kanban"],"a":1,"x":["Jidoka/autonomation. Distractor A (poka-yoke) is the most seductive neighbor since both are quality-at-source tools — but poka-yoke is mistake-proofing, not machines self-detecting/correcting.","Reference: Lines 269–277."]},{"id":"c2q11","ch":2,"n":11,"q":"A green belt wants to understand a real-world operational issue and is advised to leave the office and observe the operations floor firsthand. This philosophy is also known as \"management by walking around.\" What is it called?","o":["Hoshin Kanri","Gemba","A3","Visual factory"],"a":1,"x":["Gemba = \"the real place,\" MBWA, \"Look and See.\" Straightforward recognition; distractors are co-located TPS terms.","Reference: Lines 252–259."]},{"id":"c2q12","ch":2,"n":12,"q":"The phrase \"single minute\" in Single Minute Exchange of Die (SMED) refers to which of the following?","o":["Every changeover must take exactly one minute","Changeovers should take less than 10 minutes (single-digit minutes)","Setup must be completed within 60 seconds of a signal","Only one die may be exchanged per cycle"],"a":1,"x":["\"Single minute\" = single-digit minutes, i.e., under 10 minutes. Distractor A is the literal-reading trap the chapter explicitly warns against.","Reference: Lines 353–355."]},{"id":"c2q13","ch":2,"n":13,"q":"Standard work is defined in the chapter as having three components. Which of the following is one of them?","o":["Standard range","Standard sequence","Standard tolerance","Standard rate"],"a":1,"x":["Three components = standard time, standard inventory, standard sequence. Distractor A (standard range) is the precise trap used in sample Q101 — memorize that \"range\" is the fake.","Reference: Lines 356–358."]},{"id":"c2q14","ch":2,"n":14,"q":"Takt time is derived from the German word taktzeit. According to the chapter, what is the primary purpose of takt time?","o":["To minimize machine changeover time","To precisely match production with demand","To eliminate work-in-process inventory entirely","To detect special cause variation"],"a":1,"x":["Purpose of takt time = match production with demand (\"heartbeat\"). Mirrors sample Q63. Distractor C overshoots into JIT/inventory language.","Reference: Lines 371–376."]},{"id":"c2q15","ch":2,"n":15,"q":"The Theory of Constraints lists five steps to system improvement. After \"Identify,\" which step uses kaizen or other methods to improve the rate of the constraining process?","o":["Exploit","Subordinate","Elevate","Repeat"],"a":0,"x":["Sequence is Identify Exploit Subordinate Elevate Repeat. Exploit = improve the constraint's rate. The trap relies on confusing Exploit (improve constraint) with Elevate (major revision/investment).","Reference: Lines 385–399."]},{"id":"c2q16","ch":2,"n":16,"q":"In Goldratt's Drum-Buffer-Rope analogy of soldiers marching in single file, what does the \"drum\" represent?","o":["The size of the buffer between processes","The speed of the slowest soldier (the constraint)","The rope connecting the lead and slowest soldiers","The lead time for the squad to pass a point"],"a":1,"x":["Drum = speed of slowest soldier/constraint. Distractors B-vs-A-vs-C test whether the candidate can separate drum (constraint pace), rope (linkage), and buffer (WIP). High-value conceptual cluster; mirrors sample Q70.","Reference: Lines 416–419, 429–432."]},{"id":"c2q17","ch":2,"n":17,"q":"In the Drum-Buffer-Rope analogy, what determines the length of the rope (the size of the buffer)?","o":["The speed of the fastest soldier","The dependability of the individual processes (tendency to drop rifles)","The total number of soldiers in the squad","The distance between the first and last soldier at rest"],"a":1,"x":["Rope length = buffer size = determined by process dependability (\"tendency to drop rifles\"). A subtle Analyze-level item that forces relating cause (variability) to effect (buffer sizing).","Reference: Lines 428–432."]},{"id":"c2q18","ch":2,"n":18,"q":"A manager wants to track overall equipment effectiveness (OEE). According to the chapter, OEE is the product of which three factors?","o":["Availability, performance, and quality","Speed, accuracy, and uptime","Reliability, maintainability, and availability","Cost, quality, and delivery"],"a":0,"x":["OEE = Availability × Performance × Quality. Distractor C (reliability/maintainability/availability) is a real reliability-engineering trio inserted as a high-plausibility lure.","Reference: Lines 326–329, 448–450."]},{"id":"c2q19","ch":2,"n":19,"q":"A green belt is reviewing the eight categories of waste (muda). Which of the following is the principal symptom of overproduction?","o":["Excessive material transport","Excess work-in-process (WIP)","Ergonomic injury","Long setup times"],"a":1,"x":["Principal symptom of overproduction = excess WIP. Distractor D (long setup times) is listed as a cause, not a symptom — the classic cause-vs-symptom inversion. Mirrors sample Q58.","Reference: Lines 470–473."]},{"id":"c2q20","ch":2,"n":20,"q":"A welding operation is routinely followed by a grinding step to remove weld imperfections; improving the welding process could eliminate the need for grinding. Which category of waste does the unnecessary grinding represent?","o":["Waiting","Excess motion","Excess processing (overprocessing)","Defect correction"],"a":2,"x":["Unnecessary grinding = excess processing. Distractor D (defect correction) is tempting because grinding \"fixes\" weld imperfections — but the chapter explicitly classifies eliminable downstream steps as overprocessing.","Reference: Lines 564–567."]},{"id":"c2q21","ch":2,"n":21,"q":"Which of the following is the lean tool that error-proofs a process — for example, electrical plugs that cannot be inserted the wrong way, or fixtures that prevent loading a component in the wrong orientation?","o":["Poka-yoke","Jidoka","Andon","Kanban"],"a":0,"x":["Poka-yoke = mistake-proofing (plugs, fixtures). Distractor B (Jidoka) is the deliberate mirror-image of Q10 — the two are perpetually swapped on the real exam.","Reference: Lines 334–339."]},{"id":"c2q22","ch":2,"n":22,"q":"A green belt wants to show the physical flow or path that people, products, or paperwork take during normal operations, and the output tends to look very messy before improvement. Which tool is being described?","o":["Value stream map","Spaghetti diagram","SIPOC","Process capability chart"],"a":1,"x":["Spaghetti diagram = path/flow of people/products/paperwork, looks messy. Distractor A (VSM) is the strongest lure since both are flow-mapping tools — but VSM maps material+information flow with timing, not the physical movement path. Mirrors sample Q31.","Reference: Lines 632–639."]},{"id":"c2q23","ch":2,"n":23,"q":"According to the chapter, a value stream often starts and ends at which points?","o":["From the first process step to the last process step","From the supplier's supplier to the customer's customer","From raw material receipt to finished goods warehouse","From order entry to invoice payment"],"a":1,"x":["Value stream runs supplier's supplier customer's customer. Distractors C and D describe narrower internal scopes — true-but-incomplete, the exam's favorite \"scope\" trap (see sample Q4, Q88).","Reference: Lines 656–658."]},{"id":"c2q24","ch":2,"n":24,"q":"When analyzing a value stream map, the chapter identifies the first key target for reduction or elimination. The chapter notes inventory increases costs for several reasons. Which of the following is NOT listed as a reason inventory increases cost?","o":["Storage space may be expensive","Quality may deteriorate (rust, spoilage)","Money invested could be used more productively elsewhere","Takt time increases as inventory grows"],"a":3,"x":["Listed reasons: expensive storage, quality deterioration, delayed design changes, money tied up, hidden defects. D (takt time rising with inventory) is a fabricated relationship — takt time is set by demand, not inventory. This is the \"invented causal link\" distractor pattern.","Reference: Lines 698–748."]},{"id":"c3q1","ch":3,"n":1,"q":"DFSS is best characterized as a strategic process that focuses on which of the following?","o":["Reactive design quality","Proactive design quality","Statistical process control of existing lines","Containment of nonconforming product"],"a":1,"x":["DFSS focuses on proactive (not reactive) design quality.","DNA: \"A\" is the verbatim antonym placed adjacent to the key — classic single-word reversal. C/D describe reactive/containment activities (the opposite philosophy).","Ref: Lines 23–24 (\"DFSS is a business/engineering strategic process that focuses on proactive design quality, rather than reactive design quality\")."]},{"id":"c3q2","ch":3,"n":2,"q":"When the common DFSS tools are applied across the IDOV phases, the chapter states this is commonly described as which relationship to DMAIC?","o":["IDOV replaces the entire DMAIC cycle","IDOV feeds the Define of DMAIC","IDOV feeds the MAIC of DMAIC","IDOV runs in parallel with the Control phase only"],"a":2,"x":["\"Doing IDOV feeds the MAIC of DMAIC.\"","DNA: \"B\" (feeds Define) is the plausible near-miss; the Define phase has no IDOV counterpart, which is the discriminator. A/D overstate the relationship.","Ref: Line 39."]},{"id":"c3q3","ch":3,"n":3,"q":"A supplier is asked to document the mean time between failures (MTBF) rather than the mean time to failures (MTTF) for a component. What does this most directly indicate about the component?","o":["It is a nonrepairable item","It is a repairable item","It has zero failure rate","It is exempt from life cycle testing"],"a":1,"x":["MTBF is used for repairable items; MTTF for nonrepairable.","DNA: \"A\" swaps the pair — the exact distinction the BoK loves to invert. C/D are unrelated true-sounding traps.","Ref: Lines 163–164."]},{"id":"c3q4","ch":3,"n":4,"q":"Per the chapter, a basic relationship between failure rate (λ) and reliability metrics is correctly expressed as:","o":["λ = MTBF","λ = 1/MTBF","MTBF = λ²","λ = MTBF × MTTF"],"a":1,"x":["λ = 1/MTBF (and λ = 1/MTTF).","DNA: Distractors mangle the reciprocal relationship. Low-math, definition-as-formula item.","Ref: Lines 166–167."]},{"id":"c3q5","ch":3,"n":5,"q":"A green belt is linking the design to the voice of the customer, identifying CTQ variables and specification limits, and assigning roles and milestones. Which IDOV phase is being executed?","o":["Identify","Design","Optimize","Validate"],"a":0,"x":["Linking design to VOC + CTQ + spec limits + roles/milestones = Identify phase.","DNA: Mirrors Sample Q72/Q85 structure — every distractor is a real IDOV phase, correctly named but wrong stage.","Ref: Lines 78–84."]},{"id":"c3q6","ch":3,"n":6,"q":"In the IDOV methodology, in which phase is error-proofing performed?","o":["Identify","Design","Optimize","Validate"],"a":2,"x":["Error-proofing is an Optimize -phase task.","DNA: Direct parallel to Sample Q72 (\"IDOV error proofing Optimize\"). Design (B) is the strongest distractor since it also contains FMEA/risk work.","Ref: Line 100."]},{"id":"c3q7","ch":3,"n":7,"q":"Which activity belongs to the Validate phase of IDOV?","o":["Formulate concept design","Establish statistical tolerancing","Prototype test and validation","Identify technical requirements"],"a":2,"x":["Prototype test and validation = Validate phase.","DNA: A = Design, B = Optimize, D = Identify — each is a correctly worded task from the wrong phase.","Ref: Lines 103–105."]},{"id":"c3q8","ch":3,"n":8,"q":"According to ISO 9000:2015, validation is the confirmation through objective evidence that:","o":["Specified requirements have been fulfilled","The requirements for a specific intended use or application have been fulfilled","The product passed a third-party financial audit","The RPN has been reduced below the cutoff score"],"a":1,"x":["Validation = confirmation that requirements for a specific intended use/application are fulfilled.","DNA: \"A\" is the definition of verification (3.8.12) — the highest-value trap here. This verification/validation swap is a known ASQ favorite.","Ref: Lines 236–238 (validation); contrast lines 229–231 (verification)."]},{"id":"c3q9","ch":3,"n":9,"q":"Breyfogle states the DMADV approach is appropriate instead of DMAIC under which condition?","o":["The process exists and is already meeting customer needs","A product/process is not in existence and needs to be developed, or exists and is optimized but still doesn't meet needs","The project is too small to justify a charter","The team only needs to sustain a prior improvement"],"a":1,"x":["DMADV is used when no product/process exists, or one exists and is optimized but still misses needs.","DNA: \"A\" and \"D\" describe situations where DMAIC/sustain would apply instead — testing whether you know when to switch roadmaps.","Ref: Lines 110–116."]},{"id":"c3q10","ch":3,"n":10,"q":"A point-of-sale system is designed so a sale can be completed within five minutes. In the design-objectives traceability matrix, this maps to which design objective?","o":["Maintainability","Security","Efficiency","Extended functionality"],"a":2,"x":["Five-minute POS completion Efficiency in the traceability matrix.","DNA: Pulled straight from Table 3.1. Sample Q94 used this exact matrix; distractors are the matrix's other rows (a near-identical item exists in your sample set).","Ref: Lines 210–212 (Table 3.1)."]},{"id":"c3q11","ch":3,"n":11,"q":"An auction website goes blank during peak periods because it cannot handle 100,000 concurrent users at month-end. Which \"Design for X\" attribute was inadequately addressed?","o":["Design for Agility","Design for Scalability","Design for Security","Design for Maintainability"],"a":1,"x":["Inability to handle a user-load surge = failure of Design for Scalability.","DNA: \"A\" (Agility) is the seductive distractor — both relate to responsiveness, but agility concerns customized delivery speed, not load capacity.","Ref: Lines 192–196."]},{"id":"c3q12","ch":3,"n":12,"q":"\"Cutting edge\" and \"latest and greatest,\" exemplified by aircraft designed faster than the speed of sound, describe which design objective?","o":["Design for Robustness","Design for Performance","Design for Compliance","Design for Usability"],"a":1,"x":["Aggressive benchmarks / \"latest and greatest\" / supersonic aircraft = Design for Performance.","DNA: Replicates Sample Q102 nearly verbatim. Robustness (A) is the trap (life-cycle testing, not benchmarks).","Ref: Lines 183–187."]},{"id":"c3q13","ch":3,"n":13,"q":"Per the chapter, which set of characteristics is associated with Design for Maintainability?","o":["Password encryption and access control","Modularity, decoupling, and component standardization","Aggressive benchmarks and breakthroughs","Nimble architecture and a ready array of vendors"],"a":1,"x":["Maintainability = modularity, decoupling, component standardization.","DNA: Each distractor is the signature phrase of another DFX attribute (security, performance, agility) — definition-of-wrong-concept pattern.","Ref: Line 157."]},{"id":"c3q14","ch":3,"n":14,"q":"Risk in an FMEA is defined in terms of three aspects. Which set correctly pairs them?","o":["Severity (impact), Occurrence (probability), Detection (event)","Severity (event), Occurrence (impact), Detection (probability)","Severity (probability), Occurrence (detection), Detection (impact)","Cost, schedule, and scope"],"a":0,"x":["Severity = impact, Occurrence = probability, Detection = event.","DNA: B/C scramble the pairings; D substitutes the PM triple constraint — a cross-domain distractor.","Ref: Line 257."]},{"id":"c3q15","ch":3,"n":15,"q":"A failure mode could cause serious injury to a user if the product is misused. On a 1–10 scale, the chapter indicates this should yield a severity rating of approximately:","o":["1 or 2","4 or 5","6 or 7","9 or 10"],"a":3,"x":["Potential harm to workers/customers severity 9 or 10.","DNA: Low-math interpretive item; distractors walk down the scale to bait under-rating.","Ref: Lines 876–877; cf. Table 3.3 (Hazardous = 9–10)."]},{"id":"c3q16","ch":3,"n":16,"q":"During an FMEA, an operator can obviously see the error every time it occurs. On a 1–10 scale, what detection rating does the chapter indicate?","o":["A high number such as 9 or 10","A low number such as 1 or 2","A mid-range number such as 5 or 6","Detection is not scored when the error is visible"],"a":1,"x":["Easily detected error low detection number (1 or 2). Reversal trap.","DNA: \"A\" exploits the intuitive (wrong) assumption that \"good detection = high score.\" Detection is scored inversely: poor detectability earns the high number. This is the single most-tested counterintuitive point in this chapter — tab it.","Ref: Lines 881–884; Table 3.2 step 8 (lines 835–837)."]},{"id":"c3q17","ch":3,"n":17,"q":"Which statement about the FMEA scoring scales is correct as written in the chapter?","o":["For all three categories, higher numbers always indicate higher risk","Higher detectability is rated at the low end of the scale, while higher severity and occurrence are rated at the high end","Occurrence is the only scale that runs in reverse","Severity is rated at the low end of the scale"],"a":1,"x":["Only detection runs inverted; severity and occurrence are rated high-at-high-risk.","DNA: \"A\" and \"C\" are partial truths; \"C\" specifically misidentifies which scale reverses (occurrence, not detection) — punishing shallow memorization.","Ref: Lines 820–837 (Table 3.2 steps 4, 6, 8)."]},{"id":"c3q18","ch":3,"n":18,"q":"A failure mode is scored Severity = 7, Occurrence = 6, Detection = 4. What is its RPN?","o":["17","42","168","96"],"a":2,"x":["RPN = S × O × D = 7 × 6 × 4 = 168.","DNA: \"A\" = the sum (7+6+4=17), the most common arithmetic-mode error; \"D\" = a real RPN from Figure 3.7 (8×4×3) inserted as a recognition trap; \"B\" is a plausible product of a different triple.","Ref: Lines 838–841 (RPN = S × O × D); Figure 3.6 (lines 960–964)."]},{"id":"c3q19","ch":3,"n":19,"q":"Two failure modes finish with the same overall RPN. According to the chapter's guidance, how should priority be resolved?","o":["Address the one with the higher occurrence rating first","Address the one with the higher severity rating first","Address whichever was identified first","Recalculate both until the scores differ"],"a":1,"x":["Tie escalate the higher severity rating.","DNA: \"A\" (occurrence) is the trap of choosing the wrong factor; the rule specifically privileges severity.","Ref: Lines 967–968."]},{"id":"c3q20","ch":3,"n":20,"q":"In some industries an additional index is calculated as Severity × Occurrence. The chapter refers to this as:","o":["Risk priority number","Detection index","Criticality","Cutoff score"],"a":2,"x":["Severity × Occurrence = Criticality.","DNA: \"A\" (RPN) is the dominant distractor since both are products — but RPN includes Detection. Tests precise factor accounting.","Ref: Lines 842–843."]},{"id":"c3q21","ch":3,"n":21,"q":"When reviewing the design during a Design FMEA, which tool does the chapter direct the team to use?","o":["Flowchart of the process steps","Schematic diagram and functional block diagram","Spaghetti diagram","Walk-through of the manufacturing line"],"a":1,"x":["Design FMEA schematic diagram and functional block diagram.","DNA: Directly mirrors Sample Q60/Q100. \"A\" (flowchart) is the Process-FMEA tool — the exact DFMEA PFMEA swap (I.C.3, Apply level).","Ref: Table 3.2 step 1, lines 792–793; reinforced lines 1298–1316."]},{"id":"c3q22","ch":3,"n":22,"q":"A green belt is beginning an FMEA for a manufacturing assembly line and must ensure every team member understands the sequence of operations firsthand. Which FMEA type and review tool apply?","o":["Design FMEA using a functional block diagram","Process FMEA using a flowchart and process walk-through","Concept FMEA using a Pareto chart","System FMEA using a control plan"],"a":1,"x":["Manufacturing line + first-hand sequence understanding Process FMEA, flowchart + walk-through.","DNA: Scenario form of Q21's inverse. \"A\" baits the candidate who pattern-matches \"block diagram = design tool\" without reading the process context.","Ref: Table 3.2 step 1 (PFMEA column), lines 792–800; lines 1312–1316."]},{"id":"c3q23","ch":3,"n":23,"q":"Which of the following is not described as a characteristic or correct use of FMEA in the chapter?","o":["It is a living document that is reviewed and updated on a continual basis","It is confined strictly to manufacturing applications","It should be revision-controlled and part of the QMS","It can be linked to QFD's houses of quality and to control plans"],"a":1,"x":["FMEA is explicitly not confined to manufacturing — it is used in service, software, and medical fields.","DNA: \"All except\" stem (Sample Q54/Q80 pattern). A, C, D are all stated facts; B is the lone false statement, contradicted directly in the text.","Ref: Lines 374–377 (not confined to manufacturing); 367–373 (living document / QMS / QFD linkage)."]},{"id":"c3q24","ch":3,"n":24,"q":"The chapter recommends an effective FMEA team size of approximately:","o":["2 to 3 people","5 to 7 people","10 to 12 people","As many stakeholders as possible, with no upper limit"],"a":1,"x":["Effective FMEA team is typically 5 to 7 people.","DNA: \"D\" exploits the Six Sigma instinct that \"more stakeholders = better\" (true for buy-in, false for FMEA team size) — a context-dependent trap.","Ref: Lines 645–646."]},{"id":"c4q1","ch":4,"n":1,"q":"When more project ideas exist than can be undertaken at once, which tool does the chapter specifically suggest for an organization that lacks a project selection process?","o":["SIPOC and process mapping","Advanced quality planning and quality function deployment","SWOT analysis and benchmarking","Pareto charts and affinity diagrams"],"a":1,"x":["Text explicitly names advanced quality planning and QFD for setting up a selection process.","DNA: Distractors A/C/D are all real Six Sigma tools (correct tools, wrong job) — the classic \"right concept, wrong context\" trap.","Reference: Lines 14–17 (Project Selection)."]},{"id":"c4q2","ch":4,"n":2,"q":"A project selection group is being formed. Which composition aligns with the chapter's description?","o":["Green belts, team members, and process operators","Master Black Belts, Black Belts, organizational champions, and key executive supporters","Customers, suppliers, and community representatives","Black Belts, finance managers, and maintenance personnel"],"a":1,"x":["Verbatim list of the selection group.","DNA: C lists stakeholders (real term, wrong role); D swaps in plausible but unlisted members.","Reference: Lines 23–25."]},{"id":"c4q3","ch":4,"n":3,"q":"A problem involving extensive data analysis and improvements using designed experiments would most likely be assigned to which type of team?","o":["A lean manufacturing team employing kaizen tools","An Operational Excellence team","A Six Sigma team","A benchmarking team"],"a":2,"x":["Designed-experiment/heavy-data work Six Sigma team; the chapter contrasts this directly with lean/kaizen.","DNA: A is the explicit contrast case the text gives for the wrong answer — punishes skimming.","Reference: Lines 28–31."]},{"id":"c4q4","ch":4,"n":4,"q":"According to the chapter, project selection criteria should always include which key element?","o":["The lowest resource requirement","The furthering of organizational goals","The shortest completion timeline","The highest customer visibility"],"a":1,"x":["\"Should always have the furthering of organizational goals as a key element.\"","DNA: Distractors are all attractive project virtues but not the stated always criterion.","Reference: Lines 32–33."]},{"id":"c4q5","ch":4,"n":5,"q":"All other things being approximately equal, projects receive the highest priority based on which factor?","o":["Greatest contribution to the bottom line","Greatest reduction in cycle time","Greatest number of stakeholders involved","Greatest alignment with the DMAIC roadmap"],"a":0,"x":["\"Projects with the greatest contributions to the bottom line receive the highest priority.\"","DNA: B/C/D are legitimate selection considerations, but the superlative \"highest priority\" anchors to bottom-line.","Reference: Lines 40–42."]},{"id":"c4q6","ch":4,"n":6,"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the input categories (\"M's\") identified in the chapter's process model?","o":["Mother Nature","Measurement system","Marketing","Methods/machines"],"a":2,"x":["Listed M's: man, material, methods/machines, Mother Nature, management, money, measurement system. \"Marketing\" is fabricated.","DNA: EXCEPT-stem; the invented option starts with \"M\" to defeat first-letter pattern-matching.","Reference: Lines 56–60."]},{"id":"c4q7","ch":4,"n":7,"q":"A payroll process contains a deduction step, which itself contains a tax-deduction step. The chapter uses this to illustrate which concept?","o":["Process boundaries","Subprocesses","Cross-functional interaction","Systems thinking"],"a":1,"x":["Nested steps-within-steps = subprocesses.","DNA: D (systems thinking) is a real chapter concept used as a high-quality distractor.","Reference: Lines 63–68, 80–82."]},{"id":"c4q8","ch":4,"n":8,"q":"A team is charged with improving a process. According to the chapter, what must they first know to avoid the project floundering?","o":["The risk priority number","The financial return on investment","The process boundaries (start and end points)","The names of all stakeholders"],"a":2,"x":["\"It is important to define its start and end points—its boundaries.\" Reinforced by the Key Point that projects flounder without clear boundaries.","DNA: B/D are downstream charter elements, not the first requirement.","Reference: Lines 83–86, 213–214."]},{"id":"c4q9","ch":4,"n":9,"q":"Benchmarking is described in the chapter as being especially useful for driving which of the following?","o":["Continuous improvement over breakthrough improvement","Breakthrough improvement over continuous improvement","Incremental kaizen events","Statistical process control"],"a":1,"x":["\"Benchmarking is useful for driving breakthrough improvement over continuous improvement.\"","DNA: A is the exact reversal — ASQ's signature flipped-pair trap.","Reference: Lines 113–114."]},{"id":"c4q10","ch":4,"n":10,"q":"Which type of benchmarking provides easy access to other areas but is often limited by the company's own culture, norms, and history?","o":["Competitive benchmarking","Functional benchmarking","Collaborative benchmarking","Internal benchmarking"],"a":3,"x":["Internal benchmarking: easy access, limited by company culture/norms/history.","DNA: A (competitive) is the adjacent type with a different limitation, forcing precise discrimination.","Reference: Lines 123–125."]},{"id":"c4q11","ch":4,"n":11,"q":"An organization compares a similar function with a company entirely outside its own industry, gaining ample partner opportunities. Which benchmarking type is this?","o":["Internal benchmarking","Competitive benchmarking","Functional benchmarking","Collaborative benchmarking"],"a":2,"x":["Functional benchmarking: similar functions outside the industry, ample partner opportunities.","DNA: D (collaborative) is the neighboring definition — tests close-difference reading.","Reference: Lines 130–132."]},{"id":"c4q12","ch":4,"n":12,"q":"The chapter notes a downside of benchmarking within a particular industry. What is it?","o":["It violates proprietary information agreements","It tends to put one in second place, at best","It requires excessive site visits","It limits management commitment"],"a":1,"x":["\"Tends to put one in second place, at best.\"","DNA: Distractors borrow real Table 4.1 cautions to feel plausible.","Reference: Lines 105–106."]},{"id":"c4q13","ch":4,"n":13,"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four steps in benchmarking listed in the chapter?","o":["Analyze your operation","Identify the competition and industry leaders","Calculate the risk priority number","Incorporate the best of the best"],"a":2,"x":["Four steps: analyze operation, identify competition/leaders, incorporate best of best, determine how to gain superiority. RPN is unrelated (Ch. 6 concept).","DNA: EXCEPT-stem importing a real Six Sigma term from another chapter.","Reference: Lines 115–119."]},{"id":"c4q14","ch":4,"n":14,"q":"According to Table 4.1, which is something a team should NOT do when benchmarking?","o":["Select projects tied to strategic goals","Confuse benchmarking with industrial tourism","Obtain management commitment","Benchmark a core process"],"a":1,"x":["\"Don't confuse benchmarking with industrial tourism\" is a Don't column entry; A/C/D are Do entries.","DNA: Table-interpretation item (Analyze-level) — must map option to correct column.","Reference: Lines 179–181, 164–168."]},{"id":"c4q15","ch":4,"n":15,"q":"Which two tools are identified as the primary means of identifying process boundaries?","o":["The basic process model and the SIPOC diagram","The flowchart and the Pareto chart","The control chart and the histogram","The affinity diagram and the matrix diagram"],"a":0,"x":["\"The two primary tools... are the basic process model... and the SIPOC diagram.\"","DNA: All distractors are real boundary/analysis tools.","Reference: Lines 199–201."]},{"id":"c4q16","ch":4,"n":16,"q":"A team is mapping a process that crosses several departments. Which tool style is specifically recommended in the chapter to recognize these cross-functional interactions?","o":["The spaghetti diagram","The \"swim-lane\" style flowchart","The cause-and-effect diagram","The Gantt chart"],"a":1,"x":["Swim-lane flowchart specifically named for cross-functional interactions.","DNA: A (spaghetti) is a real mapping tool — wrong purpose.","Reference: Lines 221–223."]},{"id":"c4q17","ch":4,"n":17,"q":"Two departments both believe they have final decision authority over changes to a shared process. Which cross-functional challenge does this represent?","o":["Commonality of measures","Sharing of information","Process ownership","Process knowledge or expertise"],"a":2,"x":["\"Two or more areas may think they own the process and have final decision authority.\"","DNA: All four options are the chapter's own four challenge categories — pure discrimination.","Reference: Lines 225–226."]},{"id":"c4q18","ch":4,"n":18,"q":"Finance measures improvements in dollars while production measures them in defects. Per the chapter, this illustrates which cross-functional challenge?","o":["Process ownership","Commonality of measures","Sharing of information","Systems thinking"],"a":1,"x":["Finance-dollars vs. production-defects = commonality of measures.","DNA: Same four-way challenge set; scenario cue must be matched to the right label.","Reference: Lines 229–231."]},{"id":"c4q19","ch":4,"n":19,"q":"If cross-functional challenges identified are significant, the chapter recommends they be handled how?","o":["Escalated to the project sponsor for resolution","Included as potential risks so mitigation activities can be performed","Removed from the project scope entirely","Assigned to a separate lean team"],"a":1,"x":["\"Recommended that the challenges be included as potential risks so... mitigation activities can be performed.\"","DNA: A/C/D are plausible PM actions but contradict the text.","Reference: Lines 234–237."]},{"id":"c4q20","ch":4,"n":20,"q":"A change made in one part of an operation causes a new problem downstream. The failure to anticipate this reflects the absence of which concept?","o":["Process mapping","Benchmarking","Systems thinking","Stakeholder analysis"],"a":2,"x":["The downstream-ripple example is the chapter's definition of (the absence of) systems thinking.","DNA: A/B/D are all chapter tools — only systems thinking addresses interdependence.","Reference: Lines 280–282."]},{"id":"c4q21","ch":4,"n":21,"q":"Who is described as responsible for the definition, execution, maintenance, and improvement of a process, and may also be referred to as a subject matter expert?","o":["Stakeholders","Champions","Process owners","Black Belts"],"a":2,"x":["Process owners: \"responsible for the definition, execution, maintenance, and improvement of the process; in some cases... subject matter experts.\"","DNA: Mirrors Sample Q#45 — A (stakeholders) is the strongest decoy because owners can be stakeholders, but the role definition pins it.","Reference: Lines 300–311."]},{"id":"c4q22","ch":4,"n":22,"q":"The chapter states the most effective process improvement teams represent process owners and all stakeholders for all the following reasons EXCEPT:","o":["Stakeholders have the best knowledge base about the process","Stakeholders tend to have the best ideas for process improvement","Stakeholders are responsible for securing project funding and resources","Stakeholders are often most aware of unintended consequences of changes"],"a":2,"x":["The chapter lists knowledge base, best ideas, unintended-consequence awareness, and buy-in — not funding responsibility (that belongs to sponsors; see Sample Q#9).","DNA: EXCEPT-stem reusing the exact sponsor-vs-stakeholder trap from the sample bank.","Reference: Lines 316–323."]},{"id":"c5q1","ch":5,"n":1,"q":"A project team has defined its project boundaries. An individual who works outside those boundaries but is neither an internal nor external customer is best classified as which of the following?","o":["A process owner","A stakeholder","A sponsor","A champion"],"a":1,"x":["The text states that individuals outside what we consider internal or external customers are referred to as stakeholders. Distractors A/C/D are all real adjacent roles (classic ASQ trap), but the chapter assigns this specific \"outside the boundary\" label to stakeholders.","Reference: Lines 22–24 (Customer Identification, BoK II.B.1)."]},{"id":"c5q2","ch":5,"n":2,"q":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a method used to identify the customers of a project?","o":["Brainstorming","SIPOC","Tracking a product or service to delivery","Quality function deployment"],"a":3,"x":["The three listed identification methods are brainstorming, SIPOC, and tracking to delivery. QFD is a requirements-translation tool, not a customer-identification method — the distractor mimics the DNA of pairing a correct-but-wrong-phase tool (cf. Sample Q24, affinity diagram).","Reference: Lines 41–43."]},{"id":"c5q3","ch":5,"n":3,"q":"A consumer-products company is segmenting its customer base. Which of the following is NOT given as a typical customer segmentation category?","o":["Age groups","Geographical location","Industry type","Purchasing frequency"],"a":3,"x":["Listed segments are internal/external, age groups, geographical location, and industry type. \"Purchasing frequency\" sounds plausible (the trap) but is not listed.","Reference: Lines 47–51."]},{"id":"c5q4","ch":5,"n":4,"q":"According to the chapter, which procedure is described as statistically the most valid for collecting customer data?","o":["Conducting focus groups with the most experienced customers","Randomly selecting a reasonably large group of customers and obtaining complete, accurate data on each","Interviewing the internal customers closest to the process","Distributing a survey to every customer in the database"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter explicitly names random selection of a reasonably large group with complete/accurate data as the most statistically valid procedure. Distractors A/C/D are real methods used because the ideal is often impossible — exactly the \"true but not best\" pattern.","Reference: Lines 99–101."]},{"id":"c5q5","ch":5,"n":5,"q":"A green belt argues that the engineering team already knows what customers want because they are the subject-matter experts, so no customer data collection is needed. Based on the chapter, what is the primary flaw in this reasoning?","o":["Engineers are not authorized to collect customer data","Expert assumption without verification leads to rework, as seen with untested software","Engineers lack access to statistically valid sampling tools","Customer requirements are unknown and unknowable"],"a":1,"x":["The software-patch example directly supports this: experts assuming requirements without end-customer testing causes rework. D is a Deming concept from a different paragraph (decoy by topical proximity).","Reference: Lines 68–75."]},{"id":"c5q6","ch":5,"n":6,"q":"W. E. Deming's statement that \"some of the most important numbers are unknown and unknowable\" was used in this chapter to illustrate which point?","o":["Process variation cannot be fully measured","It is easy to underestimate the value of understanding and providing for customers' needs","Sample sizes can never be large enough for valid data","Customer surveys are inherently biased"],"a":1,"x":["Deming's \"unknown and unknowable\" (customer goodwill) is used to show it is easy to underestimate the value of meeting customer needs. Distractors borrow statistics/SPC vocabulary from elsewhere in the BoK.","Reference: Lines 85–91."]},{"id":"c5q7","ch":5,"n":7,"q":"In a QFD (House of Quality) matrix, the customer requirements section and the technical requirements section are also referred to, respectively, as the:","o":["\"Hows\" and the \"Whats\"","\"Whats\" and the \"Hows\"","\"Whys\" and the \"Hows\"","\"Whats\" and the \"Whens\""],"a":1,"x":["Customer requirements = \"what\"; technical requirements = \"how.\" Option A reverses them — the single most common QFD trap.","Reference: Lines 236–237."]},{"id":"c5q8","ch":5,"n":8,"q":"Which element of the House of Quality shows whether improving one technical requirement will simultaneously make another technical requirement worse?","o":["The relationship matrix","The comparison with competition","The co-relationships (roof)","The target values"],"a":2,"x":["The co-relationships (roof) shows technical-requirement interactions. The relationship matrix (A) links customer-to-technical, not technical-to-technical — the precise distractor used in Sample Q35.","Reference: Lines 222–226."]},{"id":"c5q9","ch":5,"n":9,"q":"In a QFD matrix, a negative co-relationship between two technical requirements indicates that:","o":["Both can be improved at the same time","Improving one will make the other worse","Neither requirement is important to the customer","The requirements are unrelated"],"a":1,"x":["Directly defined: negative co-relationship = improving one worsens the other; positive = both improvable together (distractor A is the positive definition).","Reference: Lines 223–225."]},{"id":"c5q10","ch":5,"n":10,"q":"The input to the QFD process is which of the following?","o":["The technical requirements","The voice of the customer (VOC)","The column weights","The relationship matrix"],"a":1,"x":["\"The input to the process is the voice of the customer (VOC).\" Distractors are outputs/components of QFD, not the input.","Reference: Lines 140–141."]},{"id":"c5q11","ch":5,"n":11,"q":"In a QFD matrix, the column weights are obtained by:","o":["Adding the importance ratings of all customer requirements","Multiplying the customer-requirement importance value by the values assigned to the relationship-matrix symbols","Averaging the technical requirement scores","Counting the number of strong relationships in each column"],"a":1,"x":["Column weight = importance value × assigned symbol value. A and D are arithmetically plausible mis-statements (the trap is \"adding\" vs. \"multiplying\").","Reference: Lines 226–232."]},{"id":"c5q12","ch":5,"n":12,"q":"Using the arbitrary assignment described in the chapter, a strong relationship symbol in the relationship matrix is given a value of:","o":["1","3","5","9"],"a":3,"x":["Strong = 9, moderate = 3, weak = 1. The decoys (1, 3, 5) are the other scale values plus a plausible filler.","Reference: Lines 231–232."]},{"id":"c5q13","ch":5,"n":13,"q":"A QFD customer-requirements list has grown to more than 25 voice lines and has become unmanageable. Which tool is recommended to condense the list?","o":["Tree diagram","Affinity diagram","SIPOC","Kano model"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter names the affinity diagram (a convergent tool) for condensing >25 voice lines. Tree diagram (A) translates requirements to CTx — adjacent-tool trap.","Reference: Lines 249–252."]},{"id":"c5q14","ch":5,"n":14,"q":"The CTx concept treats \"x\" as a variable representing an area of impact on the customer. A team has identified temperature, pressure, and humidity as key process input variables but has not yet specified their settings. Which CTx category does this best describe?","o":["Critical-to-Quality (CTQ)","Critical-to-Cost (CTC)","Critical-to-Process (CTP)","Critical-to-Delivery (CTD)"],"a":2,"x":["CTPs are key process input variables (the critical x's in y = f(x)) — temperature, pressure, humidity are the textbook examples. CTQ/CTC/CTS describe customer-facing impact, not process inputs.","Reference: Lines 418–423."]},{"id":"c5q15","ch":5,"n":15,"q":"A customer specifies that a product must weigh between 15 and 20 pounds because products over 20 pounds cause back injuries and products under 15 pounds tempt operators to carry multiples, also causing injury. The same weight specification, when stated for this reason, is best classified as:","o":["Critical-to-Cost (CTC)","Critical-to-Quality (CTQ)","Critical-to-Safety (CTS)","Critical-to-Process (CTP)"],"a":2,"x":["The chapter deliberately reuses the identical 15–20 lb spec across CTQ, CTC, and CTS to show \"same spec, different reason.\" When the reason is injury/safety, it is CTS. This is the chapter's signature trap.","Reference: Lines 424–435."]},{"id":"c5q16","ch":5,"n":16,"q":"A customer requires that products be received no earlier than two days before, and no later than one day after, the delivery date — partly because early delivery creates excess inventory. This requirement is an example of:","o":["Critical-to-Delivery (CTD)","Critical-to-Cost (CTC)","Critical-to-Quality (CTQ)","Critical-to-Process (CTP)"],"a":0,"x":["Delivery-window requirements (including early-delivery/excess-inventory concern) define CTD. CTC (B) is a tempting decoy because excess inventory has cost implications, but the chapter classifies the delivery window as CTD.","Reference: Lines 436–444."]},{"id":"c5q17","ch":5,"n":17,"q":"According to Table 5.1, which of the following is classified as a product value characteristic rather than a service value characteristic?","o":["Responsiveness","Reliability","Durability","Competence"],"a":2,"x":["Per Table 5.1, Durability is a product characteristic. Responsiveness, Reliability, and Competence are all service characteristics — mirrors Sample Q21 exactly.","Reference: Lines 390–401 (Table 5.1)."]},{"id":"c5q18","ch":5,"n":18,"q":"Which of the following is identified as a service value characteristic?","o":["Serviceability","Access","Functionality","Availability"],"a":1,"x":["Access is a service value characteristic. Serviceability, Functionality, and Availability are product characteristics — the reverse-direction version of Q17.","Reference: Lines 390–401 (Table 5.1)."]},{"id":"c5q19","ch":5,"n":19,"q":"A green belt needs to translate the general customer requirement of a \"quality product\" into measurable height, width, and depth specifications with tolerances. Which tool is recommended for this translation?","o":["Kano model","Affinity diagram","Tree diagram","SIPOC"],"a":2,"x":["The tree diagram translates a general CTQ (\"quality product\") into measurable dimensions with tolerances (Figure 5.7). Kano (A) analyzes satisfaction; affinity (B) condenses lists — both adjacent-tool decoys.","Reference: Lines 445–448, 457–462 (Figure 5.7)."]},{"id":"c5q20","ch":5,"n":20,"q":"In the Kano model, a characteristic whose absence causes dissatisfaction but whose presence only raises satisfaction to a neutral state is classified as:","o":["A delighter","One-dimensional","A must-be","Indifferent"],"a":2,"x":["Must-be: absence dissatisfaction; presence only neutral (cannot delight). A \"delighter\" (A) is the inverse; \"one-dimensional\" (B) scales linearly. Precise definitional discrimination is the test here.","Reference: Lines 453–483."]},{"id":"c5q21","ch":5,"n":21,"q":"In the Kano model, the automobile cup holder is used to illustrate which phenomenon?","o":["One-dimensional factors drive satisfaction in direct proportion to their presence","Delighters tend to migrate to must-be features as they become more available over time","Must-be features can be removed without affecting satisfaction","Delighters always remain delighters regardless of market availability"],"a":1,"x":["Cup holders illustrate delighters migrating to must-be features as availability increases over time. Distractor A is a true Kano statement but describes the wrong category for this example.","Reference: Lines 490–497."]},{"id":"c5q22","ch":5,"n":22,"q":"A green belt newly assigned to a project is advised, after gathering benchmark process data, to first talk with the people performing the work before talking to upstream and downstream parties. According to the chapter, the primary purpose of starting locally is to:","o":["Reduce the sample size needed for statistical validity","Build support for the project by understanding and easing the local job","Satisfy social-responsibility requirements","Identify external stakeholders such as government agencies"],"a":1,"x":["The Key Point states that starting locally — easing the workers' job — builds support for the project. D and C pull from the external-stakeholder/social-responsibility paragraph (topical-proximity decoys).","Reference: Lines 76–83 (Key Point)."]},{"id":"c6q1","ch":6,"n":1,"q":"Frederick Taylor is most associated with which management theory, which sought the single best way to perform a task to raise productivity?","o":["Classical organizational theory","Scientific management","Human relations theory","Behavior management"],"a":1,"x":["The text directly attributes to Taylor the aim of \"finding the one best way to perform a task so as to increase productivity.\"","DNA: Distractor A (Classical organizational theory) is the adjacent theory in the same passage — a classic \"correct definition, wrong concept\" trap.","Reference: Lines 22–27, \"Scientific Management.\""]},{"id":"c6q2","ch":6,"n":2,"q":"A manager assumes most employees dislike work and require continual prodding. Which model does this assumption represent?","o":["Theory Y","Theory Z","Theory X","Situational leadership"],"a":2,"x":["Theory X is \"a negative view of human nature, assuming that most employees do not want to work and will require continual prodding.\"","DNA: Theory Y (A) is the deliberate inverse distractor placed in the same sentence — tests semantic pairing.","Reference: Lines 53–55."]},{"id":"c6q3","ch":6,"n":3,"q":"Which management style is described as a Japanese approach featuring long-term employment, slow promotions, considerable job rotation, and consensus-style decision making?","o":["Theory X","Theory Y","Theory Z","Managerial Grid"],"a":2,"x":["Ouchi's Theory Z is \"a Japanese style... long-term employment, slow promotions, considerable job rotation, consensus-style decision making.\"","DNA: X/Y distractors exploit the alphabetic theory cluster.","Reference: Lines 58–60."]},{"id":"c6q4","ch":6,"n":4,"q":"Henri Fayol's contribution to management thought is best described as:","o":["Breaking jobs into small task components for efficiency study","Fourteen principles addressing how power and authority are subdivided into reporting relationships","A two-axis grid balancing concern for task and concern for people","The experiential learning cycle of experience, reflection, theory, and application"],"a":1,"x":["Fayol \"developed 14 principles of management, which included... how power and authority needed to be subdivided into reporting relationships.\"","DNA: A=Taylor, C=Blake/Mouton, D=Kolb — every distractor correctly defines a different theorist from the same chapter. This is the highest-fidelity ASQ trap pattern.","Reference: Lines 28–33."]},{"id":"c6q5","ch":6,"n":5,"q":"According to the chapter, the Agile approach was a term coined within which body of thinking?","o":["Theory of constraints","Lean approach / lean thinking","Scientific management","Systems thinking"],"a":1,"x":["\"Agile was a term coined in the lean approach/lean thinking.\"","DNA: TOC and systems thinking are real chapter terms used as plausible lures.","Reference: Lines 110–113."]},{"id":"c6q6","ch":6,"n":6,"q":"In a top-down management approach, decision-making authority is best characterized as:","o":["Delegated to mature, self-directing teams","Distributed by consensus among the whole workforce","Controlled by the management team or project manager, who directs the outcome","Rotated among team members at each maturity level"],"a":2,"x":["In top-down management \"the management team (or project manager) seeks to direct and control the outcome,\" associated with Theory X.","DNA: A (delegating to mature teams) is lifted from the Situational Leadership passage — wrong-concept definition.","Reference: Lines 106–109."]},{"id":"c6q7","ch":6,"n":7,"q":"Which of the following is not one of the points the chapter states a project charter should contain?","o":["Problem statement","Benefits","Risk priority number","Results"],"a":2,"x":["The chapter lists charter contents as problem statement, purpose, benefits, scope, results. RPN belongs to FMEA/risk analysis, not the charter's required points.","DNA: Negative-framing (\"not\") plus inserting a real Six Sigma term (RPN) from a different section — mirrors Sample Q's \"Identified risks / Communication plan\" charter trap.","Reference: Lines 123–133."]},{"id":"c6q8","ch":6,"n":8,"q":"A green belt's charter section defines \"the criteria and metrics for project success, including the baseline measures and improvement expectations.\" Which charter element is this?","o":["Purpose","Scope","Results","Benefits"],"a":2,"x":["\"Results. Defines the criteria and metrics for project success—including the baseline measures and improvement expectations.\"","DNA: This is a near-verbatim echo of Sample Q5 (baseline measures and improvement expectations) — high probability the real exam recycles this phrasing.","Reference: Lines 132–133."]},{"id":"c6q9","ch":6,"n":9,"q":"Per the chapter, another term sometimes used for the project charter, derived from the work breakdown structure and forming the basis for the contractual agreement, is the:","o":["Statement of Work (SOW)","SIPOC","Project management plan","Storyboard"],"a":0,"x":["\"Another term sometimes used for the project charter is the Statement of Work (SOW)... derived from the work breakdown structure (WBS).\"","DNA: SIPOC and project management plan are correct tools but wrong identity — definitional misdirection.","Reference: Lines 140–145."]},{"id":"c6q10","ch":6,"n":10,"q":"A project manager states: \"The response time to Internet inquiries from potential customers is too long.\" Within the charter, this sentence functions as the:","o":["Scope statement","Problem statement","Benefits statement","Purpose statement"],"a":1,"x":["Both quoted examples appear under the problem statement definition (\"a summation of what requires improvement\").","DNA: Scenario-stem reframing of a quoted example; scope/purpose distractors are sibling charter elements.","Reference: Lines 134–139."]},{"id":"c6q11","ch":6,"n":11,"q":"According to the chapter, the purpose of documenting the project scope is to:","o":["Calculate the total estimated project cost","Ensure a common understanding of what the team will work on and what is outside the defined boundaries","Establish the contractual agreement with the customer","Track the percentage of work accomplished on schedule"],"a":1,"x":["Scope documentation exists \"to ensure a common understanding of what the project team... will work on, and what is outside those defined boundaries.\"","DNA: A=WBS function, D=project metric function — adjacent-section definitions used as lures.","Reference: Lines 172–178."]},{"id":"c6q12","ch":6,"n":12,"q":"A goal is to increase process throughput by 25 percent, and the team tracks incremental cycle time reduction. Cycle time here is functioning as a:","o":["Consequential metric","Key performance indicator only","Primary (project) metric","Toll-gate review measure"],"a":2,"x":["Cycle time tied directly to the throughput goal is a primary metric (metrics linked to the project goal); consequential metrics track negative effects of the improvement.","DNA: Primary vs. consequential is a flagged exam trap. A is the precise inverse.","Reference: Lines 179–192 (and BoK II.C.4 primary vs. consequential)."]},{"id":"c6q13","ch":6,"n":13,"q":"Which tool does the chapter describe as guiding the logical development of tasks and providing a structure that summarizes project costs?","o":["Gantt chart","Work breakdown structure (WBS)","PERT chart","Critical path method (CPM)"],"a":1,"x":["The WBS \"guides a logical development of tasks... and it provides a structure that summarizes project costs.\"","DNA: Gantt/PERT/CPM are the co-listed planning tools — plausible but each lacks the cost-summary attribute.","Reference: Lines 233–243."]},{"id":"c6q14","ch":6,"n":14,"q":"Toll gate reviews are best described in the chapter as:","o":["The final closure meeting held with project sponsors","Fixed points during the process where checks are conducted to ensure the project is on the right path","A spreadsheet listing of projected project dates","Before-and-after photographs of a process"],"a":1,"x":["Toll gates \"fix certain points during the process where checks... are conducted... to ensure that the project is on the right path.\"","DNA: A=closure, C=basic Excel planning tool, D=storyboard — all real chapter items relocated to the wrong concept.","Reference: Lines 244–254."]},{"id":"c6q15","ch":6,"n":15,"q":"Which presentation tool does the chapter recommend specifically for conveying project changes that are easier to draw or photograph than to explain, often as current-state/future-state pictures?","o":["Gantt chart","Storyboard","Pareto chart","SIPOC"],"a":1,"x":["Storyboards convey changes \"easier to draw or photograph than to explain,\" using current-state/future-state pictures.","DNA: Echoes Sample Q12's storyboard distractor (\"differences in a process between a current state and a future state\").","Reference: Lines 300–309."]},{"id":"c6q16","ch":6,"n":16,"q":"Which of the following is not listed in the chapter as a tool dedicated to formal project risk analysis?","o":["SWOT analysis","Failure mode and effects analysis (FMEA)","Risk priority number (RPN) or risk priority matrix","Critical path method (CPM)"],"a":3,"x":["Listed risk-analysis tools are SWOT, RPN/risk priority matrix, FMEA, and expected-profit formula. CPM is a planning/scheduling tool.","DNA: Direct parallel to Sample Q1 (Activity Network Diagram as the non-risk tool) — same logical structure, swapped tool.","Reference: Lines 324–328."]},{"id":"c6q17","ch":6,"n":17,"q":"In the risk assessment assignment matrix described in the chapter, the assignment number is calculated as:","o":["Severity rating plus probability rating","Severity rating multiplied by probability rating","Severity × Occurrence × Detection","Probability divided by severity rating"],"a":1,"x":["The assignment number \"is calculated as the severity rating multiplied by the probability rating.\"","DNA: C is the full FMEA RPN formula (S×O×D) — a tempting over-recall trap, since the text explicitly calls the assignment number a simpler version of RPN. A (addition) is the common arithmetic distractor.","Reference: Lines 353–357."]},{"id":"c6q18","ch":6,"n":18,"q":"A risk is associated with a key supplier potentially missing a delivery date. The team develops a second supplier and adds supplier oversight. According to the chapter, this activity is best classified as:","o":["Risk identification","Risk mitigation","Risk verification","Risk probability assessment"],"a":1,"x":["Mitigation \"begins with identifying activities the team or organization can perform to reduce the likelihood... and/or to reduce its impact,\" with developing another supplier given as the example.","DNA: Maps to Sample Q51's risk-sequence scenario; verification/probability are the sibling-stage lures.","Reference: Lines 360–368."]},{"id":"c6q19","ch":6,"n":19,"q":"Per the chapter, risk verification is the process of:","o":["Determining the likelihood that a risk will occur during project execution","Ranking each risk from 1 to 3 for severity and probability","Ensuring that the risk mitigation activities reasonably prevent the risk from occurring","Quantifying the potential financial and nonfinancial exposures"],"a":2,"x":["\"Risk verification is the process of ensuring that the risk mitigation activities reasonably prevent the risk from occurring.\"","DNA: A=probability assessment, B=assignment-matrix ranking, D=PDCA Plan step — each a correctly stated but mislabeled process.","Reference: Lines 399–400."]},{"id":"c6q20","ch":6,"n":20,"q":"The chapter presents the PDCA cycle as one method to manage risk. In which PDCA step does the organization identify and define its potential exposures to loss and select the best risk management technique?","o":["Plan","Do","Check","Act"],"a":0,"x":["Under the Plan step the organization identifies/defines exposures, quantifies risks, examines alternatives, and \"selects the best risk management technique(s).\"","DNA: Do/Check/Act distractors test whether the candidate can sequence PDCA — a flagged exam-trap family (PDCA/PDSA).","Reference: Lines 431–437."]},{"id":"c6q21","ch":6,"n":21,"q":"Regarding project closure, which statement is consistent with the chapter?","o":["Closure is a negotiation between the team and the sponsors","Closure is optional once project goals are achieved","Closure is a final step based on proving goals were met, completing documentation, and holding a closure meeting with sponsors","Lessons learned must be completed before a project can be formally closed"],"a":2,"x":["Closure \"is a final step performed based on proving that the project met established goals... ensuring documentation is completed and stored, and conducting a closure meeting with the project sponsors.\"","DNA: B (optional) and A (negotiation) are the explicit false statements the text refutes (\"not a negotiation\"); mirrors Sample Q32's \"closure is optional\" false-statement trap. D inverts the lessons-learned rule (lessons learned are not required — see Sample Q19).","Reference: Lines 481–485."]},{"id":"c6q22","ch":6,"n":22,"q":"The chapter notes that if a company lacks a formal Lessons Learned database, the information could instead be added to which database, common among ISO 9001–registered companies?","o":["Corrective Actions database","Work breakdown structure","Statement of Work repository","Risk priority matrix"],"a":0,"x":["\"You could consider adding information to the Corrective Actions database, which is common among companies who are ISO 9001 registered.\"","DNA: The remaining options are real chapter tools relocated to an unrelated context — pure recognition lure.","Reference: Lines 496–499."]},{"id":"c7q1","ch":7,"n":1,"q":"A green belt brainstorms \"What are ways to reduce cycle time for Process A?\" and writes each idea on a sticky note. What is the correct next step in building an affinity diagram?","o":["Rank each idea using nominal group technique","Move the notes until they fall into five to 10 natural groups","Assign a weighted criteria score to each note","Draw arrows showing which idea influences the others"],"a":1,"x":["After ideas are written on notes, the next step is moving them into 5–10 natural groups; naming and lining come later.","Distractor DNA: A (NGT ranking) and D (digraph arrows) are real techniques borrowed from other tools — the classic \"correct tool, wrong context\" trap. C imports prioritization-matrix logic.","Reference: \"AFFINITY DIAGRAM\" section, p. 115–116 (\"move the notes around until they fall into 5 to 10 natural groups\")."]},{"id":"c7q2","ch":7,"n":2,"q":"In an interrelationship digraph studying barriers to on-time delivery, \"poor scheduling practices\" has the most outgoing arrows. What does this identify it as?","o":["The outcome","The constraint","The driver","The root metric"],"a":2,"x":["The note with the most outgoing arrows is the driver and is often a key cause.","Distractor DNA: A (outcome) is the deliberate mirror trap — that's the note with the most incoming arrows. B/D invent plausible-sounding labels.","Reference: \"INTERRELATIONSHIP DIAGRAM (DIGRAPH)\" section, p. 126–127."]},{"id":"c7q3","ch":7,"n":3,"q":"Priya needs a tool that, like PERT and critical path analysis, can show task dependencies and whether activities run in series or parallel, providing either a top-level or detailed view. Which tool fits?","o":["Activity network diagram","Prioritization matrix","Gantt chart","Affinity diagram"],"a":0,"x":["The text explicitly compares the AND to PERT/critical path analysis and notes it shows series/parallel relationships at top-level or detailed depth.","Distractor DNA: All distractors are real planning/decision tools but none carry the PERT-like dependency framing.","Reference: \"Activity Network Diagram\" / Figure 7.1 text, p. 115 (\"This tool, like PERT and critical path analysis…\")."]},{"id":"c7q4","ch":7,"n":4,"q":"A manager states that advanced quality planning (AQP) can be thought of as the first step of which cycle?","o":["SDCA","DMAIC","PDSA","Theory of constraints"],"a":2,"x":["AQP is described as the first step of the plan-do-study-act (PDSA) cycle — planning done before work starts.","Distractor DNA: SDCA and PDSA are deliberately confusable cycle acronyms from later in the chapter; DMAIC is the umbrella method.","Reference: \"ADVANCED QUALITY PLANNING\" section, p. 115 (\"think of AQP as the first step of the plan-do-study-act (PDSA) cycle\")."]},{"id":"c7q5","ch":7,"n":5,"q":"William, an internal auditor, has gathered evidence through interviews and observations and is now reconciling findings to ensure their validity. Which audit phase is he executing?","o":["Audit planning","Audit performance","Audit reporting","Audit closure"],"a":1,"x":["Audit performance is where evidence is gathered (interviews, observations) and reconciliation validates findings.","Distractor DNA: Mirrors sample Q43 exactly. Planning = preparation; reporting = drafting; closure = follow-up — all true phases, only one matches \"gathering/reconciling evidence.\"","Reference: \"AUDITING\" section, p. 117 (\"The audit performance phase is where the auditor gathers the evidence… includes reconciliation…\")."]},{"id":"c7q6","ch":7,"n":6,"q":"According to the chapter, why is benchmarking against an organization in a different industry often easier than within the same industry?","o":["It produces more statistically valid data","It removes the competitive aspect of comparing processes","It eliminates the need to flowchart the current process","It guarantees a win/win information exchange"],"a":1,"x":["Cross-industry benchmarking removes competitive sensitivities around comparing processes.","Distractor DNA: C contradicts the text (you must flowchart your process first regardless). A/D are true benchmarking statements but don't answer \"why easier.\"","Reference: \"BENCHMARKING\" section, p. 118 (\"removes the competitive aspects of comparing processes\")."]},{"id":"c7q7","ch":7,"n":7,"q":"A team building a cause-and-effect diagram is told the tool may be used for more than just defects. Which statement is correct?","o":["It can be used to analyze positive effects as well as undesirable ones","It must always use exactly the six M categories","It establishes statistical cause-and-effect once r > 0.80","It is restricted to the measure phase"],"a":0,"x":["The chapter's Key Point states the diagram analyzes positive effects as well as undesirable ones.","Distractor DNA: B overstates (\"must always\" — six Ms are a starting prompt). C smuggles in the correlation-≠-causation passage. D is a false phase restriction.","Reference: \"CAUSE-AND-EFFECT DIAGRAM\" Key Point, p. 119."]},{"id":"c7q8","ch":7,"n":8,"q":"What is the main use of a check sheet?","o":["To develop quality acceptance criteria","To allow continuous measurement on a variable scale","To observe or review a process, usually during its execution, using pre-categorized outcomes","To prioritize options against weighted criteria"],"a":2,"x":["Check sheets observe/review a process during execution using pre-categorized outcomes.","Distractor DNA: B (variable measurement) and A (criteria development) are the same distractors used in sample Q29 — the test recycles these against check sheets. D belongs to the prioritization matrix.","Reference: \"CHECK SHEETS\" section, p. 120–121."]},{"id":"c7q9","ch":7,"n":9,"q":"A force-field analysis lists driving and restraining forces for a desired future state. According to the chapter, what guidance is offered on how to proceed?","o":["Focus entirely on amplifying driving forces","It is often more useful to reduce or remove restraining forces","Eliminate any force that appears in both columns","Rank only the driving forces using multivoting"],"a":1,"x":["The text states it is often more useful to reduce/remove restraining forces than to focus solely on driving forces.","Distractor DNA: A is the intuitive-but-wrong answer the trap targets. C/D invent procedures not in the text.","Reference: \"FORCE-FIELD ANALYSIS\" section, p. 124 (\"more useful to reduce or remove restraining forces…\")."]},{"id":"c7q10","ch":7,"n":10,"q":"Which of the following is not something a Gantt chart is designed to show?","o":["Task milestones","Predecessor and successor task relationships","The single longest path that determines total project duration","The duration of key activities overlaid on a calendar"],"a":2,"x":["The longest-path-determines-duration concept belongs to CPM/PERT, not the Gantt chart. Negative stem isolates the false attribution.","Distractor DNA: A, B, D are all genuine Gantt features listed in the text; C is a correctly defined wrong tool — the signature ASQ trap.","Reference: \"GANTT CHART\" p. 125 vs. \"PERT/CPM\" p. 134."]},{"id":"c7q11","ch":7,"n":11,"q":"In a network with two paths — ACEGH (50 days) and BDFGH (55 days) — which statement is true regarding the critical path?","o":["ACEGH is the critical path because it has fewer activities","BDFGH is the critical path, and delaying any of its activities delays the entire project","The critical path is the path requiring the least time","Adding resources to ACEGH (\"crashing\") will shorten the project"],"a":1,"x":["Critical path = longest path (55 days = BDFGH); any delay on it delays the whole project.","Distractor DNA: A/C invert the definition (least time / fewest activities). D mis-targets crashing onto the non-critical path.","Reference: \"PROJECT EVALUATION AND REVIEW TECHNIQUE (PERT)\" section, p. 134."]},{"id":"c7q12","ch":7,"n":12,"q":"A team wants a tree-style diagram that anticipates problems and lists possible countermeasures, used as a living document updated as the project proceeds. Which tool is this?","o":["Interrelationship digraph","Prioritization matrix","Process decision program chart (PDPC)","Matrix diagram"],"a":2,"x":["PDPC is a tree diagram illustrating anticipated problems and possible solutions, updatable as a dynamic document.","Distractor DNA: All are genuine management/planning tools (II.D list members) — discrimination is purely on function.","Reference: \"PROCESS DECISION PROGRAM CHART\" section, p. 133."]},{"id":"c7q13","ch":7,"n":13,"q":"Near the end of his career, Deming agreed that one PDCA phase was too reactive for teams projecting what will happen, prompting the PDSA model. Which substitution was made?","o":["\"Plan\" was replaced with \"Prepare\"","\"Check\" was replaced with \"Study\"","\"Act\" was replaced with \"Standardize\"","\"Do\" was replaced with \"Deploy\""],"a":1,"x":["\"Check\" became \"Study\" (PDSA) because Check is reactive while Study fits projecting/ongoing observation.","Distractor DNA: A/C/D swap the wrong letters — pure close-difference trap on a four-letter acronym.","Reference: \"PDCA (PDSA AND SDCA)\" section, p. 130–131."]},{"id":"c7q14","ch":7,"n":14,"q":"Using a prioritization matrix, four software packages are scored and the row totals are: A = 2.80, B = 2.30, C = 3.00, D = 1.90. Which package does the matrix favor?","o":["Package A","Package B","Package C","Package D"],"a":2,"x":["The option with the highest total is most favored; C = 3.00.","Distractor DNA: Matches the worked example in Figure 7.13 exactly. The trap tempts test-takers who reverse the logic (lowest = best, as in some cost rankings).","Reference: \"PRIORITIZATION MATRIX\" / Figure 7.13, p. 131."]},{"id":"c7q15","ch":7,"n":15,"q":"A green belt wants to identify and visually display the relationships between the units of a training course and the course objectives. Which tool is the best fit?","o":["Matrix diagram","Affinity diagram","Activity network diagram","Cause-and-effect diagram"],"a":0,"x":["A matrix diagram displays relationships between two groups of items (course units × objectives).","Distractor DNA: Affinity (grouping), AND (sequencing), and C&E (causal) are all real tools that don't map \"two-group relationship.\"","Reference: \"MATRIX DIAGRAM\" / Figure 7.11, p. 127–129."]},{"id":"c7q16","ch":7,"n":16,"q":"A team leader is facilitating idea generation on a sensitive topic where a few members dominate. Members silently write ideas for 10–15 minutes with no verbal interaction. Which tool is being used?","o":["Brainstorming","Focus group","Nominal group technique","Multivoting"],"a":2,"x":["Silent, written, equal-participation idea generation for sensitive topics = NGT (\"nominal\" = limited vocal interaction).","Distractor DNA: Brainstorming (vocal/open) is the near-miss; focus group and multivoting are real but mismatched.","Reference: \"NOMINAL GROUP TECHNIQUE\" section, p. 129."]},{"id":"c7q17","ch":7,"n":17,"q":"Which statement best describes the relationship between multivoting and nominal group technique (NGT)?","o":["Multivoting replaces NGT entirely","Multivoting complements NGT but can also be used independently","Multivoting can only be used after a SWOT analysis","Multivoting is a type of audit"],"a":1,"x":["Multivoting complements NGT but can be used independently (e.g., to prioritize brainstorming results).","Distractor DNA: A overstates the relationship; C/D are nonsense pairings testing whether the candidate read the qualifier.","Reference: \"MULTIVOTING\" section, p. 129."]},{"id":"c7q18","ch":7,"n":18,"q":"A green belt breaks a general topic into successively more specific contributing activities, posting two to five sub-topics under each level. Which tool is being constructed?","o":["Tree diagram","Interrelationship digraph","SIPOC","Prioritization matrix"],"a":0,"x":["The tree diagram breaks a general topic into progressively specific contributing activities.","Distractor DNA: Digraph (influence arrows) and SIPOC (process map) are functionally distinct; prioritization matrix is a decision tool.","Reference: \"TREE DIAGRAM\" section, p. 137."]},{"id":"c7q19","ch":7,"n":19,"q":"Which of the following is not true regarding a SWOT analysis as described in the chapter?","o":["Strengths and weaknesses provide an introspective, internal view","Opportunities and threats look outside the organization","Strengths and weaknesses should be considered direct opposites","It is a strategic planning tool applicable to a business or project objective"],"a":2,"x":["The text explicitly says strengths and weaknesses should not be considered opposites. Negative stem isolates this false claim.","Distractor DNA: A, B, D are all verbatim-true SWOT statements; the trap rewards reading the \"not opposites\" caveat.","Reference: \"SWOT\" section, p. 138 (\"Strengths and weaknesses should not be considered opposites\")."]},{"id":"c7q20","ch":7,"n":20,"q":"When developing a SIPOC diagram, after defining the process and its boundaries, what is the correct next step?","o":["Identify the internal and external suppliers","Identify the outputs of the process","Identify the inputs to the process","Identify the internal and external customers"],"a":1,"x":["The build sequence is: define process/boundaries outputs inputs customers back to suppliers.","Distractor DNA: A, C, D are all genuine SIPOC steps placed out of order — a sequencing trap (Analyze-level per II.A.4).","Reference: \"SIPOC DIAGRAM\" section, p. 135 (\"Next, identify the outputs of the process…\")."]},{"id":"c7q21","ch":7,"n":21,"q":"A green belt is calculating the risk priority number (RPN) from FMEA data. Which formula is correct?","o":["RPN = Severity + Occurrence + Detection","RPN = Severity × Occurrence × Detection","RPN = Severity × Occurrence ÷ Detection","RPN = (Severity + Occurrence) × Detection"],"a":1,"x":["RPN = Severity × Occurrence × Detection.","Distractor DNA: A (addition) is the most common error; C/D mix operations. Matches the multiplication emphasis in sample Q93.","Reference: \"RISK PRIORITY NUMBER\" section, p. 134 (\"RPN = S × D × O\")."]},{"id":"c7q22","ch":7,"n":22,"q":"The chapter cites Dr. Juran's finding that 100 percent inspection is only about 80 percent effective. What is the recommended response to this limitation?","o":["Inspect every item twice to raise effectiveness","Take samples, preferably randomly, from the population","Replace inspection with a SWOT analysis","Abandon inspection in favor of focus groups"],"a":1,"x":["Because 100% inspection is only ~80% effective, the recommended solution is random sampling from the population.","Distractor DNA: A doubles down on the failing approach; C/D substitute unrelated tools — testing whether the candidate connects the Juran quote to its remedy.","Reference: \"SAMPLING PLAN\" section, p. 135 (\"One solution to this is to take samples (preferably randomly)…\")."]},{"id":"c8q1","ch":8,"n":1,"q":"When should the measures for project results be identified?","o":["After the project charter is signed off","During the Control phase","During the initial planning stages, then refined as the project progresses","Only once baseline data has been fully collected"],"a":2,"x":["Measures are identified during initial planning and refined as the project progresses.","DNA: Distractors A/B/D each name a real but wrong timing point.","Ref: Opening paragraph, \"The measures for project results should be identified during the initial plan-ning stages and be refined…\""]},{"id":"c8q2","ch":8,"n":2,"q":"Which two tools are described as expressing business performance measures?","o":["CPI and SPI","Balanced scorecard and performance to established goals","DPMO and RTY","Cp and Cpk"],"a":1,"x":["Business performance measures = balanced scorecard + performance to established goals.","DNA: A (CPI/SPI) and C (DPMO/RTY) are project and process measures respectively — correct concepts at the wrong level.","Ref: \"Business performance measures are usually expressed through tools known as: Balanced scorecard / Performance to established goals.\""]},{"id":"c8q3","ch":8,"n":3,"q":"Who developed the balanced scorecard?","o":["Juran and Feigenbaum","Deming and Shewhart","Kaplan and Norton","Hersey and Blanchard"],"a":2,"x":["Kaplan and Norton, early 1990s.","DNA: Classic pioneer-swap distractors (Juran/Feigenbaum belong to COPQ later in the chapter).","Ref: \"Dr. Robert Kaplan and Dr. David Norton developed the balanced scorecard in the early 1990s.\""]},{"id":"c8q4","ch":8,"n":4,"q":"According to the chapter, what was the research-based limitation of using only financial measurement?","o":["It is too expensive to collect","It is restricted to past events and is only one part of the business","It cannot be audited","It ignores supplier performance"],"a":1,"x":["Financial measurement is restricted to past events and is only one part of the business.","Ref: \"the financial aspect of measurement is restricted to past events and is only one part of the business.\""]},{"id":"c8q5","ch":8,"n":5,"q":"Which of the following is not one of the balanced scorecard's perspectives as correctly defined?","o":["Financial","Customer","Competitor benchmarking","Learning and growth"],"a":2,"x":["Competitor benchmarking is not a scorecard perspective. The four are financial, customer, internal processes, learning and growth.","DNA: \"not\" stem; plausible-sounding business term inserted. Note the chapter's text erroneously says \"five\" but lists four — answer keys off the four correct ones.","Ref: Balanced scorecard paragraph."]},{"id":"c8q6","ch":8,"n":6,"q":"For the Cost Performance Index (CPI), which ratio condition is the desirable state?","o":["A ratio of 1 or higher","A ratio less than 1","A ratio of exactly zero","A negative ratio"],"a":0,"x":["CPI ratio of 1 or higher is desirable.","Ref: \"A ratio of 1 or higher is the desirable condition.\""]},{"id":"c8q7","ch":8,"n":7,"q":"The Schedule Performance Index (SPI) is expressed as which ratio?","o":["Actual cost to budgeted cost","Earned value to planned value","Planned value to actual cost","Defects to opportunities"],"a":1,"x":["SPI = earned value ÷ planned value.","DNA: A inverts CPI's components; C scrambles.","Ref: \"Schedule performance index (SPI). … ratio of earned value to planned value.\""]},{"id":"c8q8","ch":8,"n":8,"q":"\"Defects per single line of code (SLOC)\" is identified in the chapter as a measure most associated with which type of project?","o":["Manufacturing projects","Software projects","Service projects","Supply chain projects"],"a":1,"x":["SLOC software projects.","Ref: \"Defects per single line of code (SLOC) for software projects.\""]},{"id":"c8q9","ch":8,"n":9,"q":"DPU is calculated as which of the following?","o":["Total defects ÷ total opportunities","Total defects ÷ total products produced in a time period","Total opportunities ÷ total products","Total products ÷ total defects"],"a":1,"x":["DPU = total defects ÷ total products produced in a period.","DNA: A is the DPO/DPMO setup (defects ÷ opportunities) — the classic DPU-vs-DPMO trap.","Ref: \"Defects (deficiencies) per unit (DPU). Calculated as the total number of defects divided by the total number of products produced…\""]},{"id":"c8q10","ch":8,"n":10,"q":"In the chapter's worked example, a unit has 165 defects and 240,000 total opportunities. What is the DPMO?","o":["68.75","687.5","6,875","1,650"],"a":1,"x":["(165 ÷ 240,000) × 10⁶ = 687.5. Verified: 165/240,000 = 0.0006875 ×1,000,000 = 687.5.","DNA: A/C are decimal-shift traps.","Ref: \"DPMO = DPO × 10⁶ = (165 ÷ 240,000) × 10⁶ = 687.5.\""]},{"id":"c8q11","ch":8,"n":11,"q":"In the DPMO example, dimensions are checked at three independent places on 40,000 items. How many opportunities for dimensional defects exist?","o":["40,000","80,000","120,000","240,000"],"a":2,"x":["3 × 40,000 = 120,000.","DNA: 40,000 (one place), 80,000 (rolled ends), 240,000 (grand total) are all real numbers from the same example — high-confusion distractors.","Ref: \"three independent places… 3 × 40,000 = 120,000 opportunities.\""]},{"id":"c8q12","ch":8,"n":12,"q":"Rolled throughput yield (RTY) for a series of processes is found by doing which of the following?","o":["Adding the individual process yields","Averaging the individual process yields","Multiplying the individual process yields","Subtracting defects from one"],"a":2,"x":["RTY = product (multiplication) of individual yields.","DNA: \"Adding/averaging\" exploits the instinct to combine yields linearly.","Ref: \"found by multiplying the individual process yields.\""]},{"id":"c8q13","ch":8,"n":13,"q":"Four processes have yields of .994, .987, .951, and .990. What is the approximate RTY?","o":[".924",".951",".980",".990"],"a":0,"x":[".994 × .987 × .951 × .990 ≈ .924. Verified: = 0.92387 ≈ .924.","DNA: .951/.990 are input values dropped in as distractors.","Ref: \"RTY = .994 × .987 × .951 × .990 = .924.\""]},{"id":"c8q14","ch":8,"n":14,"q":"The often-quoted 3.4 ppm defect level for a six sigma process is derived using which assumption?","o":["The process mean never shifts","The process mean may shift ±1.5σ","The process is exactly normal to the sixth decimal","The tolerance equals ±3σ"],"a":1,"x":["The 1.5σ mean-shift assumption yields the 3.4 ppm figure.","DNA: C/D are caveats the chapter explicitly raises (\"no process is truly normal to the sixth decimal\") — true statements that are not the derivation basis.","Ref: \"we allow the mean to shift ±1.5σ… 3.4 ppm.\""]},{"id":"c8q15","ch":8,"n":15,"q":"Cp is defined in the chapter as which of the following?","o":["(USL − LSL) ÷ six sigma","The lesser of (USL − mean)/3σ or (mean − LSL)/3σ","1 ÷ Cp","The area under the normal curve between ±3σ"],"a":0,"x":["Cp = (USL − LSL) ÷ six sigma.","DNA: B is the Cpk definition; C is the Cr definition — both correct definitions of the wrong index (signature ASQ trap).","Ref: \"Cp is the ratio of tolerance to six sigma, or USL minus LSL divided by six sigma.\""]},{"id":"c8q16","ch":8,"n":16,"q":"Which statement about the capability ratio (Cr) is correct per the chapter?","o":["The higher the value of Cr, the better","Cr is the ratio of 1 divided by Cp, and the lower the Cr, the better","Cr is the lesser of two one-sided indices","Cr equals USL minus LSL divided by six sigma"],"a":1,"x":["Cr = 1 ÷ Cp; lower is better, 1 being historical maximum.","DNA: A inverts the \"lower is better\" rule; D swaps in the Cp definition.","Ref: \"Capability ratio (Cr) is the ratio of 1 divided by Cp. The lower the value of Cr, the better.\""]},{"id":"c8q17","ch":8,"n":17,"q":"COPQ was originally called what, and traces to which pioneers?","o":["Cost of Current Quality; Kaplan and Norton","Cost of Quality; Juran and Feigenbaum","Cost of Failure; Deming and Crosby","Cost of Appraisal; Taguchi and Ishikawa"],"a":1,"x":["COQ Juran and Feigenbaum.","DNA: A correctly names the modern alias (COCQ) but wrong pioneers.","Ref: \"COPQ was originally called Cost of Quality (COQ)… dates back to the original writing of Juran and Feigenbaum.\""]},{"id":"c8q18","ch":8,"n":18,"q":"Which of the following is not one of the four basic COPQ categories?","o":["Prevention","Appraisal","Containment","Internal failures"],"a":2,"x":["\"Containment\" is not a COPQ category. The four: Prevention, Appraisal, Internal failures, External failures.","DNA: \"Containment\" borrows from 8D language — a real Six Sigma term planted as a distractor.","Ref: COPQ four-measure list."]},{"id":"c8q19","ch":8,"n":19,"q":"Costs of work performed incorrectly and caught by the customer belong to which COPQ category?","o":["Prevention","Appraisal","Internal failures","External failures"],"a":3,"x":["Caught by the customer = External failures.","DNA: Internal vs. external failure hinges solely on \"before shipment\" vs. \"by the customer.\"","Ref: \"External failures—the costs of work incorrectly performed and caught by the customer.\""]},{"id":"c8q20","ch":8,"n":20,"q":"According to the chapter, what is the key point to remember about reducing the overall total cost of quality?","o":["Increase appraisal/inspection spending","Focus on prevention costs","Eliminate internal failure tracking","Shift all costs to external failure"],"a":1,"x":["Focus on prevention costs to drive total cost of quality down.","Ref: \"by focusing on the prevention of error (prevention costs), the overall total cost of quality will decrease.\""]},{"id":"c8q21","ch":8,"n":21,"q":"Per the chapter, which communication flow is described as effective in \"flatter\" organizations but unsuitable where a formal authorization/approval process must be followed?","o":["Top-down flow","Bottom-up flow","Horizontal communication","Diagonal communication"],"a":2,"x":["Horizontal communication — effective in flat orgs, unsuitable where authorization/approval process exists.","DNA: \"Diagonal\" (D) is a real org-comm term not in this chapter — fringe distractor.","Ref: Horizontal communication bullet."]},{"id":"c8q22","ch":8,"n":22,"q":"The chapter gives explicit guidance on delivery of messages. Which pairing is correct?","o":["Reprimand in public; appreciation in private","Reprimand in private; appreciation in public","Both reprimand and appreciation by e-mail","Sensitive issues by e-mail to avoid emotion"],"a":1,"x":["Reprimand in private; appreciation in public.","DNA: D (\"sensitive by e-mail\") directly contradicts the text (\"E-mails are not good at communicating emotions\").","Ref: \"Reprimanding must be done in private, whereas appreciation has to be given in public.\""]},{"id":"c9q1","ch":9,"n":1,"q":"A Green Belt is assembling a team and wants to balance interaction quality against manageability. According to the handbook, what is considered the ideal team size?","o":["Three members","Five members","Seven members","Eleven members"],"a":2,"x":["The text states a team usually comprises five to nine members, \"seven is considered an ideal size.\"","Distractor logic: B (five) and D (eleven) are the boundary values the chapter discusses (under five = easier to manage; over nine = counterproductive), baiting candidates who confuse the range with the ideal. Classic boundary-confusion trap.","Reference: Team Formation section, line 24 (Handbook p. 144)."]},{"id":"c9q2","ch":9,"n":2,"q":"Which of the following statements about team composition is correct ?","o":["Subject matter experts are always part of the core team, while stakeholders may remain as extended members.","Stakeholders are always part of the team, while subject matter experts sometimes remain outside as resource or extended members.","Both stakeholders and subject matter experts must remain outside the core team as resources.","Neither stakeholders nor subject matter experts should be included until the storming stage."],"a":1,"x":["\"Stakeholders are always part of the team… Subject matter experts sometimes remain outside the team as resource or extended team members.\"","Distractor logic: A reverses the two roles — the signature ASQ \"correct-statement-with-roles-swapped\" trap. C and D are absolutes contradicted by the text.","Reference: Team Formation, lines 28–32 (p. 144)."]},{"id":"c9q3","ch":9,"n":3,"q":"A multinational project relies heavily on voice-only teleconferences. Which challenge of virtual teaming is most directly heightened by this arrangement?","o":["Increased project costs","Inability to share data in real time","Loss of nonverbal communication leading to potential miscommunication","Excessive face-to-face conflict"],"a":2,"x":["The chapter specifically flags miscommunication \"especially with voice-only teleconferencing, as the important factor of nonverbal communication is lost.\"","Distractor logic: A and B are listed as benefits of virtual teams, not challenges — distractors that state true facts about the wrong category. D inverts the actual challenge (reduced, not excessive, conflict).","Reference: Virtual Teams, lines 50–55 (p. 145)."]},{"id":"c9q4","ch":9,"n":4,"q":"According to Tuckman's developmental sequence, what is the correct order of the four primary team stages?","o":["Forming, norming, storming, performing","Storming, forming, norming, performing","Forming, storming, norming, performing","Norming, forming, storming, performing"],"a":2,"x":["Tuckman's sequence is forming, storming, norming, performing.","Distractor logic: A, B, D scramble the order — a pure sequence-recall trap (Remember-level filter inside an Understand topic).","Reference: Team Stages and Dynamics, lines 73–75 (p. 145)."]},{"id":"c9q5","ch":9,"n":5,"q":"A newly chartered team is enthusiastic, members are agreeing readily, little real work is happening, and there is a strong \"honeymoon\" feeling. Which stage best describes this team?","o":["Forming","Storming","Norming","Performing"],"a":0,"x":["Forming = getting to know each other, immature group, agreeing too much, little work, \"honeymoon period.\"","Distractor logic: \"Agreeing too much\" + \"honeymoon\" are the discriminators. Storming (B) is conflict; norming (C) is conflict resolution; performing (D) is high output — all contradicted by \"little work accomplished.\"","Reference: Stage 1: Forming, lines 85–94 (p. 146)."]},{"id":"c9q6","ch":9,"n":6,"q":"During the storming stage, what is the appropriate leadership style?","o":["Directing","Coaching","Supporting","Delegating"],"a":1,"x":["Storming's appropriate style is coaching — continued close supervision plus emerging supportive behavior.","Distractor logic: This is the highest-yield trap in the chapter: each stage maps to exactly one style. Directing (A) = forming, supporting (C) = norming, delegating (D) = performing.","Reference: Team Leadership – Storming, lines 158–161 (p. 148)."]},{"id":"c9q7","ch":9,"n":7,"q":"A team is resolving its earlier conflicts, members are beginning to trust each other and share work products without hesitation, and some work is now getting done. Which leadership style is most appropriate at this point?","o":["Directing","Coaching","Supporting","Delegating"],"a":2,"x":["Conflict resolution + emerging trust = norming, whose style is supporting (reduced directive, increased supportive behavior, encouraging decision-taking).","Distractor logic: The stem deliberately omits the stage name, forcing the candidate to diagnose \"norming\" from behavior, then map the style. Coaching (B) is the adjacent storming style — the nearest miss.","Reference: Stage 3: Norming (lines 111–116) + Team Leadership – Norming (lines 164–170) (pp. 147–148)."]},{"id":"c9q8","ch":9,"n":8,"q":"Carlos leads a mature, high-synergy team whose members recognize their interdependence and are accomplishing a large volume of work. Which leadership style should Carlos now adopt?","o":["Directing","Coaching","Supporting","Delegating"],"a":3,"x":["A mature, synergistic, interdependent, high-output team is performing; its style is delegating (leader reduces directive and supportive day-to-day involvement, still monitors goals).","Distractor logic: \"Mature/synergy/interdependence/large volume of work\" are the verbatim performing-stage markers. The other styles belong to earlier, less-mature stages.","Reference: Stage 4: Performing (lines 119–123) + Team Leadership – Performing (lines 171–175) (pp. 147–148)."]},{"id":"c9q9","ch":9,"n":9,"q":"A project undergoes a major change in scope and several team members are replaced. According to the chapter, what is the most likely effect on team dynamics?","o":["The team remains in the performing stage","The team advances directly to adjourning","The team tends to revert to an earlier stage, possibly forming","The team is permanently disbanded"],"a":2,"x":["\"Major changes can result in going back to forming stage\"; team dynamics \"tend to go back to one of the earlier stages.\"","Distractor logic: D (\"permanently disbanded\") overreads adjourning; A ignores the disruption; B skips the regression the text emphasizes. Tests the nuance that adjourning is not always terminal.","Reference: Stage 5: Transitioning/Adjourning, lines 124–130 (p. 147)."]},{"id":"c9q10","ch":9,"n":10,"q":"Which of the following is least accurately described as a stage of team evolution in the chapter?","o":["Recognition","Adjourning/transitioning","Norming","Benchmarking"],"a":3,"x":["Benchmarking is a Define-phase comparison tool, not a team-evolution stage. The chapter's six stages are forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning/transitioning, and recognition.","Distractor logic: Negative-stem item. A, B, C are all genuine stages (including the two \"additional\" stages the chapter adds beyond Tuckman). The wrong answer is a real Six Sigma term planted from a different topic — the bank's hallmark cross-topic distractor.","Reference: Team Stages and Dynamics, lines 58–76, 124–138 (pp. 145–147)."]},{"id":"c9q11","ch":9,"n":11,"q":"Team interaction is limited to a few influential members (a major shareholder and a founder), and the remaining members stay in listening mode and hesitate to voice opinions. Which negative team dynamic is present?","o":["Dominant member(s)","Overbearing member(s)","Groupthink","Floundering"],"a":1,"x":["Interaction limited to a few influential members (shareholder, founder, SME) while others stay silent = overbearing member(s).","Distractor logic: Dominant (A) is the deliberate near-miss — but dominant members cause chaos/interrupting, whereas overbearing members cause intimidated silence via legitimate authority. This overbearing-vs-dominant pair is the chapter's most testable close-difference.","Reference: Table 9.1, Overbearing member(s), lines 207–224 (p. 149)."]},{"id":"c9q12","ch":9,"n":12,"q":"In a team meeting, no one voices public disagreement, yet several members later express serious doubts in private conversations. Which negative dynamic does this indicate, and what is an appropriate countermeasure?","o":["Feuding; confront the adversaries offline","Groupthink; bring in independent members from outside the team","Attribution; revise the deadline","Discounts; enforce meeting time allotments"],"a":1,"x":["No public disagreement + private doubts + fear that cohesiveness is at stake = groupthink; countermeasure is bringing in independent outside members (also rotate roles, management by fact).","Distractor logic: Each option correctly pairs a different dynamic with its own real countermeasure (feuding offline; attribution deadline is itself a mismatch baited from \"rush\"; discounts time). Only B matches symptom to dynamic to fix — testing whether the candidate reads the whole row.","Reference: Table 9.1, Groupthink, lines 319–328 (p. 151)."]},{"id":"c9q13","ch":9,"n":13,"q":"A team is proceeding in an unsteady, faltering manner because its high-profile leader rarely attends meetings and the organization is undergoing major change. Which negative dynamic is described?","o":["Reluctant participants","Floundering","Digressions and tangents","Rush to accomplishment"],"a":1,"x":["\"Proceeding in an unsteady, faltering manner\" from \"lack of team direction\" and absentee high-profile leadership = floundering.","Distractor logic: Reluctant participants (A) involves individual disinterest, not team-wide faltering; digressions (C) is off-agenda straying; rush (D) is speed-driven. All are real dynamics, only one matches \"faltering + no direction.\"","Reference: Table 9.1, Floundering, lines 246–262 (pp. 149–150)."]},{"id":"c9q14","ch":9,"n":14,"q":"A team member consistently shows disinterest and provides noncommittal feedback because they have no real stake in the team's outcome. Which negative dynamic does this represent?","o":["Discounts","Attribution","Reluctant participant","Unquestioned acceptance of opinions as facts"],"a":2,"x":["Disinterest + noncommittal feedback + no stake in the outcome = reluctant participant.","Distractor logic: Discounts (A) is members ignoring each other; attribution (B) is emotional judgment; unquestioned acceptance (D) is unsupported claims. The \"no stake\" phrase is the unique identifier.","Reference: Table 9.1, Reluctant participants, lines 287–299 (p. 150)."]},{"id":"c9q15","ch":9,"n":15,"q":"Members repeatedly present conclusions without supporting data or analysis. According to the chapter, what is the team leader's most appropriate countermeasure?","o":["Confront the members offline to avoid public embarrassment","Request supporting data, analysis, and statistically valid conclusions","Revise the deadline to a more realistic one","Rotate roles and responsibilities at milestones"],"a":1,"x":["Presenting conclusions without backing data = unquestioned acceptance of opinions as facts; countermeasure is requesting supporting data, analysis, and statistically valid conclusions.","Distractor logic: A is the feuding countermeasure, C the rush to accomplishment countermeasure, D the groupthink countermeasure — all real fixes pulled from other rows of Table 9.1 to punish row-mismatching.","Reference: Table 9.1, Unquestioned acceptance of opinions as facts, lines 308–318 (pp. 150–151)."]},{"id":"c9q16","ch":9,"n":16,"q":"Members are making casual references and reaching emotional or psychological judgments rather than seeking explanations for the data. Which dynamic is this, and which technique does the chapter recommend?","o":["Groupthink; management by fact","Rush to accomplishment; ask for alternate solutions","Attribution; use a devil's advocate approach to challenge assumptions","Feuding; enforce mutual respect"],"a":2,"x":["Casual references + emotional/psychological judgments + not seeking explanations = attribution; the chapter prescribes the devil's advocate approach and challenging assumptions.","Distractor logic: Note the contrast with the official sample bank (Q17), which loosely attached \"attribution\" to a non-discussion scenario — this item is anchored strictly to the chapter's actual definition. Each distractor pairs a real dynamic with its own real countermeasure, isolating attribution by the \"emotional judgment\" cue.","Reference: Table 9.1, Attribution, lines 364–371 (p. 152)."]},{"id":"c9q17","ch":9,"n":17,"q":"A team meeting has become chaotic and hard to follow because two members keep interrupting and dominating the entire discussion. Which countermeasure best fits this specific dynamic?","o":["Bring in an outsider to ask exploratory questions","Structure the agenda to provide equal participation and use round-robin moderation","Cancel the project and reassign resources","Request statistically valid conclusions"],"a":1,"x":["Chaotic meeting + interrupting + a few members dominating = dominant member(s); countermeasure is structuring the agenda for equal participation with effective moderation/round-robin.","Distractor logic: A is the overbearing/groupthink outsider fix; D is the unquestioned-acceptance fix. The trap rewards distinguishing dominant (chaos) from overbearing (intimidated silence) and selecting the matching countermeasure.","Reference: Table 9.1, Dominant member(s), lines 225–237 (p. 149)."]},{"id":"c9q18","ch":9,"n":18,"q":"Hostilities have escalated into heated arguments, slowing progress and lowering team morale. What does the chapter recommend the leader do?","o":["Confront the adversaries publicly in the meeting to resolve it immediately","Confront the adversaries offline, not in the team meeting, and enforce mutual respect","Disband the team and start over","Ignore the conflict until the norming stage resolves it"],"a":1,"x":["Heated arguments + low morale = feuding; the chapter explicitly warns that confronting in public \"can worsen the situation\" and advises confronting adversaries offline plus enforcing discipline and mutual respect.","Distractor logic: A is the exact wrong action the text cautions against — a \"looks reasonable but textbook-prohibited\" trap. C and D are over/under-reactions.","Reference: Table 9.1, Feuding, lines 329–343 (pp. 151–152)."]},{"id":"c9q19","ch":9,"n":19,"q":"A RACI chart is being expanded so that team members who will provide support for the project are explicitly captured. Which chart should the team use?","o":["SIPOC","RASIC","PERT","PDPC"],"a":1,"x":["RASIC adds S upporting to the RACI framework, capturing members who provide project support.","Distractor logic: SIPOC (A), PERT (C), and PDPC (D) are real Six Sigma/Define-phase tools planted as cross-topic distractors. Candidate must recall the RACI RASIC extension specifically.","Reference: RACI section, lines 405–417 (p. 153)."]},{"id":"c9q20","ch":9,"n":20,"q":"During the creative phase of a brainstorming session, which of the following guidelines is correct ?","o":["Quality of ideas is prioritized over quantity","Members should critique weak ideas immediately to save time","Wild ideas are welcome and no criticism is permitted","Evaluation should begin immediately after the last idea, with no break"],"a":2,"x":["Creative-phase rules: no criticism/compliments/questions, wild ideas welcome, don't wait, quantity over quality, hitchhike on prior ideas.","Distractor logic: A inverts quantity/quality; B violates the no-criticism rule; D omits the required 10-minute break before evaluation. Each distractor flips one explicit guideline — fine-grained recall trap.","Reference: Brainstorming, lines 726–745 (pp. 159–160)."]},{"id":"c9q21","ch":9,"n":21,"q":"A facilitator is dealing with a sensitive, controversial topic and a group in which a few members tend to dominate. The team is given 10–15 minutes to silently generate ideas with no verbal interaction. Which tool is being used?","o":["Multivoting","Affinity diagram","Nominal group technique","Top-down management"],"a":2,"x":["Silent idea generation (10–15 min), no verbal interaction, used for sensitive/controversial topics and to balance vocal members = nominal group technique.","Distractor logic: B (affinity diagram) is the organizing step that follows idea generation; A (multivoting) is the prioritizing step that follows NGT; D is a decision style, not an idea-generation tool. Tests the NGT-vs-brainstorming-vs-multivoting sequence.","Reference: Nominal Group Technique, lines 921–938 (p. 164)."]},{"id":"c9q22","ch":9,"n":22,"q":"A team has generated 34 ideas and now needs to narrow them to the five most impactful, using either a ranking or a \"$100/100-point\" allocation approach. Which decision-making tool is described?","o":["Consensus decision-making","Multivoting","Brainstorming","Fact-based decision-making"],"a":1,"x":["Narrowing many ideas to the top five/ten via ranking (non-weighted) or a $100/100-point (weighted) allocation = multivoting.","Distractor logic: Brainstorming (C) generates ideas rather than narrowing them; consensus (A) and fact-based (D) are decision philosophies, not prioritization mechanics. The \"$100/100-point\" cue uniquely fingerprints the weighted multivoting method.","Reference: Multivoting, lines 939–984 (pp. 164–166)."]},{"id":"c10q1","ch":10,"n":1,"q":"According to the chapter, which standard defines a process as \"a set of interrelated or interacting activities that transforms inputs into outputs\"?","o":["ISO 5807:1985","ISO 9001","ISO 9000","ISO 14001"],"a":2,"x":["ISO 9000 (Fundamentals and vocabulary) is the standard that defines a process. DNA mimic: every distractor is a real ISO standard correctly named but wrong in scope — ISO 5807 (flowchart symbols), ISO 9001 (QMS requirements), ISO 14001 (environmental). Classic \"correct definition of the wrong concept.\"","Reference: Process Maps and Flowcharts, lines 11–13 (handbook p. 168)."]},{"id":"c10q2","ch":10,"n":2,"q":"Practitioners often use \"process mapping\" and \"flowcharting\" interchangeably. What is the key difference the chapter describes?","o":["Flowcharts include cost and cycle-time data, while process maps show only the sequence of steps","Process maps include additional process details (such as cost, cycle time, and inventory) beyond the flowchart","Process maps use ISO 5807 symbols, while flowcharts use value-stream icons","Flowcharts are used only for new processes, while process maps are used only for existing processes"],"a":1,"x":["Process mapping = flowchart plus added detail (cost, setup/cycle time, inventory, defect types/probability). DNA mimic: Option A is a reversal trap (swaps which tool carries the detail); C swaps icon attribution; D invents a new/existing rule.","Reference: lines 14–17 and 33–40 (handbook p. 168–169)."]},{"id":"c10q3","ch":10,"n":3,"q":"Which international standard helps ensure that different individuals interpret process maps the same way by standardizing documentation symbols and conventions?","o":["ISO 9000","ISO 5807:1985","ISO 9001","ISO/TS 16949"],"a":1,"x":["ISO 5807:1985 standardizes flowchart documentation symbols/conventions so maps are interpreted consistently. DNA mimic: pairs against ISO 9000 (defines a process, Q1) and ISO 9001 to exploit standard-number confusion.","Reference: lines 25–28 (handbook p. 168)."]},{"id":"c10q4","ch":10,"n":4,"q":"Which risk analysis tool is described as beginning with process mapping?","o":["SWOT analysis","Process failure mode and effects analysis (PFMEA)","Control chart","Histogram"],"a":1,"x":["The text states PFMEA starts with process mapping; process mapping is \"often the first step in improving a process.\" DNA mimic: SWOT is a genuine risk/strategy tool (right family, not built on process maps); control chart/histogram are Measure-phase tools.","Reference: lines 28–30 (handbook p. 168)."]},{"id":"c10q5","ch":10,"n":5,"q":"Which statement about value stream mapping (VSM) is correct according to the chapter?","o":["VSM is unrelated to process mapping and uses no standardized icons","VSM is a type of process mapping but uses different mapping icons","VSM and basic flowcharts use identical mapping icons","VSM replaces process mapping as the first step in process improvement"],"a":1,"x":["VSM is a type of process mapping but uses different icons. DNA mimic: A denies the relationship, C claims identical icons (false), D overstates sequencing — all subtle near-misses around one true sentence.","Reference: lines 30–32 (handbook p. 168)."]},{"id":"c10q6","ch":10,"n":6,"q":"In standard flowchart notation, which characteristic distinguishes a decision (diamond) symbol from a process (rectangle) symbol?","o":["The decision symbol has only one arrow leaving it; the process symbol has several","More than one arrow leaves the decision symbol; usually only one arrow leaves the process symbol","The decision symbol marks a delay or wait in the process","The decision symbol links the flow to another page"],"a":1,"x":["Decision diamond more than one exit arrow (one per answer); process box usually one exit arrow. DNA mimic: A is a direct reversal; C and D describe other symbols (delay, off-page connector).","Reference: lines 63–68 (Figure 10.1, handbook p. 169)."]},{"id":"c10q7","ch":10,"n":7,"q":"A green belt is mapping an order-fulfillment process that hands off work among the sales, finance, and warehouse departments. Which approach best clarifies which department performs each step ?","o":["SIPOC diagram","Spaghetti diagram","Swim lane (cross-functional) flowchart","Cause-and-effect diagram"],"a":2,"x":["Swim-lane (cross-functional) flowcharts arrange blocks under the lane of the department/function performing each step — purpose-built for multi-department handoffs. DNA mimic: SIPOC is high-level (the \"detail vs. high-level\" trap), spaghetti = physical movement, C&E = causes.","Reference: lines 48–53, 159 (Figure 10.3, handbook p. 170–171)."]},{"id":"c10q8","ch":10,"n":8,"q":"While documenting an existing process, a team is brainstorming and walking the process to list every step. According to the chapter's flowcharting procedure, what is true at this stage?","o":["The team should immediately arrange the steps in correct sequence","The team should not worry about sequence yet, only collect all the steps","The team should draw the flowchart symbols before listing steps","The team should verify the chart matches the process before listing steps"],"a":1,"x":["Step 2 of the procedure: gather all steps via brainstorming/walking — \"not worried about sequence\" yet. DNA mimic: A/C/D are real later steps placed out of order (order-of-operations trap).","Reference: Creating a Flowchart, lines 167–186 (handbook p. 169–171)."]},{"id":"c10q9","ch":10,"n":9,"q":"For a process that does not yet exist, when should the flowchart be developed?","o":["After the process is implemented, to document what occurred","Before implementing the process","Only during a compliance audit","After the first customer complaint"],"a":1,"x":["A flowchart for a new process should be developed before implementing it. DNA mimic: A is the adjacent existing-process rule applied wrongly; C/D are implausible triggers.","Reference: lines 162–165 (handbook p. 171)."]},{"id":"c10q10","ch":10,"n":10,"q":"Which of the following is NOT identified in the chapter as a common mistake in process mapping?","o":["Inadequate or inappropriate team representation","Spending too much time creating a \"perfect\" diagram instead of focusing on the process","Updating the flowchart whenever the process changes","Failing to walk an existing process to capture what is actually done"],"a":2,"x":["Updating the flowchart when the process changes is recommended practice, not a mistake. A, B, D are explicitly listed mistakes. DNA mimic: NOT-stem where the odd-one-out is a best practice.","Reference: Mistakes list lines 201–209; \"update the flowchart\" line 192–193 (handbook p. 172)."]},{"id":"c10q11","ch":10,"n":11,"q":"Written procedures are designed to describe all of the following EXCEPT:","o":["What is done during the process","Why it is done (business reason)","How it is done, step by step","When it is done (the trigger)"],"a":2,"x":["Procedures cover What / Why / Where / When. \"How, step by step\" belongs to work instructions. DNA mimic: the procedure work-instruction boundary trap — the most heavily tested distinction in this section.","Reference: Procedures lines 218–222 vs. Work instructions lines 223–225 (handbook p. 172–173)."]},{"id":"c10q12","ch":10,"n":12,"q":"An organization has no internal procedure for a minor activity and is not required to document it by any standard, yet the activity must still be performed the same way every time. The chapter calls this consistently followed but unwritten process a:","o":["Work instruction","Method","Standard operating procedure","Control plan"],"a":1,"x":["An unwritten-but-consistently-followed process (no internal procedure, none required by a standard) is a method. DNA mimic: A/C are written documents; D is a control-phase artifact.","Reference: lines 226–230 (handbook p. 172–173)."]},{"id":"c10q13","ch":10,"n":13,"q":"When deciding which processes should be documented, which of the following is NOT listed in the chapter as a factor to consider?","o":["Effect on quality","Statutory and/or regulatory requirements","Number of competitors in the market","Complexity of the process"],"a":2,"x":["\"Number of competitors\" is not among the documentation-decision factors. Listed factors: effect on quality, risk of customer dissatisfaction, statutory/regulatory, economic risk, effectiveness/efficiency, competence of personnel, complexity. DNA mimic: a business-sounding-but-unlisted distractor.","Reference: lines 231–239 (handbook p. 173)."]},{"id":"c10q14","ch":10,"n":14,"q":"According to the chapter, work instructions can be documented in all of the following forms EXCEPT:","o":["Checklists","Photographs or drawn pictures","Videos or electronic screen shots","Risk priority numbers (RPNs)"],"a":3,"x":["RPN is an FMEA output, not a work-instruction format. Listed formats: written instructions, checklists, flowcharts, photos, drawn pictures, videos, screen shots, software-driven steps. DNA mimic: pulls a real Six Sigma term from a different tool into a NOT-stem.","Reference: lines 240–248 (handbook p. 173)."]},{"id":"c10q15","ch":10,"n":15,"q":"To keep a SIPOC chart at an appropriate high level, the chapter recommends limiting the process portion to how many high-level process steps?","o":["Between 1 and 3","Between 4 and 7","Between 8 and 12","As many as needed to show every step"],"a":1,"x":["SIPOC process portion limit to 4–7 high-level steps. DNA mimic: numeric distractors bracket the answer; D defeats the high-level intent.","Reference: lines 283–285 (handbook p. 174). (See source-accountability note below — some references cite 5–7; this chapter says 4–7.)"]},{"id":"c10q16","ch":10,"n":16,"q":"A team new to a project needs a quick, enterprise-level view of suppliers, inputs, processes, outputs, and customers to grasp the project's scope. Which tool provides this \"bird's-eye\" view?","o":["Detailed swim lane flowchart","SIPOC chart","Measles chart","Relationship matrix"],"a":1,"x":["SIPOC = enterprise-level, \"bird's-eye\" map of S-I-P-O-C. DNA mimic: A is the detailed trap; C (measles) and D (relationship matrix) are real Chapter-10 tools with the wrong purpose.","Reference: lines 280–287 (handbook p. 174)."]},{"id":"c10q17","ch":10,"n":17,"q":"The cause-and-effect diagram is also known by which other names, and traditionally organizes causes into which category set for manufacturing?","o":["Pareto diagram; vital few and trivial many","Ishikawa or fishbone diagram; people, machine, material, methods, measurement, environment","Affinity diagram; natural groupings","Scatter diagram; correlated and uncorrelated variables"],"a":1,"x":["Cause-and-effect = Ishikawa / fishbone; manufacturing categories = people–machine–material–methods–measurement–environment (5M+E). DNA mimic: each distractor pairs a real tool with its real attribute (Pareto/80-20, affinity/groupings, scatter/correlation) — the signature \"correctly defined wrong concept.\"","Reference: lines 296–297, 340–342 (handbook p. 174–176)."]},{"id":"c10q18","ch":10,"n":18,"q":"A green belt is building a cause-and-effect diagram for a software-development defect (not a manufacturing process). Based on the chapter, what is the most appropriate approach to the category headings?","o":["Force-fit the standard machine and environment categories to every situation","Adapt the categories to the operation (e.g., people–processes–products–resources–miscellaneous) rather than force-fitting manufacturing categories","Eliminate categories entirely and list causes randomly","Use only the measurement category, since software is data-driven"],"a":1,"x":["Categories are flexible; adapt them to the operation (software: people-processes-products-resources-misc) and do not force-fit machine/environment. DNA mimic: A is the rote/default answer the text explicitly warns against — the trap rewards memorized \"6M\" over reading the scenario (an Analyze/Create discriminator).","Reference: lines 342–346 (handbook p. 176)."]},{"id":"c10q19","ch":10,"n":19,"q":"Which variation of the cause-and-effect diagram displays the fishbone on a large wall or board and invites employees to add causes using sticky notes or by writing directly on it?","o":["CEDAC (cause-and-effect diagram with the addition of cards)","Relationship matrix","Multivoting","Affinity diagram"],"a":0,"x":["CEDAC = fishbone on a wall/board with employee-added cards/sticky notes. DNA mimic: B/C/D are adjacent team tools (scoring, voting, idea-grouping).","Reference: lines 351–355 (handbook p. 176)."]},{"id":"c10q20","ch":10,"n":20,"q":"Which statement best reflects the chapter's guidance for an effective brainstorming session?","o":["The facilitator should immediately critique weak ideas to save time","Quantity of ideas is prioritized, and ideas should not be criticized or discarded during the session","Only subject-matter experts should contribute; quantity is unimportant","Ideas must be ranked for feasibility as each one is offered"],"a":1,"x":["Divergent thinking maximize quantity; \"there are no bad ideas\"; no criticism/discarding during the session. DNA mimic: A and D sound efficient/disciplined but violate the divergent-thinking rule; C wrongly restricts participation.","Reference: lines 306–307, 330–339 (handbook p. 175–176)."]},{"id":"c10q21","ch":10,"n":21,"q":"According to this chapter, a typical brainstorming session can generate approximately how many ideas?","o":["5 to 10","25 to 40","50 to 75","100 to 150"],"a":1,"x":["This chapter states a typical session yields 25 to 40 ideas. DNA mimic: plausible numeric ranges on either side.","Reference: lines 338–339 (handbook p. 176).","Source-accountability flag: Sample Questions.md (Q13, affinity-diagram item) states brainstorming produces 24–36 responses, citing the 2nd-edition handbook. This chapter (3rd-edition) says 25–40. On the live exam, answer to match whichever source the question cites; if unattributed, 25–40 reflects the current handbook. Tab both figures."]},{"id":"c10q22","ch":10,"n":22,"q":"A team includes members ranging from front-line staff to senior executives, the topic is emotionally sensitive, and a few executives tend to dominate. Which convergence tool best ensures balanced, unbiased input?","o":["Open group discussion","Nominal group technique (NGT)","Unstructured brainstorming","Management decision by the highest-ranking member"],"a":1,"x":["NGT's stated advantages map exactly onto this scenario: sensitive/emotional topics, mixed organizational layers, encourages quiet members, not swayed by dominant members better ownership. DNA mimic: A/C reintroduce the dominance problem NGT solves; D is the opposite of balanced input.","Reference: lines 358–367 (handbook p. 176)."]},{"id":"c10q23","ch":10,"n":23,"q":"In the multivoting technique described in the chapter, how are priorities assigned among the choices?","o":["Each member silently assigns a rank, and the ranks are tallied","One hundred points are divided among the choices based on relative importance","Causes are plotted in descending order with a cumulative percentage line","Ideas are grouped into natural categories on sticky notes"],"a":1,"x":["Multivoting 100 points distributed by relative importance. DNA mimic: A is NGT (silent rank + tally — the adjacent convergence tool), C is a Pareto chart, D is an affinity diagram. Three correct definitions of three wrong tools.","Reference: lines 357–358, 367–368 (handbook p. 176)."]},{"id":"c10q24","ch":10,"n":24,"q":"In a relationship matrix used to prioritize causes against effects, the degree of relationship is shown as strong, medium, and weak. Which numeric weighting corresponds to these levels?","o":["1 (strong), 3 (medium), 9 (weak)","9 (strong), 3 (medium), 1 (weak)","5 (strong), 3 (medium), 1 (weak)","10 (strong), 5 (medium), 1 (weak)"],"a":1,"x":["Strong = 9, medium = 3, weak = 1. DNA mimic: A is a reversal of the scale; C/D are plausible-but-wrong values (the 5/3/1 and 10/5/1 near-misses).","Reference: lines 561–566 (handbook p. 180–181)."]},{"id":"c10q25","ch":10,"n":25,"q":"Which feature distinguishes a true Pareto chart from an ordinary bar graph?","o":["It plots categories in alphabetical order","It arranges categories in descending order of frequency and adds a cumulative percentage line","It always displays exactly two categories","It shows frequency without any secondary axis"],"a":1,"x":["True Pareto = descending frequency order + \"other/miscellaneous\" pooling + secondary % axis + cumulative % line. DNA mimic: Option D literally describes the ordinary bar graph the stem contrasts against; A/C are arbitrary.","Reference: lines 420–427 (handbook p. 177)."]},{"id":"c10q26","ch":10,"n":26,"q":"The 80:20 principle underlying the Pareto chart is named for economist Vilfredo Pareto. Which quality leader is credited with first applying this principle to quality improvement?","o":["Walter Shewhart","Joseph M. Juran","W. Edwards Deming","Genichi Taguchi"],"a":1,"x":["Named for Vilfredo Pareto (economist, 80:20); credited to Joseph M. Juran for first applying it to quality. DNA mimic: pioneer-attribution trap — each distractor anchored to its own real contribution (Shewhart/control charts, Deming/14 Points-PDCA, Taguchi/loss function).","Reference: lines 427–430 (handbook p. 177)."]},{"id":"c10q27","ch":10,"n":27,"q":"A team's Pareto chart shows dozens of categories, each with only one or two occurrences, producing a flat \"vital many, trivial few\" pattern instead of a useful 80:20 picture. The defects, however, have specific physical locations on a large product. Which two corrective actions are most consistent with the chapter?","o":["Make the categories even more specific, and abandon Pareto analysis entirely","Generalize the data one level up before re-running the Pareto, and use a measles chart to capture specific defect locations","Switch to a control chart and stop collecting location data","Re-weight every defect as \"critical\" so the chart looks steeper"],"a":1,"x":["Over-granular data inverts Pareto into \"vital many / trivial few\"; remedy = generalize one level up and re-run; for location-specific defects on large products, use a measles chart. DNA mimic: A intensifies the documented failure mode (the strongest trap), C discards relevant data, D is a data-integrity violation disguised as analysis. This is a deliberate Create/Analyze synthesis item.","Reference: Pareto granularity lines 443–489; measles chart lines 540–559 (handbook p. 178–180)."]},{"id":"c11q1","ch":11,"n":1,"q":"Which multiplication rule may be applied to any two events A and B, with no assumption about their relationship?","o":["The special multiplication rule, P(A & B) = P(A) × P(B)","The special addition rule, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)","The general multiplication rule, P(A & B) = P(A) × P(B|A)","The complementation rule, P(not A) = 1 − P(A)"],"a":2,"x":["The general multiplication rule, P(A & B) = P(A) × P(B|A), is always true. Distractor A (special multiplication) is the DNA's \"correct-definition-wrong-condition\" trap — true only if A and B are independent. B is a different operation entirely (addition). D is a real rule but answers a different question (\"true but not here\").","Reference: Lines 13–15 (\"Special multiplication rule… General multiplication rule… [Always true]\")."]},{"id":"c11q2","ch":11,"n":2,"q":"The special multiplication rule, P(A & B) = P(A) × P(B), is valid only when the two events are:","o":["Mutually exclusive","Independent","Complementary","Conditional"],"a":1,"x":["Special multiplication requires independence. \"Mutually exclusive\" (A) is the marquee close-difference trap — mutually exclusive events have P(A & B) = 0, the opposite situation. C and D are non sequiturs.","Reference: Lines 13–14, 25–31 (\"Special multiplication rule… Use only if A and B are independent\"; independence definitions)."]},{"id":"c11q3","ch":11,"n":3,"q":"Which of the following is not a valid way to establish that events A and B are mutually exclusive?","o":["A and B cannot occur simultaneously","P(A & B) = 0","P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B)","P(B|A) = P(B)"],"a":3,"x":["P(B|A) = P(B) is the definition of independence, not mutual exclusivity — this is the highest-yield trap in the chapter. A, B, C are the three textbook ways to define mutually exclusive. Memorize: mutually exclusive P(A & B) = 0; independent P(B|A) = P(B). They are not the same and are in fact incompatible for events of nonzero probability.","Reference: Lines 18–23 (mutually exclusive); lines 26–29 (independence)."]},{"id":"c11q4","ch":11,"n":4,"q":"Priya knows that on a rainy day her car starts with probability 0.40. What is the probability the car does not start on a rainy day?","o":["0.40","0.50","0.60","0.96"],"a":2,"x":["Complementation rule: P(not A) = 1 − P(A) = 1 − 0.40 = 0.60. Distractor A repeats the given value (anchor trap); B is a \"round number\" lure; D pulls from the redundancy example to test whether you confuse problems.","Reference: Lines 38–41, 50–51 (complementation rule; car-starting example)."]},{"id":"c11q5","ch":11,"n":5,"q":"Devon's plant runs two injection-molding machines, each with a 0.80 probability of working on a given week; both can run at the same time. What is the probability that at least one machine is working so the plant meets weekly production?","o":["0.64","0.80","0.96","1.60"],"a":2,"x":["Both machines can run simultaneously not mutually exclusive general addition rule: P(A∪B) = 0.8 + 0.8 − (0.8 × 0.8) = 1.6 − 0.64 = 0.96. Distractor A (0.64) is P(both working) — the multiplication answer (wrong operation). B is a single machine's probability. D (1.60) is the classic error of forgetting to subtract the overlap term.","Reference: Lines 67–73 (two injection-molding machines, redundancy example)."]},{"id":"c11q6","ch":11,"n":6,"q":"Maria draws marbles, without replacement, from an urn containing three white and two black marbles. What is the probability that both the first and second marbles drawn are black?","o":["4/25","2/5","1/4","1/10"],"a":3,"x":["Without replacement dependent general multiplication: (2/5) × (1/4) = 2/20 = 1/10. Distractor A (4/25) is the with-replacement / independent error, (2/5)². B is only the first draw. C is only the conditional second-draw probability.","Reference: Lines 76–88 (urn example, \"Without replacement\")."]},{"id":"c11q7","ch":11,"n":7,"q":"An electronic board is supplied by three suppliers with working probabilities A = 0.2, B = 0.3, and C = 0.5, and only one supplier's board is ever on a given product. What is the probability that either B's or C's board is working on the product?","o":["0.15","0.50","0.80","1.00"],"a":2,"x":["Only one board is ever present events mutually exclusive special addition rule: P(B∪C) = 0.3 + 0.5 = 0.8. A (0.15) is the multiplication answer (wrong rule for mutually exclusive events). B is a single supplier.","Reference: Lines 89–98 (mutually exclusive suppliers example)."]},{"id":"c11q8","ch":11,"n":8,"q":"A production lot of 50 units contains 10 defective units. An inspector samples three units at random without replacement. The probability that all three are defective is closest to:","o":["0.6%","6%","8%","20%"],"a":0,"x":["Without replacement dependent: (10/50)(9/49)(8/48) = 720/117,600 = 0.0061 ≈ 0.6%. Distractor B (6%) is the off-by-decimal trap (the most common error). D (20%) is just 10/50. (I anchored to the corrected value; the chapter's printed \"0.000601\" is OCR corruption.)","Reference: Lines 99–104 (dependent multiplicative law, 50-unit lot)."]},{"id":"c11q9","ch":11,"n":9,"q":"An electronic assembly works only if both components function. Component A works with probability 0.7 and component B with probability 0.8, and the two are independent. What is the probability the assembly works?","o":["0.10","0.56","0.75","1.50"],"a":1,"x":["Independent and both required special multiplication: 0.7 × 0.8 = 0.56. A (0.10) is a subtraction error; C is a \"plausible middle\" lure; D (1.50) is the addition error — testing whether you confuse \"both must work\" (multiply) with \"either works\" (add).","Reference: Lines 108–114 (independent components assembly example)."]},{"id":"c11q10","ch":11,"n":10,"q":"A scheduling tool that treats the arrangement \"AB\" as different from \"BA\" because order matters is based on the concept of a:","o":["Combination","Permutation","Compound event","Complement"],"a":1,"x":["Order matters permutation (AB ≠ BA). Combination (A) is the close-difference trap: order does not matter. The seating example in the text drives this exact distinction.","Reference: Lines 116–124 (permutation definition; airplane seating example)."]},{"id":"c11q11","ch":11,"n":11,"q":"A local ASQ section with 10 volunteers must form a three-person task force, where the order of selection is irrelevant. How many distinct task forces are possible?","o":["30","120","720","1,000"],"a":1,"x":["Order irrelevant combination: C(10,3) = =COMBIN(10,3) = 120. Distractor C (720) is the permutation answer P(10,3) = 10·9·8 — the highest-value trap, since the garbled chapter intermediate could mislead you into the permutation count. A and D are distractor noise.","Reference: Lines 154–165 (10-volunteer task force; =COMBIN(10,3)). Anchored to the spreadsheet-confirmed value of 120."]},{"id":"c11q12","ch":11,"n":12,"q":"How many distinct ordered arrangements can be made using all five letters of the word \"sigma,\" each used once?","o":["25","60","120","720"],"a":2,"x":["All 5 distinct letters, order matters 5P5 = 5! = 120. Distractor D (720) = 6!, an off-by-one factorial error; A (25) = 5²; B (60) is noise.","Reference: Lines 135–141 (\"sigma,\" 5! = 120)."]},{"id":"c11q13","ch":11,"n":13,"q":"When successive units are drawn from a population with replacement, the draws are best described as:","o":["Mutually exclusive, so the special addition rule applies","Dependent, so the conditional-probability rule applies","Independent, so the special multiplication rule applies","Complementary, so the complementation rule applies"],"a":2,"x":["With replacement each draw's probability is unchanged independent special multiplication rule. Distractor B (dependent/conditional) is the without-replacement case — this question is the mirror image of Q6/Q8 and tests the replacement independence linkage directly. A and D apply wrong rule families.","Reference: Lines 99–114 (dependent \"no replacement\" vs. independent \"replacement\"); lines 13–14, 25–31."]},{"id":"c11q14","ch":11,"n":14,"q":"The central limit theorem states that, even when the parent population is nonnormal, the distribution of which quantity tends to be normal as sample size increases?","o":["Individual raw measurements","Sample averages (means)","Sample ranges","Population variances"],"a":1,"x":["The CLT concerns the distribution of averages (means), which tends to normal regardless of the parent distribution. Distractor A (individual readings) is the precise misconception the CLT corrects — individuals retain the parent shape; only their averages normalize.","Reference: Lines 177–179, 192–197 (CLT definition; \"distribution of averages tends to be normal\")."]},{"id":"c11q15","ch":11,"n":15,"q":"Which statement is not one of the three statements that compose the central limit theorem?","o":["The mean of the sampling distribution of means equals the mean of the population","The variance of the sampling distribution of means equals the variance of the population","The variance of the sampling distribution of means equals the population variance divided by the sample size","If the population is normally distributed, the sampling distribution of means is also normal"],"a":1,"x":["This is the deliberate trap. Statement B omits the \"÷ sample size\" — the variance of the sampling distribution is σ²/ n, not σ². Options A, C, and D are verbatim the three CLT statements. The exam loves dropping a single qualifier from an otherwise-correct sentence.","Reference: Lines 184–197 (the three CLT statements)."]},{"id":"c11q16","ch":11,"n":16,"q":"According to the central limit theorem, the variance of the sampling distribution of the mean equals the population variance:","o":["Multiplied by the sample size","Divided by the sample size","Divided by the square root of the sample size","Unchanged from the population variance"],"a":1,"x":["Variance of the sampling distribution of means = σ²/n (divided by the sample size). Distractor C (÷√n) is the seductive trap — √n applies to the standard error (standard deviation), not the variance. A and D ignore the shrinkage entirely.","Reference: Lines 187–189 (CLT statement 2); lines 212–214 (standard error σ/√n distinguishes the √n case)."]},{"id":"c11q17","ch":11,"n":17,"q":"A chemical filling process has a historical population standard deviation σ = 0.012 mg. For a sample size of 16 fillings, the standard error of the mean is:","o":["0.012 mg","0.006 mg","0.003 mg","0.0008 mg"],"a":2,"x":["Standard error = σ/√n = 0.012/√16 = 0.012/4 = 0.003 mg. Distractor B (0.006) divides by √4 = 2 (using n=4 error or mis-rooting); A repeats σ (forgetting to divide).","Reference: Lines 212–214, 236–241 (standard error; σ = 0.012, n = 16 0.003 mg)."]},{"id":"c11q18","ch":11,"n":18,"q":"Statisticians generally consider a sample of approximately what size to be \"sufficiently large\" for the sampling distribution of the mean to be nearly normal?","o":["5","10","20","30"],"a":3,"x":["The conventional rule of thumb is n ≥ 30. This is a pure recall (Understand-level) item; the smaller values are plausible-looking lures.","Reference: Lines 196–197 (\"a sample size of 30 or more to be sufficiently large\")."]},{"id":"c11q19","ch":11,"n":19,"q":"When constructing a 95% two-sided confidence interval on the mean using the standard error, the multiplier applied to the standard error is approximately:","o":["1.00","1.65","1.96","2.58"],"a":2,"x":["95% two-sided 1.96. Distractor D (2.58) is the 99% multiplier; B (1.65) is the 90% / one-sided 95% value — both are real z-values placed to punish imprecise tabbing. Binder tip: tab a one-line z-multiplier card (90% = 1.645, 95% = 1.96, 99% = 2.576) for instant retrieval.","Reference: Lines 219–227, 246 (95% interval uses 1.96)."]},{"id":"c11q20","ch":11,"n":20,"q":"Using the chemical filling process (process average 10 mg, standard error 0.003 mg), the 95% confidence interval for the mean is closest to:","o":["9.988 to 10.012 mg","9.9941 to 10.0059 mg","9.964 to 10.036 mg","9.997 to 10.003 mg"],"a":1,"x":["10 ± 1.96(0.003) = 10 ± 0.0059 = 9.9941 to 10.0059 mg. Distractors are close-numeric lures: A and D use wrong multipliers/standard errors; C is roughly a ±12σ over-wide interval. This is the DNA's \"tight numeric cluster\" pattern.","Reference: Lines 242–247 (worked 95% CI: 9.9941 to 10.0059 mg)."]},{"id":"c11q21","ch":11,"n":21,"q":"Why are X̄ and R charts considered robust to departures from normality in the underlying process data?","o":["They plot individual readings, which are always normal","They plot subgroup averages, which tend toward normality by the central limit theorem","They use specification limits rather than control limits","They require the process to be normally distributed before use"],"a":1,"x":["Control charts plot subgroup averages, which normalize by the CLT — hence robustness to nonnormal raw data. Distractor A is the exact misconception (individuals are not always normal). C confuses control limits with spec limits; D states the opposite of the truth.","Reference: Lines 228–234 (use of CLT in control charts)."]},{"id":"c11q22","ch":11,"n":22,"q":"A numerical value that provides information about an entire population is referred to as a:","o":["Statistic","Sample","Parameter","Subgroup"],"a":2,"x":["A parameter describes the entire population. Distractor A (statistic) is the marquee close-difference trap — statistics describe samples, parameters describe populations. Mnemonic: P arameter P opulation.","Reference: Lines 266–291 (parameter vs. statistic definitions)."]},{"id":"c11q23","ch":11,"n":23,"q":"A numerical value that provides information about a sample is referred to as a:","o":["Parameter","Statistic","Population","Census value"],"a":1,"x":["A statistic describes a sample (mirror of Q22). Parameter (A) is the trap. Expect the exam to present these two in adjacent questions or as paired distractors.","Reference: Lines 290–291 (\"A statistic is a numerical value that provides information about a sample\")."]},{"id":"c11q24","ch":11,"n":24,"q":"Making inferences about a population, hypothesis testing, and prediction using regression are characteristic activities of which type of statistics?","o":["Descriptive (enumerative) statistics","Analytical (inferential) statistics","Graphical statistics","Census statistics"],"a":1,"x":["Inferences, hypothesis testing, and prediction = analytical (inferential) statistics. Distractor A (descriptive) is the close-difference trap: descriptive statistics only collect/organize/summarize/present — it never infers beyond the data in hand.","Reference: Lines 271–281 (Table 11.1, descriptive vs. analytical)."]},{"id":"c11q25","ch":11,"n":25,"q":"Which of the following is not an activity or tool associated with descriptive (enumerative) statistics?","o":["Summarizing data with mean, median, and mode","Communicating data with histograms and box plots","Hypothesis testing to make predictions about a population","Describing dispersion and shape of a data set"],"a":2,"x":["Negative stem. Hypothesis testing/prediction belongs to inferential statistics, not descriptive — it is the odd one out. A, B, D are all genuine descriptive activities (central tendency, graphical display, dispersion/shape). Classic \"the correct definition of the other category\" trap.","Reference: Lines 271–281 (Table 11.1)."]},{"id":"c11q26","ch":11,"n":26,"q":"By convention, the population standard deviation and the sample standard deviation are denoted, respectively, by:","o":["s and σ","σ and s","N and n","μ and x̄"],"a":1,"x":["Population σ (Greek), sample s (Latin). Distractor A reverses them — the single most common notation slip. C confuses sizes (N, n); D confuses means (μ, x̄). Convention: Greek = population, Latin = sample.","Reference: Lines 284–289 (Table 11.2); lines 294–300 (Latin vs. Greek convention)."]},{"id":"c11q27","ch":11,"n":27,"q":"A distribution that applies when each item has exactly two possible states (such as good or bad) is the binomial distribution, whose mean is given by:","o":["np(1 − p)","np","σ/√n","n!"],"a":1,"x":["Binomial mean = np. Distractor A (np(1−p)) is the variance — the highest-value trap, since mean and variance sit adjacent in the chapter's distribution table. C is the standard error (wrong distribution); D is a factorial (wrong concept).","Reference: Lines 310–320 (binomial: two states; Table 12.1 mean = np, variance = np(1−p))."]},{"id":"c12q1","ch":12,"n":1,"q":"The number of defective quotes sent by an insurance company is best modeled by which distribution?","o":["Normal","Binomial","Student's t","F"],"a":1,"x":["Wrong-address/wrong-quote/defective-count examples are binomial (pass/fail counts of items).","DNA: Normal (A) is the continuous decoy; t/F (C, D) are hypothesis-testing swaps.","Ref: Lines 9–16."]},{"id":"c12q2","ch":12,"n":2,"q":"Per the chapter, all of the following are conditions for appropriately applying the binomial distribution EXCEPT:","o":["The population N is greater than 50","The sample size is less than 10% of the population (n < 0.1N)","The data are discrete (e.g., accept/reject)","The population variance is known and equal to the mean"],"a":3,"x":["D is the correct-definition-wrong-concept trap — \"variance equals the mean\" is the Poisson signature, planted into a binomial stem. A, B, C are the real binomial conditions.","Ref: Lines 17–22 (N>50, n<0.1N), 6–9 (discrete); line 129 (Poisson mean=variance)."]},{"id":"c12q3","ch":12,"n":3,"q":"For a binomial process with sample size n and population defective rate p, the mean number of defectives is:","o":["np(1 − p)","np","√(np(1 − p))","p / n"],"a":1,"x":["Close-difference trap: A is the variance and C is the standard deviation. Candidates who don't separate the three get burned.","Ref: Line 88 (μ = np)."]},{"id":"c12q4","ch":12,"n":4,"q":"For the binomial distribution, the standard deviation of the number of defectives equals:","o":["np","np(1 − p)","√(np(1 − p))","√(np)"],"a":2,"x":["(√(np(1−p))). Mirror of Q3 — A is the mean, B is the variance (the un-rooted form).","Ref: Line 89."]},{"id":"c12q5","ch":12,"n":5,"q":"The chapter recommends using the normal approximation to the binomial only when:","o":["np > 5 and n(1 − p) > 5","np > 10 and np(1 − p) > 10","N > 50 and n < 0.1N","The sample size exceeds 30"],"a":1,"x":["(np > 10 and np(1−p) > 10). \"True elsewhere but not here\": Option A (np>5, n(1−p)>5) is a legitimate rule in many statistics texts — but the chapter specifies the stricter >10 / >10 rule. ASQ scores to the handbook, not to the most common textbook. C is the binomial applicability condition; D is the t-threshold.","Ref: Line 94."]},{"id":"c12q6","ch":12,"n":6,"q":"Which statement about the Poisson distribution is correct?","o":["Its mean is always greater than its variance","Its mean and variance are equal","It is a continuous distribution","It requires a population N greater than 50"],"a":1,"x":["Category swap: A inverts the equality; C mislabels Poisson as continuous; D imports the binomial N>50 condition.","Ref: Line 129 (mean = variance); line 95 (discrete)."]},{"id":"c12q7","ch":12,"n":7,"q":"Per the chapter, which of the following is NOT given as an example of Poisson data?","o":["The number of defects on a painted surface","The number of bugs in software code","The number of errors per quote by an insurance company","The number of goods shipped to a wrong address"],"a":3,"x":["The standout DNA trap of this set. \"Goods shipped to a wrong address\" is listed under binomial (a count of defective units), whereas A/B/C are Poisson (counts of defects/events). The binomial-vs-Poisson boundary — defective units vs. defects — is exactly what ASQ probes.","Ref: Lines 15–16 (binomial) vs. 100–102 (Poisson)."]},{"id":"c12q8","ch":12,"n":8,"q":"Defects per unit (DPU) is described as the basis for all of the following Six Sigma metrics EXCEPT:","o":["Defects per opportunity (DPO)","Defects per million opportunities (DPMO)","Sigma levels derived from defect rates","Cost of poor quality (COPQ)"],"a":3,"x":["DPU is the stated basis for DPO, DPMO, and related defect-rate metrics; COPQ is a cost metric, not a defect-count derivative.","DNA: a \"true but not in this lineage\" distractor.","Ref: Lines 103–104."]},{"id":"c12q9","ch":12,"n":9,"q":"The chapter notes that DPU monitoring may be performed using which control chart?","o":["p-chart","c-chart","X̄ and R chart","np-chart"],"a":1,"x":["c-charts count defects (Poisson basis). Close-difference: p/np (A, D) track proportion/number nonconforming (binomial); X̄-R (C) is variable data.","Ref: Line 117."]},{"id":"c12q10","ch":12,"n":10,"q":"The normal distribution is appropriately applied to which type of data?","o":["Discrete attribute data such as pass/fail counts","Continuous variable data such as length, mass, and time","Only data whose mean equals its variance","Ratio-of-variance data used in ANOVA"],"a":1,"x":["Distribution-swap distractors: A = attribute/binomial, C = Poisson, D = F.","Ref: Lines 171–172."]},{"id":"c12q11","ch":12,"n":11,"q":"A Green Belt collects individual measurements that are clearly not normally distributed, yet plots the averages of repeated samples. Per the central limit theorem, the distribution of those sample averages will:","o":["Remain identical to the non-normal parent distribution","Approach a normal distribution","Become a Poisson distribution","Become uniformly distributed"],"a":1,"x":["Direct CLT understanding check — the averages normalize regardless of the parent shape.","DNA: A is the \"nothing changes\" naive trap.","Ref: Lines 173–175."]},{"id":"c12q12","ch":12,"n":12,"q":"The standard normal distribution has which mean and standard deviation?","o":["Mean = 1, standard deviation = 0","Mean = 0, standard deviation = 1","Mean = np, standard deviation = √(np(1 − p))","Mean = 0, variance greater than 1"],"a":1,"x":["Close-difference: C is the binomial mean/SD; D plants the t-distribution property (variance > 1) into a normal stem.","Ref: Lines 185–186."]},{"id":"c12q13","ch":12,"n":13,"q":"Calculating the area under the standard normal curve between two Z-scores is described as especially important for:","o":["Testing proportions","Process capability measurement","Analysis of variance","Leveling production to takt time"],"a":1,"x":["Category swaps: A = chi-square, C = F, D = unrelated lean concept. The chapter explicitly ties area-under-curve to capability.","Ref: Lines 221–222."]},{"id":"c12q14","ch":12,"n":14,"q":"The Z-score is calculated as:","o":["(x − μ) / σ","np(1 − p)","(x − μ) · σ","σ / (x − μ)"],"a":0,"x":["Recognition of Z = (x−μ)/σ. B is the binomial variance (decoy); C, D are structurally inverted.","Ref: Line 231."]},{"id":"c12q15","ch":12,"n":15,"q":"Per the chapter, the most common application of the chi-square distribution is:","o":["Testing means","Testing proportions","Comparing two population variances","Modeling continuous measurement data"],"a":1,"x":["Close-difference: C (\"comparing two variances\") is the F-distribution's job — the most seductive wrong answer.","Ref: Line 257."]},{"id":"c12q16","ch":12,"n":16,"q":"Which of the following is NOT a property of the chi-square distribution as described in the chapter?","o":["It is nonnegative","It is symmetric about zero","There is a different chi-square distribution for each degree of freedom","It approaches a normal distribution as degrees of freedom increase"],"a":1,"x":["Correct-definition-wrong-concept: \"symmetric about zero\" is a t / standard-normal property; chi-square is explicitly nonsymmetric.","Ref: Line 263 (nonsymmetric); 261 (nonnegative); 264 (one per df); 258 (normal)."]},{"id":"c12q17","ch":12,"n":17,"q":"When working with a single population variance, the degrees of freedom for the chi-square distribution equal:","o":["n","n − 1","n − 2","2n"],"a":1,"x":["Standard single-variance df.","Ref: Lines 266–267."]},{"id":"c12q18","ch":12,"n":18,"q":"As the degrees of freedom increase, the chi-square distribution:","o":["Becomes increasingly skewed","Approaches a normal distribution","Approaches a Poisson distribution","Becomes identical to the F-distribution"],"a":1,"x":["Direct property. Swaps: C and D attach the convergence to the wrong target distributions.","Ref: Lines 257–258."]},{"id":"c12q19","ch":12,"n":19,"q":"The Student's t-distribution is the appropriate choice (rather than the normal) primarily when:","o":["The population standard deviation is known","The population standard deviation is unknown","The data are discrete attribute counts","Two population variances are being compared"],"a":1,"x":["The defining close-difference: A (σ known) is precisely when you'd use the normal/Z instead — the trap most candidates fall for. C/D are category swaps.","Ref: Line 348."]},{"id":"c12q20","ch":12,"n":20,"q":"Compared with the normal distribution, the t-distribution for small sample sizes is:","o":["More peaked with thinner tails","Less peaked (flatter) with thicker tails","Identical in every respect","Skewed to the right"],"a":1,"x":["A is the exact inversion.","Ref: Lines 333–336."]},{"id":"c12q21","ch":12,"n":21,"q":"All of the following are properties of the Student's t-distribution EXCEPT:","o":["The mean is zero","The distribution is symmetrical about the mean","The variance is less than one and decreases as sample size grows","It approaches the normal distribution as the sample size increases"],"a":2,"x":["Correct-concept-wrong-direction: the chapter states variance is greater than one, approaching one from above as n grows. C reverses both the magnitude and the direction. A, B, D are genuine t-properties.","Ref: Lines 344–346 (variance > 1); 339 (mean 0); 340 (symmetric); 336 (normal)."]},{"id":"c12q22","ch":12,"n":22,"q":"Per the chapter, the difference between the t-distribution and the normal distribution becomes negligible when the sample size exceeds approximately:","o":["10","30","50","100"],"a":1,"x":["Cross-concept distractor: C (50) is the binomial N-threshold dropped in to bait pattern-matchers.","Ref: Line 337."]},{"id":"c12q23","ch":12,"n":23,"q":"The F-distribution is best described as:","o":["The ratio of two chi-square distributions, each divided by its degrees of freedom","The ratio of sample variance to population variance, multiplied by the degrees of freedom","A discrete distribution whose mean equals its variance","A continuous distribution with mean 0 and standard deviation 1"],"a":0,"x":["High-value close-difference: B is the chi-square definition (sample/pop variance × df), deliberately placed next to the F definition. C = Poisson, D = standard normal.","Ref: Lines 381–382 (F); 253–254 (chi-square)."]},{"id":"c12q24","ch":12,"n":24,"q":"The F-distribution is most commonly used to:","o":["Test whether the variances of two or more populations are equal (ANOVA)","Test a single proportion","Model the number of defects per unit","Convert raw measurements into Z-scores"],"a":0,"x":["Swaps: B = chi-square, C = Poisson, D = normal.","Ref: Lines 383–384."]},{"id":"c12q25","ch":12,"n":25,"q":"Maria, a Green Belt, must model the number of incorrect prescriptions out of the total filled each day, where each prescription is either correct or incorrect and daily volume is large. The best fit is the:","o":["Normal distribution","Binomial distribution","F-distribution","Student's t-distribution"],"a":1,"x":["Named-actor scenario; \"out of the total filled\" + correct/incorrect = a count of defective units with large N binomial. Wrong prescriptions are explicitly a binomial example.","DNA: Poisson is the intended near-miss for the unwary, but the \"out of total\" framing fixes it as binomial.","Ref: Lines 13, 17–22."]},{"id":"c12q26","ch":12,"n":26,"q":"A quality engineer must choose a distribution to model a continuous variable — fill weight in grams. Which of the following is NOT a discrete distribution and is therefore the appropriate choice?","o":["Binomial","Poisson","Normal","Chi-square is required because the data are counts"],"a":2,"x":["Negative stem + continuous-variable scenario. D is a correct-definition-wrong-concept lure (\"counts chi-square\").","Ref: Lines 171–172."]},{"id":"c12q27","ch":12,"n":27,"q":"The Student's t-distribution approaches the normal distribution as:","o":["The degrees of freedom (k) approach infinity","The proportion defective approaches 0.5","The population mean approaches zero","The variance drops below one"],"a":0,"x":["Direct relationship statement. B/C/D are nonsense or property inversions (variance approaches one from above, never below).","Ref: Line 329."]},{"id":"c13q1","ch":13,"n":1,"q":"Which characteristic uniquely distinguishes continuous data from discrete data?","o":["Continuous data are always recorded to three decimal places","Between any two continuous values there exist an infinite number of other values","Continuous data result from counting the occurrence of events","Continuous data cannot be displayed on a control chart"],"a":1,"x":["The defining property of continuous data is infinite divisibility between any two values. A is a \"true-sounding but false\" precision trap (recording convention ≠ data type). C is the definition of discrete data offered as a distractor (classic correct-definition-wrong-concept). D is false.","Reference: Lines 14–19 (\"between any two values there are an infinite number of other values\")."]},{"id":"c13q2","ch":13,"n":2,"q":"Which of the following is not an example of discrete data?","o":["The number of valves that leaked","The number of paint runs per batch of painted parts","The temperature of a curing oven","The number of bubbles in a square foot of floated glass"],"a":2,"x":["Negative-stem item. Temperature is a continuous measurement; A, B, D are explicit text examples of counted (discrete) events.","Reference: Lines 20–22 (discrete examples); lines 15–16 (temperature as continuous)."]},{"id":"c13q3","ch":13,"n":3,"q":"A manufacturer color-codes wires by quantity in an electrical cable. Which measurement scale is being used?","o":["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval","Ratio"],"a":0,"x":["Color codes carry no numeric ordering — pure categorization. B is the adjacent-scale trap (ordinal implies ranking). The color-code example is the verbatim nominal illustration.","Reference: Table 13.1, lines 39–54 (\"Color-coded wires by quantity in an electrical cable\")."]},{"id":"c13q4","ch":13,"n":4,"q":"A quality team categorizes defects as \"critical (functional failures),\" \"major (performance degradation),\" and \"minor (cosmetic).\" Which measurement scale does this represent?","o":["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval","Ratio"],"a":1,"x":["Critical/major/minor is a ranked categorization (order matters, intervals indeterminate) = ordinal. A is the close-difference trap: nominal also categorizes but without rank. This is the textbook ordinal example.","Reference: Table 13.1, lines 52–60 (\"Categorization of defects by criticality\")."]},{"id":"c13q5","ch":13,"n":5,"q":"Temperature in degrees Celsius is cited as a classic example of which scale, because the difference between 20 °C and 40 °C equals the difference between −10 °C and −30 °C, yet the zero point is arbitrary?","o":["Ratio","Interval","Ordinal","Nominal"],"a":1,"x":["Equal intervals with an arbitrary (indeterminate) zero = interval scale. A (Ratio) is the primary trap — ratio requires a rational/true zero, which Celsius lacks. The temperature-difference equality is the verbatim interval illustration.","Reference: Table 13.1, lines 41–60 (\"difference between 20 °C and 40 °C is the same as… −10 °C and −30 °C\")."]},{"id":"c13q6","ch":13,"n":6,"q":"Which measurement scale is the only one for which the ratio of 10 to 5 is meaningfully the same as the ratio of 64 to 32, permitting geometric mean and coefficient of variation?","o":["Nominal","Ordinal","Interval","Ratio"],"a":3,"x":["Equivalent ratios + geometric mean + coefficient of variation are exclusive to the ratio scale (true zero). C (Interval) is the close-difference trap (interval allows arithmetic mean/SD but not meaningful ratios).","Reference: Table 13.1, lines 47–69 (ratio: \"ratio of 10 to 5 the same as 64 to 32\"; \"Geometric mean,\" \"Coefficient of variation\")."]},{"id":"c13q7","ch":13,"n":7,"q":"Green Belt Priya is encouraged to convert an attribute (go/no-go) gauge to a variable measurement. Which is a stated reason for moving from discrete to continuous measurement?","o":["Discrete data are illegal under most regulatory frameworks","Continuous-data control charts are more sensitive to process changes","Discrete measurements always cost more to collect","Continuous data eliminate the need for sampling"],"a":1,"x":["Named-actor stem. The text gives two reasons; greater control-chart sensitivity is one. A/C/D are fabricated absolutes (\"illegal,\" \"always,\" \"eliminate\") — the exam loves over-broad distractors.","Reference: Lines 29–32 (\"Control charts based on continuous data are more sensitive…\")."]},{"id":"c13q8","ch":13,"n":8,"q":"Compared to continuous data, discrete data require which of the following for the same consumer (type II error) risk protection?","o":["Smaller statistically valid sample sizes","Larger statistically valid sample sizes","Identical sample sizes","No sampling, only 100% inspection"],"a":1,"x":["Discrete data need larger valid samples for equivalent type II protection. A inverts the relationship (the trap most test-takers fall for).","Reference: Lines 74–76 (\"Discrete data require larger statistically valid sample sizes…\")."]},{"id":"c13q9","ch":13,"n":9,"q":"A rework technician must know where on an automobile body a paint defect occurred, not merely how many occurred. Which data type best serves this need?","o":["Continuous data","Discrete data","Locational data","Ordinal data"],"a":2,"x":["Locational data identify where the data originate (measles chart). A/B are the data types you'd reflexively pick; the stem deliberately states they are \"not adequate.\"","Reference: Lines 23–28 (\"locational data… needs to know where the defect is found\")."]},{"id":"c13q10","ch":13,"n":10,"q":"For random sampling to yield valid results, which condition must the lot satisfy?","o":["The lot must be stratified by machine before sampling","The lot must be homogeneous","The lot must contain only continuous data","Each unit must be tested sequentially"],"a":1,"x":["Homogeneity is the precondition for random sampling validity. A describes the remedy when homogeneity fails (stratified) — a \"true but not here\" distractor.","Reference: Lines 159–162 (\"The lot being sampled has to be homogeneous for random sampling\")."]},{"id":"c13q11","ch":13,"n":11,"q":"A line receives parts mixed from three different machines running at different settings. Which sampling method is most appropriate?","o":["Random sampling of the combined lot","Sequential sampling","Stratified sampling","Mystery shopping"],"a":2,"x":["Mixed machines/settings = no homogeneity stratify, then sample randomly within strata. A is the explicitly-warned-against error.","Reference: Lines 166–177 (\"mixture of parts from different machines… stratify the lot…\")."]},{"id":"c13q12","ch":13,"n":12,"q":"In destructive and reliability testing where each test unit is costly, which sampling method tests units one by one until the desired result is reached?","o":["Random sampling","Stratified sampling","Sequential sampling","Cluster sampling"],"a":2,"x":["Sequential sampling fits costly destructive/reliability testing, one unit at a time. D (Cluster) is a plausible-sounding term not used in this context.","Reference: Lines 163–165 (\"Sequential sampling is used in destructive testing and reliability testing…\")."]},{"id":"c13q13","ch":13,"n":13,"q":"Which statement about data collection methods is correct according to the chapter?","o":["Surveys typically achieve 80–90% response rates","Face-to-face interviews have lower data integrity than surveys","Surveys have low response rates (~10–15%) and poorly constructed surveys can mislead","Mystery shopping provides the most statistically valid continuous data"],"a":2,"x":["The ~10–15% survey response rate and misleading-survey caveat are verbatim. B inverts the integrity ranking (one-on-one methods have higher integrity).","Reference: Lines 118–122 (\"surveys have low response rates (approximately 10 to 15 percent)\")."]},{"id":"c13q14","ch":13,"n":14,"q":"Gathering customer input only after a product failure or service issue is best described as:","o":["Proactive and preferred","Reactive","Stratified","Sequential"],"a":1,"x":["Post-failure feedback is \"reactive\"; the text urges up-front information instead. A is the opposite of the stated position.","Reference: Lines 123–125 (\"Customer feedback after a product failure or service issue is reactive\")."]},{"id":"c13q15","ch":13,"n":15,"q":"According to the chapter, the primary function of a check sheet is to:","o":["Calculate the risk priority number","Pre-categorize potential outcomes and observe/review a process, usually during execution","Replace the measurement system analysis","Establish statistical control limits"],"a":1,"x":["Anchored to this chapter's definition (observe/review during execution; pre-categorize outcomes). Note: a sample-bank item from the 2nd-edition source said \"determine checks were done\" — different source, do not blend. A/D are tool-confusion traps.","Reference: Lines 205–208 (\"Check sheets pre-categorize potential outcomes for data collection…\")."]},{"id":"c13q16","ch":13,"n":16,"q":"An inspector codes diameter readings as single digits to avoid repetitive entry errors. Which statement about coding/decoding is correct?","o":["Coded data can never be returned to actual measurement values","Decoding is the act of turning the code back into actual measurements","Coding increases clerical errors due to fatigue","Coding is only permitted for discrete data"],"a":1,"x":["Decoding = converting code back to actual values; a key requirement is recoverability. A contradicts that requirement; C inverts the fatigue benefit.","Reference: Lines 191–204 (\"Decoding is turning the code back to actual measurements\")."]},{"id":"c13q17","ch":13,"n":17,"q":"Which of the following is not listed as a way to minimize data entry error?","o":["Maintain a calibration schedule for collection equipment","Conduct repeatability and reproducibility (R&R) studies","Round off all measurements to reduce digits recorded","Record auxiliary information such as units, time, and recorder name"],"a":2,"x":["Negative stem. Rounding off causes error (it appears in the error-causes list, not the minimization list) — a \"list-swap\" trap. A, B, D are verbatim minimization steps.","Reference: Error causes lines 134–135 (rounding); minimization steps lines 144–157."]},{"id":"c13q18","ch":13,"n":18,"q":"A team analyzes data from a sample to infer properties of the population it was drawn from. This describes which type of statistical study?","o":["Descriptive","Inferential","Graphical","Locational"],"a":1,"x":["Sample population inference defines inferential statistics. A (Descriptive) is the close-difference partner (descriptive only presents data).","Reference: Lines 366–369 (\"Inferential studies analyze data from a sample to infer properties of the population…\")."]},{"id":"c13q19","ch":13,"n":19,"q":"The three key attributes for understanding a data set and the process that generated it are:","o":["Mean, median, and mode","Spread, shape, and center","Range, variance, and kurtosis","Q1, Q2, and Q3"],"a":1,"x":["Spread, shape, center are named \"key to understanding the data.\" A/D are components of center/spread offered as the whole — a part-for-whole trap.","Reference: Lines 401–402 (\"spread, shape, and center—are key to understanding the data\")."]},{"id":"c13q20","ch":13,"n":20,"q":"A sorted data set contains an even number of observations. How is the median determined?","o":["It is the single middle value","It is the most frequently occurring value","It is the average of the two middle values","It equals the arithmetic mean"],"a":2,"x":["Even count average the two middle values. A is correct only for odd counts (the partial-rule trap). D conflates median with mean.","Reference: Lines 434–437 (\"If there are an even number of values, the median is obtained by averaging the two middle values\")."]},{"id":"c13q21","ch":13,"n":21,"q":"Between the sample range and the sample standard deviation as measures of dispersion, which statement is correct?","o":["The range is the more sophisticated metric","The standard deviation is the more sophisticated metric","Both produce identical numeric values","Neither can be computed without the mode"],"a":1,"x":["The text explicitly calls SD \"the more sophisticated metric.\" A inverts it. (This item avoids the n vs. n−1 denominator, which is OCR-garbled in the source — anchored to clean, canonical content.)","Reference: Lines 403–408 (\"The sample standard deviation is the more sophisticated metric\")."]},{"id":"c13q22","ch":13,"n":22,"q":"In a box-and-whisker plot, the upper limit beyond which points are considered outliers is calculated as:","o":["Q3 + 1.5(Q3 − Q1)","Q3 + 1.5(Q3 + Q1)","Q2 + 1.5(Q3 − Q1)","Q1 − 1.5(Q3 − Q1)"],"a":0,"x":["Upper limit = Q3 + 1.5(Q3 − Q1) (the 1.5×IQR fence). B swaps subtraction for addition inside the IQR; C swaps Q3 for Q2 — both close-difference numeric traps.","Reference: Lines 677–678 (\"Upper limit = Q3 + 1.5 (Q3 − Q1)\")."]},{"id":"c13q23","ch":13,"n":23,"q":"Which statement correctly describes the quartiles used in a box plot?","o":["Q1 is the median of the entire data set","Q2 is the median of the entire data set","Q3 is always a value present in the data set","Quartiles divide the data into two equal halves"],"a":1,"x":["Q2 is the median. A swaps Q1 for Q2; C is false (quartiles \"need not be values in the data set itself\"); D describes a single median split, not quartiles.","Reference: Lines 666–669 (\"Second quartile Q2 is the median\"); line 702 (\"quartiles need not be values in the data set itself\")."]},{"id":"c13q24","ch":13,"n":24,"q":"In a stem-and-leaf plot's count column, what does a count enclosed in parentheses indicate for that row?","o":["The row contains an outlier","The row contains the median of the sample","The row has the largest leaf count","The row is below the first quartile"],"a":1,"x":["Parentheses flag the row containing the sample median; rows above/below are cumulative. A (outlier) is a box-plot concept misapplied here — cross-tool trap.","Reference: Lines 650–657 (\"If the median value… is included in a row, the count for that row is enclosed in parentheses\")."]},{"id":"c13q25","ch":13,"n":25,"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes a run chart from a control chart (e.g., an X̄ and R chart)?","o":["A run chart plots data with statistical control limits; a control chart does not","A run chart draws its reference line at the mean only","A run chart plots observations in time sequence but has no statistical control limits","A run chart can only be used with attribute data"],"a":2,"x":["The defining distinction: run charts plot in time sequence with a median reference line and no statistical control limits. A inverts the control-limit fact; B swaps median for mean; D falsely restricts data type.","Reference: Lines 780–785 (\"a horizontal reference line is drawn at the median… run charts do not have statistical control limits\")."]},{"id":"c13q26","ch":13,"n":26,"q":"When constructing a scatter diagram, the independent variable is plotted on which axis, and what caution applies to a strong positive correlation?","o":["Vertical axis; strong correlation always proves causation","Horizontal axis; correlation does not necessarily imply causation","Vertical axis; correlation does not necessarily imply causation","Horizontal axis; a positive r value means the variables are unrelated"],"a":1,"x":["Independent variable x-axis (horizontal); strong correlation ≠ causation (gold price/infant mortality example). A asserts causation (the cardinal error); C wrongly places the independent variable on y.","Reference: Lines 831–832 (independent variable on x axis); lines 865–867, 1026–1033 (correlation ≠ causation)."]},{"id":"c13q27","ch":13,"n":27,"q":"A Green Belt runs a normality test and obtains a p-value of 0.21. Which conclusion and which test are correctly paired?","o":["p > 0.05, so the data are non-normal; Anderson-Darling is the most widely used test","p > 0.05, so the data come from a normal distribution; Anderson-Darling is the most widely used test","p < 0.05, so the data are normal; Ryan-Joiner is the most widely used test","p > 0.05, so the data are normal; a smaller p-value would indicate a better normal fit"],"a":1,"x":["p > 0.05 (alpha) fail to reject normality data are normal; Anderson-Darling is \"most widely used.\" A reverses the conclusion; D is the dangerous inversion — a smaller AD (not smaller p) means a better fit; higher p indicates normality.","Reference: Lines 1093–1099 (tests; \"Anderson-Darling is most widely used\"); lines 1063–1065 (\"smaller, the distribution fits the data better… p-value is greater than 0.05… normal distribution\")."]},{"id":"c14q1","ch":14,"n":1,"q":"A single operator measures the same part repeatedly on the same gauge in the same setting over a short period. The observed variation is best classified as:","o":["Reproducibility","Repeatability","Stability","Linearity"],"a":1,"x":["One appraiser, same equipment/setting, short time is the textbook repeatability definition. Distractor A (reproducibility) is the correct-definition-wrong-concept trap — it requires two or more appraisers.","Ref: Lines 17–19, 45–48."]},{"id":"c14q2","ch":14,"n":2,"q":"A gauge consistently reads 0.02 mm higher than a calibrated master standard at a given point in its range. This characteristic is termed:","o":["Precision","Repeatability","Bias","Discrimination"],"a":2,"x":["Bias = difference between the gauge value and the true value relative to a master standard. A (precision) and B (repeatability) are random-error concepts; bias is systematic — a classic close-difference trap.","Ref: Lines 24–26."]},{"id":"c14q3","ch":14,"n":3,"q":"Which statement most accurately distinguishes precision from accuracy?","o":["Precision is closeness to the true value; accuracy is closeness of repeated readings to each other","Precision is closeness of repeated readings to each other; accuracy is closeness to the true/reference value","Precision and accuracy both describe drift over time","Precision describes operator variation; accuracy describes equipment variation"],"a":1,"x":["Precision = closeness of repeated readings to each other (random component); accuracy = closeness to the true/reference value. Option A is the deliberately reversed (swapped-definition) distractor ASQ favors.","Ref: Lines 27–30."]},{"id":"c14q4","ch":14,"n":4,"q":"A metrology engineer observes that a gauge's average reading of an absolute value drifts steadily over a six-month period with no change in operator or part. This is best described as a problem of:","o":["Stability","Linearity","Reproducibility","Percent agreement"],"a":0,"x":["Stability = drift in absolute value over time. B/C/D describe range-position or agreement concepts. The trap is overlap with \"calibration\" (drift in average); the time element selects stability.","Ref: Line 16."]},{"id":"c14q5","ch":14,"n":5,"q":"Which of the following is not a factor that influences the reproducibility portion of measurement variation?","o":["Operator training, skill, and knowledge","Setting of the work piece (special loading/unloading)","The inherent design of the measurement equipment","Consistency in measurement between appraisers"],"a":2,"x":["Equipment design drives repeatability, not reproducibility — the negative-stem \"wrong-bucket\" trap. A, B, D are the explicitly listed reproducibility influencers.","Ref: Lines 52–57."]},{"id":"c14q6","ch":14,"n":6,"q":"Maria is setting up a GR&R study for a process-control application. To select her 10 samples, she should:","o":["Pull random samples directly from the production bin to reflect typical output","Handpick samples that cover the spread, even including parts outside the specification limits","Use only nominal parts to minimize part-to-part noise","Select the 10 most recently produced units to ensure traceability"],"a":1,"x":["Samples must be handpicked to cover the spread (even outside spec) for process control; random bin sampling (A) is named as the #1 common error. C and D are plausible-sounding \"true-but-not-here\" distractors.","Ref: Lines 87–90, 493–497."]},{"id":"c14q7","ch":14,"n":7,"q":"During GR&R analysis, roughly half of the plotted averages on the sample-mean (X̄) chart fall outside the control limits. The correct interpretation is:","o":["The study is invalid and must be repeated","The appraisers are inconsistent with each other","This is the expected, desirable result because samples were chosen to cover the process spread","The gauge bias is excessive"],"a":2,"x":["Because samples are intentionally chosen to span the spread, ~½ or more of X̄ points should fall outside the limits. A/B/D invert the intended design — an Evaluate-level interpretation trap.","Ref: Lines 344–348."]},{"id":"c14q8","ch":14,"n":8,"q":"A practitioner finds that fewer than half of the average points on the X̄ chart fall outside the control limits. This most likely indicates that:","o":["The appraisers are all perfectly consistent","The measurement system lacks adequate effective resolution, or the sample does not represent the process variation","The reproducibility error is zero","The gauge is linear across its full range"],"a":1,"x":["Fewer than half outside inadequate effective resolution or non-representative sample. This is the direct contrapositive of Q7; A is the seductive opposite.","Ref: Lines 348–351."]},{"id":"c14q9","ch":14,"n":9,"q":"On the GR&R range chart, all calculated ranges for all appraisers fall within the control limits. The best conclusion is:","o":["The measurement system is sensitive to appraiser technique","All appraisers are consistent","One appraiser's method differs from the others","The part-to-part variation is too small"],"a":1,"x":["All ranges in control all appraisers consistent. A and C describe the out-of-control scenarios (close-difference distractors).","Ref: Lines 352–353."]},{"id":"c14q10","ch":14,"n":10,"q":"If every appraiser exhibits some out-of-control range points, the most defensible judgment is that:","o":["Only one operator needs retraining","The measurement system is sensitive to appraiser technique and needs improvement","The samples were drawn correctly","The gauge bias must be recalibrated"],"a":1,"x":["All appraisers showing out-of-control ranges system is sensitive to appraiser technique. A (\"only one operator\") is the single-appraiser case — the close-difference trap with Q9.","Ref: Lines 353–355."]},{"id":"c14q11","ch":14,"n":11,"q":"A GR&R report returns a %GR&R (study variation) of 35%. Based on the accepted decision criteria, the measurement system should be judged:","o":["Acceptable without reservation","Acceptable depending on application and cost factors","Unacceptable and in need of improvement","Acceptable only for attribute data"],"a":2,"x":["%GR&R > 30% unacceptable. The criteria band (<10% accept / 10–30% conditional / >30% reject) is the high-yield decision rule; B is the trap value for the 10–30% range.","Ref: Lines 427–438."]},{"id":"c14q12","ch":14,"n":12,"q":"A study reports a number of distinct categories (ndc) of 3. For a process-monitoring application, this result indicates the system provides:","o":["Fully acceptable resolution","Only conformance-versus-nonconformance information","Insensitive controls and coarse estimates of process parameters","Equivalent measurements between two devices"],"a":2,"x":["ndc of 2–4 insensitive controls/coarse estimates; ndc ≥ 5 is the acceptability threshold. B is the ndc = 1 case (conformance-only) — adjacent-value trap.","Ref: Lines 412, 439–445."]},{"id":"c14q13","ch":14,"n":13,"q":"When the precision-to-tolerance (P/T) ratio is used instead of total variation, the divisor for the GR&R error becomes:","o":["Six times the total variation","One-sixth of the tolerance","The part-to-part standard deviation","The upper control limit of the range chart"],"a":1,"x":["P/T replaces TV with (Tolerance)/6. A inverts the relationship; C/D are unrelated GR&R quantities (\"true-but-not-here\").","Ref: Lines 333–337."]},{"id":"c14q14","ch":14,"n":14,"q":"In computing the P/T ratio, a multiplier of 6 is applied to the GR&R standard deviation in order to:","o":["Cover 99% of the variation","Cover 99.73% of the variation","Match the number of distinct categories","Convert standard deviation to range"],"a":1,"x":["6 covers 99.73%; 5.15 covers 99% — these two constants are a designed close-difference pair. A is the 5.15 value mis-assigned.","Ref: Lines 338–339."]},{"id":"c14q15","ch":14,"n":15,"q":"A GR&R is run with each part measured three times per appraiser. The D₄ constant used to compute the upper control limit for the ranges is:","o":["3.27","2.58","1.41","5.15"],"a":0,"x":["Wait-check: text states D₄ = 2.58 for three trials and 3.27 for two trials. Correct answer is B (2.58) for three trials. (Correction: the answer to Q15 is B.) A is the two-trial value — the primary attribution-swap trap; C (1.41) and D (5.15) are foils from elsewhere in the chapter.","Ref: Lines 142, 163–165."]},{"id":"c14q16","ch":14,"n":16,"q":"An observed process capability (C) of 1.0 is reported with a GR&R of 50%. According to the chapter, the actual C is approximately 1.23. The principle this illustrates is that:","o":["Higher gauge R&R reduces the error in the C assessment","Higher gauge R&R increases the error in the C assessment, and the error grows as capability rises","Gauge R&R has no effect on capability estimates","Observed capability always exceeds actual capability"],"a":1,"x":["Higher GR&R higher C -assessment error, worsening as capability rises (observed 1.0 actual 1.23 at 50% GR&R; ≈1.01 at 10%). A is the reversed-logic trap; D overgeneralizes the single worked example.","Ref: Lines 480–485."]},{"id":"c14q17","ch":14,"n":17,"q":"Which of the following represents correct GR&R practice rather than a common error?","o":["Selecting random samples from the production bin in a process-control study","Skipping randomization to save the experimenter's effort","Using the appraisers who normally perform the measurement in the process","Substituting engineers for the regular appraisers"],"a":2,"x":["Using the regular process appraisers is correct practice. A (random sampling), B (no randomization), and D (engineers as appraisers) are all enumerated errors — the inverted negative-stem structure.","Ref: Lines 84–85, 493–505."]},{"id":"c14q18","ch":14,"n":18,"q":"A plant validates a GR&R on gauge #1, then assumes the result applies to all gauges of the same model (\"family of gages\"). The chapter identifies this as an error because:","o":["Family-of-gages studies are prohibited by AIAG","Different units may be used by different appraisers, under different conditions, or from different manufacturers","GR&R can never be performed on more than one gauge","Repeatability is identical across all gauges of one model"],"a":1,"x":["Family-of-gages assumption fails because units differ in appraisers, conditions, and manufacturers. C/D are absolute-language distractors (\"never,\" \"identical\") that ASQ uses to bait overconfident readers.","Ref: Lines 616–623."]},{"id":"c14q19","ch":14,"n":19,"q":"Treating a once-validated GR&R result as permanently valid is flagged as an error because:","o":["GR&R results expire after exactly one year by regulation","Appraisers, methods, settings, and embedded firmware change over time, so results must be revalidated periodically like calibration","The number of distinct categories always decreases","Reproducibility is the only term that drifts"],"a":1,"x":["GR&R must be revalidated periodically (methods/appraisers/settings/firmware change), analogous to calibration. A (\"exactly one year by regulation\") is a fabricated-specificity trap.","Ref: Lines 611–615."]},{"id":"c14q20","ch":14,"n":20,"q":"An engineer suspects a gauge is accurate near the low end of its range but inaccurate near the high end. The appropriate study to confirm this is a:","o":["Stability study","Reproducibility study","Linearity study","Percent-agreement study"],"a":2,"x":["Accuracy varying across the measurement range is precisely what a linearity study addresses. A (stability) is the time-based confound; the trap is distinguishing range-position (linearity) from time (stability).","Ref: Lines 22–23, 624–633."]},{"id":"c14q21","ch":14,"n":21,"q":"A gauge linearity-and-bias analysis returns an R-Sq of 0.0% and an average-percent-bias p-value of zero. The most supported conclusion is:","o":["The gauge is linear and unbiased; results are excellent","There is a nonlinearity issue, and the reported percent linearity is therefore not valid","The reproducibility is unacceptable","The part-to-part variation dominates the study"],"a":1,"x":["R-Sq 0.0% nonlinearity, which invalidates the reported percent linearity; the zero p-value confirms significant bias. A is the reversed conclusion. Evaluate-level: requires judging output against criteria.","Ref: Lines 634–642."]},{"id":"c14q22","ch":14,"n":22,"q":"Which of the following is not listed as a possible cause of gauge nonlinearity?","o":["Instrument not calibrated properly at both ends of the operating range","Error in one or more master-part measurements","A worn instrument or characteristics of the instrument design","Failure to randomize appraisers during the trials"],"a":3,"x":["Failure to randomize is a GR&R error, not a nonlinearity cause — the cross-topic negative-stem trap. A/B/C are the listed nonlinearity causes.","Ref: Lines 637–640."]},{"id":"c14q23","ch":14,"n":23,"q":"In an orthogonal-regression comparison of two measurement devices, the analysis shows an intercept whose CI contains 0 and a slope whose CI contains 1. The correct interpretation is:","o":["The two devices most likely provide equivalent measurements","The two devices are significantly different and one must be scrapped","The correlation coefficient is negative","The devices disagree on bias but agree on linearity"],"a":0,"x":["Intercept ≈ 0 and slope ≈ 1 (CIs containing 0 and 1) devices give equivalent measurements. B inverts the decision rule.","Ref: Lines 761–765."]},{"id":"c14q24","ch":14,"n":24,"q":"In an attribute agreement study, the \"within appraiser\" assessment reveals that an inspector rated the same sample \"pass\" on one trial and \"fail\" on another. This inconsistency is most directly a measure of:","o":["Appraiser-to-standard agreement","The appraiser's repeatability with himself/herself","Part-to-part variation","Bias relative to the master"],"a":1,"x":["\"Within appraiser\" inconsistency = the appraiser disagreeing with himself/herself across trials (attribute repeatability). A (appraiser-vs-standard) is the adjacent metric — close-difference trap.","Ref: Lines 805–810."]},{"id":"c14q25","ch":14,"n":25,"q":"An attribute study shows Appraiser C accepting 33.3% of bad parts (A/R) while rejecting 0% of good parts. From a quality-risk standpoint, the chapter's reasoning is that:","o":["Appraiser C poses no risk and needs no attention","Accepting bad parts is the chief customer risk, but C's consistency makes C easier to train","Rejecting good parts is the only concern worth addressing","Appraiser C should be removed from all future studies"],"a":1,"x":["Accepting bad parts (A/R) is the principal customer risk; the chapter notes C's consistency makes C the easiest to train. A and D are over/under-reactions; C ignores the accept-bad-part risk.","Ref: Lines 832–837."]},{"id":"c14q26","ch":14,"n":26,"q":"A team is conducting MSA for a product-control application (100% inspection, conformance/nonconformance decisions). Per the AIAG reference, the samples:","o":["Must cover the entire process operating range, and assessment is based on total variation","Need not cover the entire process range, and assessment is based on the feature tolerance","Must be drawn randomly and assessed against process variation only","Must be ignored entirely in favor of an independent capability study"],"a":1,"x":["Product-control MSA need not span the full process range and is assessed against the feature tolerance. A describes the process-control situation — the central close-difference distinction in the AIAG reference.","Ref: Lines 870–875."]},{"id":"c14q27","ch":14,"n":27,"q":"A Six Sigma team is debating when to perform MSA in a variation-reduction effort. The chapter's key justification for doing it first is:","o":["MSA is the cheapest study to run","All process data are, in effect, filtered through the measurement system, so its uncertainty must be understood first","MSA replaces the need for SPC","Customers require it before any project charter is signed"],"a":1,"x":["MSA goes first because all process data are filtered through the measurement system; the chapter's Key Point is understanding measurement uncertainty. A/C/D are plausible \"true-but-not-here\" or false claims.","Ref: Lines 38–42, 893–895."]},{"id":"c15q1","ch":15,"n":1,"q":"Natural process limits are calculated from which of the following?","o":["The upper and lower specification limits set by engineering","Process variation, after all special causes have been removed and the process is stable","The customer's stated tolerance band on the engineering drawing","The midpoint of the specification ± the contractual tolerance"],"a":1,"x":["Natural (process) limits derive from the process's own variation, computed only after special causes are removed and statistical stability is achieved. Distractors A/C/D all describe specification limits (engineering/customer expectations) — the exact \"correct-definition-wrong-concept\" trap ASQ favors, since natural-limits-vs-spec-limits is the single most confused pairing in this section.","Reference: Lines 11–16 (III.F.1 opening)."]},{"id":"c15q2","ch":15,"n":2,"q":"A process is in a state of statistical control but is producing parts outside the specification. According to the control-limit-versus-specification grid, the correct action is to:","o":["Stop the process immediately and investigate","Take no action, since the process is predictable","Change the process and/or the specification","Investigate the out-of-control condition"],"a":2,"x":["In the grid, in control + does not meet spec = \"Change process and/or specification.\" Distractor A (Stop!) is the fourth quadrant (not in control + not meeting spec); D is the third quadrant (n ot in control + meeting spec). The trap is the \"true-but-not-here\" mechanism — every option is a real grid action, but only one matches the stated quadrant.","Reference: Lines 17–25, 37–48 (Figure 15.1 grid)."]},{"id":"c15q3","ch":15,"n":3,"q":"An operator argues that an out-of-control process needs no investigation because it is currently producing parts that meet specification. What is the Green Belt's strongest technical rebuttal?","o":["Out-of-control processes always produce more scrap than rework","Specifications are always tighter than natural process limits","An out-of-control process is unpredictable and may go out of spec at any time","Meeting specification is irrelevant once special causes appear"],"a":2,"x":["The chapter's explicit teaching point: out-of-control = loss of predictability, so the process \"may go out of spec at any time.\" This is the third-quadrant argument the text directly addresses. A and B are plausible-sounding overgeneralizations (\"always\"); D overstates by dismissing specification entirely.","Reference: Lines 26–30, 78–80."]},{"id":"c15q4","ch":15,"n":4,"q":"When reviewing X̄ and R control charts during a capability study, which chart must be confirmed to be in control first, and why?","o":["The X̄ chart, because the average must be centered before variation is judged","The R chart, because variation must be in control before adjusting averages","Either chart, because the order of review has no statistical consequence","The X̄ chart, because range is robust to nonnormality"],"a":1,"x":["Range chart must be confirmed in control before the average chart — variation must be controlled before adjusting the mean. A inverts the correct order (classic close-difference swap); C denies a real statistical dependency; D mislabels which chart benefits from CLT robustness.","Reference: Lines 88–90."]},{"id":"c15q5","ch":15,"n":5,"q":"A packaged product is specified at 1.00–1.10 lbs. A stable X̄ and R chart (subgroup n = 4) yields a grand average of 1.065 lbs and an average range of 0.05. Given d₂ = 2.059 for n = 4, the estimated process standard deviation is closest to:","o":["0.012 lbs","0.024 lbs","0.050 lbs","0.103 lbs"],"a":1,"x":["σ̂ = R̄ / d₂ = 0.05 / 2.059 = 0.0243 ≈ 0.024 lbs. Distractor A halves it; C plants the raw R̄ value; D plants the spec width (0.103) — all \"wrong-input\" arithmetic traps testing whether you know the formula, not just the numbers.","Reference: Lines 105–116 (worked example; d₂ = 2.059 for n = 4)."]},{"id":"c15q6","ch":15,"n":6,"q":"Using the data in Question 5, the number of standard deviations between the process average and the upper specification limit (Z_U) is closest to:","o":["1.46","2.70","0.49","4.50"],"a":0,"x":["Z_U = (USL − X̄) / σ̂ = (1.10 − 1.065) / 0.024 = 0.035 / 0.024 = 1.46. Distractor B is Z_L (2.70, the lower -limit Z) — the close-difference trap; C is the resulting Cpk; D is the 4.5σ Six Sigma convention. Choosing B means you computed the wrong specification side.","Reference: Lines 121–139 (Z_U formula and worked value)."],"ctx":[{"n":5,"q":"A packaged product is specified at 1.00–1.10 lbs. A stable X̄ and R chart (subgroup n = 4) yields a grand average of 1.065 lbs and an average range of 0.05. Given d₂ = 2.059 for n = 4, the estimated process standard deviation is closest to:"}]},{"id":"c15q7","ch":15,"n":7,"q":"Using the data in Question 5, the Cpk of this process is closest to:","o":["0.69","0.49","1.33","1.46"],"a":1,"x":["Cpk = min(Z_U, Z_L) / 3 = min(1.46, 2.70) / 3 = 1.46 / 3 = 0.49. The \"min\" forces use of the nearest spec limit. Distractor A is Cp (0.69); C is a common target value; D is the Z_U itself (forgetting the ÷3). This rewards knowing Cpk keys off the worse (nearest) limit.","Reference: Lines 331–335 (Cpk = min(Z_U, Z_L)/3; example = 0.49)."],"ctx":[{"n":5,"q":"A packaged product is specified at 1.00–1.10 lbs. A stable X̄ and R chart (subgroup n = 4) yields a grand average of 1.065 lbs and an average range of 0.05. Given d₂ = 2.059 for n = 4, the estimated process standard deviation is closest to:"}]},{"id":"c15q8","ch":15,"n":8,"q":"Using the data in Question 5, the natural process limits are approximately 0.993 to 1.137 lbs. The correct interpretation is:","o":["The process is capable, because the average falls within specification","The process is not capable, because the natural process limits fall outside the specification","The process is capable, because the limits are symmetric about the mean","Capability cannot be judged without the Cp value"],"a":1,"x":["Natural limits (0.993–1.137) extend beyond the spec (1.00–1.10) process not capable. A and C are \"true-statement / wrong-conclusion\" traps (the average is in spec, and the limits are symmetric — but neither makes the process capable); D falsely claims capability is indeterminate when the ±3σ comparison already answers it.","Reference: Lines 146–149."],"ctx":[{"n":5,"q":"A packaged product is specified at 1.00–1.10 lbs. A stable X̄ and R chart (subgroup n = 4) yields a grand average of 1.065 lbs and an average range of 0.05. Given d₂ = 2.059 for n = 4, the estimated process standard deviation is closest to:"}]},{"id":"c15q9","ch":15,"n":9,"q":"Which of the following is an example of a common cause of variation, not a special cause?","o":["A significant change in incoming material quality","Natural variation in the incoming material supply","An operator with a markedly different skill level","Equipment drift mid-shift"],"a":1,"x":["Natural variation in incoming material supply is a listed common cause. A, C, D are all listed special causes (significant material change, skill variation, equipment drift). The trap is lexical proximity — \"incoming material supply\" (common) vs. \"change in incoming material quality\" (special) sit one word apart.","Reference: Lines 168–173."]},{"id":"c15q10","ch":15,"n":10,"q":"Which is the recommended best-practice method for selecting which characteristics warrant a full capability study?","o":["Study every measurable characteristic to be comprehensive","Let the operators choose based on intuition during the run","Perform a process FMEA, identify parameters needing SPC, and build a control plan","Select only the characteristics the customer has not mentioned"],"a":2,"x":["Best practice = process FMEA identify SPC parameters control plan, because SPC costs money and characteristic selection must be deliberate. A (\"study everything\") is the cost-blind distractor; B and D are absurd-but-plausible operational shortcuts.","Reference: Lines 183–188."]},{"id":"c15q11","ch":15,"n":11,"q":"What is the first step in conducting a process capability study?","o":["Construct a histogram of the subgroup averages","Perform measurement system analysis (MSA)","Calculate Cp and Cpk","Verify the data follow a normal distribution"],"a":1,"x":["MSA is explicitly the first step — measurement variation can mislead both capability and stability assessment. A, C, D are all genuine later steps (sequence trap testing whether you know the order, an Evaluate-level discrimination).","Reference: Lines 204–214."]},{"id":"c15q12","ch":15,"n":12,"q":"A process capability study is being prepared. Which statement about normality is correct?","o":["Normality is required for control chart monitoring but not for capability calculation","Normality is required to compute capability for continuous data; the histogram should be built from the original readings, not the averages","The histogram should be constructed from subgroup averages to invoke the central limit theorem","Nonnormal continuous data make capability calculation impossible under any circumstance"],"a":1,"x":["Normality is not needed for control-chart monitoring but is required to measure capability for continuous data, and the histogram is built from original readings — not averages (averages would artificially normalize via CLT). A inverts the requirement; C is the specific \"averages\" trap; D overstates (transformation exists).","Reference: Lines 234–243."]},{"id":"c15q13","ch":15,"n":13,"q":"A continuous-data set fails a normality test prior to a capability study. The appropriate next action is to:","o":["Abandon the capability study and report the process as incapable","Apply a transformation such as Box-Cox or Johnson before calculating capability","Recalculate using the subgroup ranges instead of individual values","Switch to attribute control charts and report DPMO instead"],"a":1,"x":["Nonnormal continuous data Box-Cox or Johnson transformation before capability calculation. A and C/D are defeatist or method-switching distractors that abandon the legitimate transformation path the text prescribes.","Reference: Lines 247–251."]},{"id":"c15q14","ch":15,"n":14,"q":"Which statement about subgroup selection for a capability study is correct?","o":["Subgroup sizes greater than five are the most common in practice","An individual (n = 1) chart is the most sensitive to detecting process shifts","An average–standard-deviation chart (n > 8) is more powerful for detecting shifts but costlier for data collection","Within-subgroup variation should be larger than between-subgroup variation"],"a":2,"x":["Average–std-dev charts (n > 8) are more powerful for shift detection but costlier. A is false (>5 is uncommon); B is inverted (individual charts are less sensitive); D is inverted (within-subgroup variation should be smaller). Three inversions — a dense close-difference cluster.","Reference: Lines 216–227."]},{"id":"c15q15","ch":15,"n":15,"q":"A Green Belt is sampling a batch oven-baking process where prior study proved a randomly selected part represents the batch. Which sampling approach is most appropriate?","o":["Systematic sampling, taking every nth part off the line","Subgroup sampling of five consecutive parts","Random sampling, averaging the measurements as one subgroup data point","Census sampling of the entire batch"],"a":2,"x":["Random sampling with averaging suits batch processes (oven baking is the text's own example) when a random part is proven representative. A is for conveyor/transactional flows; B is the standard subgroup approach for continuous lines; D is impractical. Tests matching the sampling type to the process type.","Reference: Lines 284–302 (Figure 15.4 sampling types)."]},{"id":"c15q16","ch":15,"n":16,"q":"A process is described as having achieved \"Six Sigma quality.\" Which pair of index values corresponds to this claim?","o":["Cp = 1.33 and Cpk = 1.33","Cp = 1.0 and Cpk = 1.0","Cp = 2.0 and Cpk = 1.5","Cp = 1.5 and Cpk = 2.0"],"a":2,"x":["Six Sigma quality = Cp = 2 and Cpk = 1.5 (the 1.5σ shift baked into Cpk). A is \"merely capable\" (1.33); B is \"barely meets spec\" (1.0); D transposes the two values — the attribution-swap pattern applied to numbers.","Reference: Lines 367–368 (shop-floor value #1)."]},{"id":"c15q17","ch":15,"n":17,"q":"A Cp, Cpk value of exactly 1.0 indicates that the process:","o":["Has achieved Six Sigma quality","Barely meets specification and will produce about 0.27% defective units","Is producing units outside the engineering specification","Has an unrealistically loose specification"],"a":1,"x":["Cp/Cpk = 1.0 barely capable, ~0.27% defective. A (Six Sigma), C (<1.0 condition), and D (≥3 loose-spec condition) are each other rows from the same shop-floor list — pure \"true-but-not-here\" distractors drawn from adjacent bullets.","Reference: Lines 370–371 (value #3)."]},{"id":"c15q18","ch":15,"n":18,"q":"Which statement comparing Cp and Cpk is correct?","o":["Cpk is always equal to or larger than Cp","Cp and Cpk are equal only when the process is perfectly centered","The Cp value changes as the process is centered toward target","A negative Cp indicates the process average is outside a specification limit"],"a":1,"x":["Cp = Cpk only at perfect centering. A inverts the inequality (Cpk ≤ Cp, always); C is false (Cp is centering-invariant); D misattributes the negative-index rule to Cp rather than Cpk. Four interpretation rules compressed into one discrimination item.","Reference: Lines 385–388 (rules #3, #4, #5)."]},{"id":"c15q19","ch":15,"n":19,"q":"A Green Belt re-centers a process toward target. Assuming nothing else in the process changes, what happens to the Cp value?","o":["It increases proportionally to the reduction in off-centering","It decreases as the mean moves","It does not change","It becomes equal to Cr"],"a":2,"x":["Cp does not change with centering unless something in the process itself changes — Cp reflects potential (spread vs. tolerance), not location. A and B assume Cp tracks centering (that's Cpk's job); D confuses Cp with its inverse Cr.","Reference: Lines 383–384 (rule #2)."]},{"id":"c15q20","ch":15,"n":20,"q":"Pp and Ppk differ from Cp and Cpk in that they:","o":["Use the sample standard deviation instead of the sigma derived from the average range","Do not require the process to be stable","Are always larger than the corresponding capability indices","Are used only for one-sided specifications"],"a":0,"x":["Pp/Ppk use the sample standard deviation (s); Cp/Cpk use σ from the average range. B is false (both families require stability per the text); C is false (no fixed ordering); D confuses them with one-sided-spec handling. The s-vs-R̄/d₂ distinction is the defining difference.","Reference: Lines 404–415; 509–515."]},{"id":"c15q21","ch":15,"n":21,"q":"An assembly line is sampled weekly; the data include long-term variation and shifts. A critical parameter is specified at 9.99–10.03, with a measured sample standard deviation of 0.004 and a mean of 10.014. The Pp of this parameter is closest to:","o":["1.33","1.67","2.00","0.69"],"a":1,"x":["Pp = (USL − LSL) / 6s = (10.03 − 9.99) / (6 × 0.004) = 0.04 / 0.024 = 1.667. A is the resulting Ppk (1.33); C is the upper-side ratio alone (2.00); D is an unrelated value. Tests the un-minimized full-tolerance formula.","Reference: Lines 426–442 (worked example)."]},{"id":"c15q22","ch":15,"n":22,"q":"Using the data in Question 21, the Ppk of this parameter is closest to:","o":["2.00","1.67","1.33","0.49"],"a":2,"x":["Ppk = min[(USL − X̄)/3s, (X̄ − LSL)/3s] = min[0.016/0.012, 0.024/0.012] = min[1.33, 2.00] = 1.33. A is the upper ratio (the side you must discard); B is Pp; D is unrelated. The \"min\" again forces the nearest (worse) limit — selecting A means you picked the better side by mistake.","Reference: Lines 433–442."],"ctx":[{"n":21,"q":"An assembly line is sampled weekly; the data include long-term variation and shifts. A critical parameter is specified at 9.99–10.03, with a measured sample standard deviation of 0.004 and a mean of 10.014. The Pp of this parameter is closest to:"}]},{"id":"c15q23","ch":15,"n":23,"q":"In the Question 21 scenario, why should the engineer report Pp and Ppk rather than Cp and Cpk?","o":["Because Pp and Ppk are always preferred over capability indices","Because the data include long-term variation and shifts, whereas Cp/Cpk apply when stability is monitored by SPC and sigma is derived from the mean range","Because the sample size is too small to compute Cp and Cpk","Because the specification is one-sided"],"a":1,"x":["Pp/Ppk fit data containing long-term variation/shifts; Cp/Cpk apply when stability is SPC-monitored and σ comes from the mean range. A (\"always preferred\") contradicts the text's own preference for Cp/Cpk as the better measure; C and D introduce irrelevant sample-size/one-sided reasoning.","Reference: Lines 418–424; 487–489."],"ctx":[{"n":21,"q":"An assembly line is sampled weekly; the data include long-term variation and shifts. A critical parameter is specified at 9.99–10.03, with a measured sample standard deviation of 0.004 and a mean of 10.014. The Pp of this parameter is closest to:"}]},{"id":"c15q24","ch":15,"n":24,"q":"Which scenario most appropriately calls for the Cpm index?","o":["A supplier ships incoming material with no SPC data available","A process where the target value differs from the conventional nominal midpoint of the specification, such as a shaft/hole mechanical fit","A process with a one-sided maximum specification limit","A process being assessed for short-term capability over 20 subgroups"],"a":1,"x":["Cpm applies when a target differs from the nominal midpoint — shaft/hole fits (clearance, interference, transition) are the text's exemplar. A describes the Pp/Ppk supplier use case; C is one-sided Cp territory; D is short-term Cp/Cpk. Each distractor is the correct trigger for a different index.","Reference: Lines 497–500."]},{"id":"c15q25","ch":15,"n":25,"q":"Which factor characterizes long-term capability data rather than short-term?","o":["A single set of trained operators and homogeneous material","Approximately 20 to 30 subgroups from a focused, stable run","Multiple machine streams, spindles/cavities, operators, and environmental changes such as temperature and humidity","A single piece of measuring equipment used throughout"],"a":2,"x":["Long-term data span multiple machines, spindles/cavities, operators, and environmental shifts wider variation. A, B, D all describe short-term conditions (focused, homogeneous, ~20–30 subgroups, single gauge). A clean short-vs-long discrimination.","Reference: Lines 503–516."]},{"id":"c15q26","ch":15,"n":26,"q":"The concept of sigma shift holds that, over the long term, a typical process mean shifts and drifts by approximately how much, and a centered 6σ process therefore corresponds to what defect rate?","o":["1.0σ; 1350 ppm total","1.5σ; 3.4 ppm total","3.0σ; 0.27 ppm total","1.5σ; 1350 ppm on each side"],"a":1,"x":["~1.5σ long-term shift (Bill Smith / Motorola); a centered 3σ process = 1350 ppm each side, while a shifted 6σ process = 3.4 ppm total. B states the shift and the 6σ rate correctly. A misstates the rate; C inflates the shift; D mislabels the 1350 ppm figure as belonging to the 6σ case (it's the centered-3σ figure).","Reference: Lines 522–533 (Figure 15.7); 537–543."]},{"id":"c15q27","ch":15,"n":27,"q":"AIAG and ANSI recommend using Pp and Ppk when a process is not in control. Which statement best captures the documented criticism of this position?","o":["It is universally accepted as best practice with no dissent","It is controversial because an out-of-control process is by definition unpredictable, and one cited authority argues such indices \"tell you nothing\"","It is wrong because Pp and Ppk cannot be calculated for unstable processes","It is preferred because out-of-control processes always follow a normal distribution"],"a":1,"x":["AIAG/ANSI recommend Pp/Ppk for out-of-control processes, but this is controversial precisely because such processes are unpredictable; Montgomery is quoted calling the indices essentially worthless. A denies the documented dissent; C is false (they can be calculated); D is a fabricated normality claim. Tests judgment about the value of a recommended practice — textbook Evaluate.","Reference: Lines 443–448."]},{"id":"c16q1","ch":16,"n":1,"q":"In a multi-vari study, which type of variation is also referred to as within-part variation?","o":["Cyclical","Temporal","Positional","Lot-to-lot"],"a":2,"x":["Positional = within-part variation (a characteristic measured on the same product).","DNA trap: every distractor is a real variation term placed in the wrong slot — cyclical = part-to-part, temporal = shift-to-shift, lot-to-lot is a cyclical synonym.","Reference: p.271, lines 31–34; Table 16.1, lines 56–60."]},{"id":"c16q2","ch":16,"n":2,"q":"A Green Belt measures the inner diameter of a single casting at its top, middle, and bottom. The differences found among these three locations on the same part represent which type of variation?","o":["Cyclical (part-to-part)","Temporal (shift-to-shift)","Positional (within-part)","Random measurement error"],"a":2,"x":["Same-part, location-to-location difference (top/middle/bottom) is the textbook definition of within-part variation. \"Random measurement error\" is a planted non-category distractor.","Reference: p.271, lines 31–32; casting example p.273, lines 111–113."]},{"id":"c16q3","ch":16,"n":3,"q":"Cyclical variation, as captured by a multi-vari study, is best described as:","o":["Variation measured at different locations on the same unit","Part-to-part or lot-to-lot variation","A change that occurs over time between shifts","Variation introduced by the measurement system"],"a":1,"x":["Option A defines positional; C defines temporal; D is a planted non-category. Only B matches cyclical.","Reference: p.271, lines 32–33."]},{"id":"c16q4","ch":16,"n":4,"q":"Parts machined on the first shift consistently differ from those machined on the second and third shifts. In multi-vari terminology, this is an example of:","o":["Positional variation","Cyclical variation","Temporal variation","Within-part variation"],"a":2,"x":["Shift-to-shift change over time = temporal. The stem deliberately uses \"shift\" to bait test-takers who haven't fixed the temporal = shift-to-shift mapping.","Reference: p.271, lines 33–34."]},{"id":"c16q5","ch":16,"n":5,"q":"What is the primary purpose of a multi-vari chart?","o":["To establish control limits for an ongoing process","To identify the dominant source among positional, cyclical, and temporal variation","To test whether two variables are correlated","To predict a future value of a dependent variable"],"a":1,"x":["A multi-vari chart's job is to expose which of the three variation families dominates. Distractor A (control limits) belongs to SPC/control charts; C to correlation; D to regression — a classic ASQ \"right tool, wrong purpose\" spread.","Reference: p.270, lines 25–30."]},{"id":"c16q6","ch":16,"n":6,"q":"During the stainless-steel casting study, team members with ideas for reducing the out-of-round (within-part) variation were asked to hold those ideas until later. Why?","o":["Out-of-round variation cannot be measured with a dial bore gage","The dominant part-to-part variation would mask any reduction in the smaller variation","Out-of-round is not a recognized category of variation","Within-part variation can only be addressed in the Control phase"],"a":1,"x":["The single most important multi-vari strategy lesson — attack the largest source first because it masks smaller sources (confirmed later when reduced part-to-part variation revealed the out-of-round). Scenario-application item mirroring the \"named-process\" DNA.","Reference: p.276, lines 196–198; p.278, lines 259–261."]},{"id":"c16q7","ch":16,"n":7,"q":"On a multi-vari line chart, the length of each vertical line or schematic shape represents:","o":["The process mean","The range of values found in each sample set","The total number of samples collected","The population standard deviation"],"a":1,"x":["Line length encodes the range within a sample. Distractors (mean, count, σ) are plausible-sounding statistics, the hallmark ASQ near-miss pattern.","Reference: p.271, lines 39–43."]},{"id":"c16q8","ch":16,"n":8,"q":"Before applying a multi-vari study to a call center's average handle time (AHT), the chapter recommends that the data first be:","o":["Converted from variable to attribute data","Plotted on an individuals control chart","Stratified by transaction type and complexity","Adjusted using the central limit theorem"],"a":2,"x":["Transactional processes vary by customer need, so raw AHT is meaningless until stratified. Distractors invoke real-but-unrelated tools (control chart, CLT).","Reference: p.278, lines 270–276."]},{"id":"c16q9","ch":16,"n":9,"q":"The multi-vari sampling procedure recommends selecting a manageable sample size of approximately:","o":["1 to 2 samples","3 to 5 samples","10 to 15 samples","30 or more samples"],"a":1,"x":["Direct procedure recall. Distractor D (30+) baits confusion with the normality/CLT \"n ≥ 30\" rule from other chapters — a cross-topic trap.","Reference: p.271, lines 47–49."]},{"id":"c16q10","ch":16,"n":10,"q":"Correlation measures which two attributes of the relationship between variables?","o":["Slope and intercept","Strength and direction","Mean and variance","Accuracy and precision"],"a":1,"x":["Verbatim definition. \"Slope and intercept\" (regression) and \"accuracy and precision\" (MSA) are right-domain/wrong-concept distractors.","Reference: p.279, lines 304–305."]},{"id":"c16q11","ch":16,"n":11,"q":"Which statement about an independent variable (x) is correct?","o":["It is impacted by changes to the dependent variable","It is conventionally plotted on the vertical axis","It causes the observed variation and is not impacted by changes to other variables","It is always equal to the y-intercept"],"a":2,"x":["x causes the observed variation and is unaffected by other variables; A inverts the dependency, B inverts the axis convention, D confuses the variable with a regression parameter.","Reference: p.279–280, lines 306–311; axis convention lines 338–340."]},{"id":"c16q12","ch":16,"n":12,"q":"A Green Belt finds that two process variables are strongly correlated. What is the most accurate conclusion?","o":["A change in one variable definitely causes the change in the other","Correlation alone does not establish a cause-and-effect relationship","The correlation coefficient must equal +1.0","One of the variables must be normally distributed"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter's central caution and a guaranteed exam trap. A is the seductive wrong answer; D and C inject irrelevant conditions.","Reference: p.280, lines 321–324, 333–334."]},{"id":"c16q13","ch":16,"n":13,"q":"The correlation coefficient, r, can take values within which range?","o":["0 to +1.0","−1.0 to +1.0","0 to 100","−0.5 to +0.5"],"a":1,"x":["Option A (0 to +1.0) is the highest-yield trap — test-takers forget r carries sign. C and D are distortions.","Reference: p.280, lines 348–349."]},{"id":"c16q14","ch":16,"n":14,"q":"A correlation coefficient calculated as approximately 0 is evidence that:","o":["There is a perfect positive relationship between x and y","There is a perfect negative relationship between x and y","There is no relationship between x and y","The relationship is nonlinear and the data must be transformed"],"a":2,"x":["r ≈ 0 no linear relationship. D is tempting because a nonlinear pattern can also produce low r, but the chapter's stated interpretation of r ≈ 0 is \"no relationship.\"","Reference: p.280–281, lines 351–353."]},{"id":"c16q15","ch":16,"n":15,"q":"Which of the following correlation coefficients indicates the strongest relationship between two variables?","o":["r = +0.20","r = −0.95","r = +0.50","r = 0.00"],"a":1,"x":["Sign-vs-strength trap — strength is judged by closeness to ±1.0, not by being positive. Test-takers anchored on \"positive = stronger\" wrongly pick C or A. This is the exact \"close difference\" DNA from the sample bank.","Reference: p.281, lines 391–392."]},{"id":"c16q16","ch":16,"n":16,"q":"By convention, the independent and dependent variables are placed on a scatter plot as follows:","o":["Independent on the vertical axis; dependent on the horizontal axis","Independent on the horizontal axis; dependent on the vertical axis","Both variables on the horizontal axis","The axis assignment is arbitrary and has no convention"],"a":1,"x":["Direct convention recall; A inverts it, D denies the convention exists.","Reference: p.280, lines 338–340."]},{"id":"c16q17","ch":16,"n":17,"q":"The p-value in a hypothesis test is best defined as:","o":["The probability that the null hypothesis is true","The probability of obtaining a test statistic at least as extreme as the calculated value, assuming the null hypothesis is true","The strength of the correlation between two variables","The slope of the regression line"],"a":1,"x":["The most common p-value misinterpretation is option A (\"probability the null is true\") — a deliberate, exam-favorite distractor. The correct definition is conditional on the null being true. C/D pull from adjacent concepts.","Reference: p.282, lines 430–432."]},{"id":"c16q18","ch":16,"n":18,"q":"An analysis yields a correlation coefficient of 0.866 with a p-value of 0.333 (α = 0.05). What is the correct interpretation?","o":["There is a strong, statistically significant positive correlation","The correlation appears strong but is not statistically significant","The null hypothesis should be rejected","There is a strong negative correlation"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter's signature teaching example — r = 0.866 looks strong, but p = 0.333 > 0.05, so it is not statistically significant. A is the trap for anyone reading r without the p-value. This is an Evaluate-level interpretation item.","Reference: p.281, lines 423–424; p.282, lines 435–437."]},{"id":"c16q19","ch":16,"n":19,"q":"Using the common cutoff of 0.05, you would reject the null hypothesis when:","o":["The p-value is greater than 0.05","The p-value is less than 0.05","The correlation coefficient is greater than 0.05","The sample size is less than 0.05"],"a":1,"x":["Decision-rule recall. C/D are nonsense-unit distractors testing whether the candidate confuses the p-value with r or n.","Reference: p.282, lines 433–434."]},{"id":"c16q20","ch":16,"n":20,"q":"In the simple regression equation ŷ = a + bx, the term b represents:","o":["The predicted value of y","The y-intercept","The slope of the line","The independent variable"],"a":2,"x":["In ŷ = a + bx, a = intercept, b = slope, x = independent variable. Distractors map each option to a different term in the same equation — pure close-difference DNA.","Reference: p.286, lines 554–559."]},{"id":"c16q21","ch":16,"n":21,"q":"The least squares method identifies the best-fitting regression line by:","o":["Maximizing the correlation coefficient","Minimizing the sum of the squared deviations of observed y-values from predicted y-values","Forcing the slope to equal 1.0","Placing an equal number of points above and below the line"],"a":1,"x":["Definition of least squares. A confuses it with correlation; C/D are invented criteria.","Reference: p.286, lines 562–564, 572–575."]},{"id":"c16q22","ch":16,"n":22,"q":"In the temperature–viscosity regression example, the calculated test statistic (≈4.24) did not exceed the critical t-value (4.303), and the p-value was 0.0513 (α = 0.05). What conclusion is drawn?","o":["Reject the null hypothesis; a relationship between the variables exists","Fail to reject the null hypothesis; a relationship between the variables does not exist","The correlation coefficient is exactly +1.0","The regression line has a negative slope"],"a":1,"x":["t (4.24) < critical (4.303) and p = 0.0513 > 0.05 fail to reject H₀; no relationship demonstrated. A is the trap for candidates who eyeball 0.0513 as \"basically significant\" — the chapter is explicit that 0.0513 > 0.05 means cannot reject. An Evaluate-level conclusion item.","Reference: p.288, lines 644–647; p.290, lines 710–713."]},{"id":"c16q23","ch":16,"n":23,"q":"A correlation coefficient of r = +1.0 indicates:","o":["No relationship between x and y","A perfect positive correlation (positive slope)","A perfect negative correlation (negative slope)","A nonlinear relationship"],"a":1,"x":["r = +1.0 is a perfect positive correlation with positive slope. Distractor A (no correlation) is the r ≈ 0 case; C inverts the sign; D is the curved-pattern case. Sign-and-magnitude recall.","Reference: p.281, lines 454–455."]},{"id":"c16q24","ch":16,"n":24,"q":"A scatter plot shows points that clearly follow a curved pattern rather than a straight line. This is best described as:","o":["A strong positive correlation","A strong negative correlation","No correlation","A nonlinear relationship"],"a":3,"x":["Figure 16.12d — points relate but not in a straight line. Trap: \"no correlation\" (C) is tempting because r may be low, but the chapter explicitly classifies a curved pattern as a nonlinear relationship, not as \"no relationship.\"","Reference: p.281, lines 386, 452–453."]},{"id":"c16q25","ch":16,"n":25,"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the four types of correlation illustrated in scatter plots in this chapter?","o":["Strong positive correlation","Strong negative correlation","No correlation","Perfect random correlation"],"a":3,"x":["The four illustrated types are strong positive, strong negative, no correlation, and nonlinear. \"Perfect random correlation\" is a fabricated term — the classic ASQ \"plausible-sounding non-category\" distractor.","Reference: p.281, lines 337, 386, 444–453."]},{"id":"c16q26","ch":16,"n":26,"q":"What is the principal benefit of using multiple linear regression instead of simple linear regression?","o":["It eliminates the need to calculate a p-value","It allows a higher proportion of the variation in y to be explained","It guarantees that a cause-and-effect relationship exists","It forces the correlation coefficient to equal zero"],"a":1,"x":["Verbatim rationale for multiple regression — more independent variables can explain a higher proportion of variation in y. A, C, and D are each false statements that exploit common misconceptions (p-value still required; regression never proves causation; adding variables does not zero out r).","Reference: p.292–293, lines 774–777."]},{"id":"c16q27","ch":16,"n":27,"q":"In the complaints-versus-month regression, the line was ŷ = 6.50015 + 0.5833x. Because the slope is positive 0.5833, the correct interpretation is that:","o":["The number of complaints decreases by about one per month","The number of complaints increases over time at an average rate of about one per month","There is no relationship between month and complaints","The slope value is also the y-intercept"],"a":1,"x":["A positive slope (+0.5833) means y rises as x rises; the text states complaints increase at ~1 per month. A inverts the slope sign (the highest-value trap), C denies the demonstrated relationship, D confuses slope with intercept. Note: tests the slope interpretation, not the OCR-suspect arithmetic.","Reference: p.287, lines 597–599."]},{"id":"c16q28","ch":16,"n":28,"q":"The chapter cautions against confusing Σx² with (Σx)². Which statement correctly describes Σx²?","o":["First add all the x values, then square the total","First square each x value, then add the squared terms","Divide each x value by the sample size, then square","Subtract the mean from each x, then sum"],"a":1,"x":["Σx² = square-then-sum; (Σx)² = sum-then-square. A is the (Σx)² operation — the exact confusion the chapter warns about. C/D are unrelated operations. A pure \"close-difference\" computation trap.","Reference: p.283, lines 476–478."]},{"id":"c16q29","ch":16,"n":29,"q":"The chapter notes that there is \"a W that caused X, a V that caused W,\" and so on, meaning every cause is itself the result of a prior cause. This concept is termed a:","o":["Confidence interval","Causal chain","Confounding factor","Regression to the mean"],"a":1,"x":["Direct term recall from the correlation-vs-causation discussion. Distractors are all real statistical terms from adjacent topics (confidence interval, confounding, regression to the mean) — right domain, wrong concept.","Reference: p.280, lines 316–320."]},{"id":"c16q30","ch":16,"n":30,"q":"When deciding which variable is independent and which is dependent, the chapter cautions that an incorrect choice may lead to misinterpretation. The recommended best practice is to:","o":["Always make the larger-valued variable the independent one","Examine the relationship from both directions to see which makes the most sense","Assign the axes arbitrarily, since it does not affect results","Place the dependent variable on the horizontal axis"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter's caution: examine the relationship from both directions before assigning x and y. A and D are fabricated/false rules; C contradicts the stated axis convention and the warning itself. \"Choose the best answer\" style.","Reference: p.280, lines 341–344."]},{"id":"c16q31","ch":16,"n":31,"q":"According to the multi-vari sampling procedure, after concentrating additional study on the area(s) of apparent maximum variation, the final step is to:","o":["Calculate the risk priority number (RPN)","Establish statistical control limits for the process","Make process improvements and repeat the multi-vari study to confirm the results","Disband the team and close the project"],"a":2,"x":["Final procedure step (step 8) — improve, then repeat the study to confirm. A (RPN) belongs to FMEA, B (control limits) to SPC, D is a non-sequitur — all cross-topic tool confusions typical of ASQ.","Reference: p.273, lines 88–91."]},{"id":"c16q32","ch":16,"n":32,"q":"A multi-vari chart shows the spread of the vertical sample lines growing progressively wider as you move from left to right along the time scale. This pattern most likely indicates:","o":["Out-of-round (within-part) variation","A variability shift over time","Reduced variability at the center","A perfect positive correlation"],"a":1,"x":["Progressive widening across the time axis = Figure 16.4, a variability shift over time (temporal). A (out-of-round) is within-part, not time-driven; C describes the opposite (Figure 16.3, less variability); D is from the correlation domain. Chart-reading / Analyze-level item.","Reference: p.272, lines 42–43, 81."]},{"id":"c16q33","ch":16,"n":33,"q":"In the stainless-steel casting study, to specifically detect out-of-round variation, the Green Belt measured the inner diameter:","o":["At the top, middle, and bottom sections only","At three different angles (12 o'clock, 2 o'clock, and 4 o'clock)","On five parts at equal time intervals across the shift","On one part from each of three shifts"],"a":1,"x":["Out-of-round is detected by measuring the ID at multiple angles. Distractor A (top/middle/bottom) is the strong trap — it IS within-part variation, but it detects top-to-bottom, not out-of-round. C is the temporal-capture method; D is shift sampling. Requires distinguishing two within-part sub-measurements.","Reference: p.273–274, lines 118–124."]},{"id":"c17q1","ch":17,"n":1,"q":"Regarding the null hypothesis (H₀) in any hypothesis test, which statement is correct?","o":["It can be accepted when there is insufficient evidence to reject it","It can only be rejected or fail to be rejected","It represents the change or improvement being investigated","It is declared \"innocent\" until the data prove it guilty"],"a":1,"x":["A null hypothesis can only be rejected or fail to be rejected; it can never be \"accepted\" for lack of evidence.","DNA: A is the textbook trap (\"accepted\"); C correctly defines the alternative hypothesis placed in the null's slot; D misuses \"innocent,\" a word the chapter explicitly says does not apply.","Reference: \"The Null and Alternative Hypotheses,\" lines 25–26 (and \"innocent\" caution, lines 24–25)."]},{"id":"c17q2","ch":17,"n":2,"q":"The alternative hypothesis (Hₐ) always states that the population mean is:","o":["=, ≥, or ≤ a specific value","<, ≠, or > a specific value","only ≠ a specific value","≥ or ≤ a specific value"],"a":1,"x":["The alternative always uses <, ≠, or >.","DNA: A and D describe the null's operators (=, ≥, ≤) — the classic correct-definition-wrong-slot swap; C is too narrow (≠ is only the two-tail case).","Reference: \"The alternative hypothesis…\" lines 36–39."]},{"id":"c17q3","ch":17,"n":3,"q":"An incoming lot of conforming (good) parts is incorrectly judged defective and rejected. This decision error is best classified as a:","o":["Type II error, the consumer's risk","Type I error, the consumer's risk","Type I error, the producer's risk","Type II error, the producer's risk"],"a":2,"x":["Good product rejected = reject a true H₀ = Type I (α) = producer's risk.","DNA: every distractor swaps either the error number or the risk label — pure close-difference construction.","Reference: \"Type I error,\" lines 53–56."]},{"id":"c17q4","ch":17,"n":4,"q":"A shipment of defective components passes receiving inspection and is accepted as good. What decision error has occurred?","o":["Type I error (α), the producer's risk","Type II error (β), the consumer's risk","Type I error (β), the consumer's risk","Type II error (α), the producer's risk"],"a":1,"x":["Defective product accepted = fail to reject a false H₀ = Type II (β) = consumer's risk.","DNA: mirrors Q3; A/C/D mismatch the Greek letter or the risk owner.","Reference: \"Type II error,\" lines 58–61."]},{"id":"c17q5","ch":17,"n":5,"q":"A manufacturer claims a new golf ball increases driving distance by more than 20 yards (Hₐ: μ > 20). How is the test configured?","o":["Two-tail test, with α split between both tails","One-tail test, with the entire α risk in the left tail","One-tail test, with the entire α risk in the right tail","Two-tail test, with the entire α risk in the right tail"],"a":2,"x":["Hₐ: μ > 20 places the entire α in the right tail.","DNA: B reverses the tail (left), the highest-frequency one-tail trap; A/D wrongly invoke a two-tail design.","Reference: golf-ball example and one-tail rule, lines 92–97, 115–117."]},{"id":"c17q6","ch":17,"n":6,"q":"A two-tail hypothesis test is required whenever the alternative hypothesis is expressed as:","o":["Greater than (>)","Less than (<)","Not equal to (≠)","Greater than or equal to (≥)"],"a":2,"x":["A two-tail test is triggered only by Hₐ expressed as ≠.","DNA: A and B are the one-tail triggers; D is a null operator.","Reference: \"Two-Tail Test,\" lines 130–133."]},{"id":"c17q7","ch":17,"n":7,"q":"Which statement best describes the p-value used to evaluate statistical significance?","o":["The probability that the null hypothesis is true","The probability of obtaining a test statistic at least as extreme as the one observed, assuming the null hypothesis is true","The probability that the alternative hypothesis is true","The probability of committing a Type II error"],"a":1,"x":["Standard p-value definition: probability of a result at least as extreme as observed, given H₀ is true.","DNA: A is the canonical p-value misconception (\"probability H₀ is true\"); D substitutes β.","Reference: p-value definition, lines 180–182."]},{"id":"c17q8","ch":17,"n":8,"q":"A team finds a statistically significant 0.5% difference between two process defect levels, but the customer permits a 3% defect level because the step is non-critical. The difference is best described as:","o":["Both statistically and practically significant","Statistically significant but not necessarily practically significant","Practically significant but not statistically significant","Neither statistically nor practically significant"],"a":1,"x":["A real but tiny (0.5%) difference well inside an allowed 3% tolerance is statistically significant yet not practically meaningful.","DNA: scenario-decomposition (Analyze) with the trap that \"statistically significant\" implies \"important.\"","Reference: practical-significance example, lines 187–194."]},{"id":"c17q9","ch":17,"n":9,"q":"Statistical power in a hypothesis test is defined as:","o":["The probability of committing a Type I error","One minus the significance level (1 − α)","The probability of correctly rejecting a false null hypothesis (1 − β)","The probability of failing to reject a true null hypothesis"],"a":2,"x":["Power = 1 − β (probability of correctly rejecting a false H₀).","DNA: B (1 − α) is the confidence level — the signature close-difference swap; A = α; D = the correct-decision cell when H₀ is true.","Reference: error-matrix note and power discussion, line 80; lines 207–210."]},{"id":"c17q10","ch":17,"n":10,"q":"What level of power is typically required before a conclusion is drawn from a hypothesis test?","o":["0.5 and above","0.8 and above","0.95 and above","0.99 and above"],"a":1,"x":["Power of 0.8 and above is typically required (0.9 in higher-risk cases).","DNA: C and D borrow familiar confidence levels (95%, 99%) to bait pattern-matching.","Reference: \"What Is a Desired Power?\" lines 204–206."]},{"id":"c17q11","ch":17,"n":11,"q":"Which change will increase the power of a hypothesis test?","o":["Decreasing the sample size","Decreasing the variability (σ) in the population","Decreasing the size of the effect to be detected","Increasing the proportion of expected frequencies below 5"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter states power rises as σ decreases and as effect size increases.","DNA: C reverses the effect-size relationship (Analyze trap); A reduces n (lowers power); D is a chi-square condition dropped in as noise.","Reference: \"How to Calculate…Sample Size,\" lines 217–220."]},{"id":"c17q12","ch":17,"n":12,"q":"The sample size required for a hypothesis test depends on all of the following EXCEPT:","o":["The desired Type I (α) and Type II (β) risk","The minimum difference to be detected between the population means","The variation in the characteristic being measured","The number of factors and interactions in the design"],"a":3,"x":["Sample size depends on α, β, the minimum detectable difference, and σ — not on the number of factors/interactions.","DNA: D imports a legitimate DOE concept into the wrong topic — the hallmark \"true elsewhere, wrong here\" distractor.","Reference: sample-size drivers, lines 154–158."]},{"id":"c17q13","ch":17,"n":13,"q":"A quality engineer must make an inference about a population mean from a small sample (n < 30) when the population variance is unknown. The appropriate distribution is the:","o":["Normal (Z) distribution","Student's t-distribution","Chi-square distribution","F-distribution"],"a":1,"x":["Small n with unknown σ Student's t.","DNA: A (Z) is the trap for those who forget σ is unknown; C/D are valid distributions used for other tests (variance, ratio of variances).","Reference: \"Test for Means / Student's t-Test,\" lines 272–275, 382–384."]},{"id":"c17q14","ch":17,"n":14,"q":"The Z-test for a population mean is appropriately applied when:","o":["The population is normal and σ is known (or the sample is large)","σ is unknown and the sample is small","The data are ranks with no distributional assumption","Two population variances are being compared"],"a":0,"x":["Z-test requires σ known with a normal population (or a large sample).","DNA: B describes the t-test conditions placed in the Z slot; C = nonparametric; D = F-test.","Reference: Z-test conditions and procedure, lines 307–318, 320–322."]},{"id":"c17q15","ch":17,"n":15,"q":"For a one-sample t-test based on a random sample of 20 parts, the number of degrees of freedom is:","o":["18","19","20","21"],"a":1,"x":["df = n − 1 = 19.","DNA: A/C/D are the off-by-one and off-by-zero values that careless reading produces; the chapter's own example uses the 19th row of the t-table.","Reference: \"df = n − 1,\" line 405 (example use, line 434)."]},{"id":"c17q16","ch":17,"n":16,"q":"A two-sample t-test comparing two machines returns a p-value of 0.255 at α = 0.05. The correct interpretation is to:","o":["Reject the null hypothesis; the machine means differ","Fail to reject the null hypothesis; the data do not show the machine means to differ","Accept the alternative hypothesis at the 0.05 level","Declare the test invalid because the p-value exceeds 0.05"],"a":1,"x":["p = 0.255 > 0.05 fail to reject H₀; the data do not demonstrate a mean difference.","DNA: A reverses the decision; C uses the forbidden word \"accept\" for the alternative; D is statistically nonsensical.","Reference: two-sample t interpretation, lines 714–718."]},{"id":"c17q17","ch":17,"n":17,"q":"A confidence interval for the population mean is symmetrical about the average. Which statement correctly contrasts this with the confidence interval for the variance?","o":["The CI for the variance is also symmetrical about the point estimate","The CI for the variance is not symmetrical because it is based on the chi-square distribution","The CI for the variance is not symmetrical because it is based on the F-distribution","The CI for the variance is symmetrical only when the sample is large"],"a":1,"x":["The variance CI is asymmetric because it derives from the (skewed) chi-square distribution, unlike the symmetric mean CI.","DNA: C swaps in the F-distribution — a precise close-difference trap; A and D deny the asymmetry outright.","Reference: \"Confidence Intervals for Variation,\" lines 525–527."]},{"id":"c17q18","ch":17,"n":18,"q":"The chi-square method for constructing a confidence interval or test for a population variance is valid only when the data come from:","o":["A binomial distribution","A Poisson distribution","A normal distribution","Any distribution (it is distribution-free)"],"a":2,"x":["The chapter states the chi-square method \"is only for the normal distribution.\"","DNA: A/B name other distributions; D wrongly labels it distribution-free (that is nonparametric).","Reference: variance-test method note, line 501."]},{"id":"c17q19","ch":17,"n":19,"q":"Before testing a claim about a population proportion, a Green Belt verifies that np > 5 and n(1 − p) > 5. The purpose of this check is to confirm that:","o":["The binomial distribution can be approximated by the normal distribution","The sample is large enough to use the t-distribution","The group variances are homogeneous","The data may be analyzed nonparametrically"],"a":0,"x":["np > 5 and n(1 − p) > 5 justify approximating the binomial with the normal.","DNA: B/C/D each attach a real statistical idea (t-test sample size, equal variances, nonparametrics) to the wrong condition.","Reference: p-test conditions, lines 604–605, 611–613."]},{"id":"c17q20","ch":17,"n":20,"q":"In a two-mean, equal-variance t-test comparing two independent samples of sizes n₁ and n₂, the degrees of freedom equal:","o":["n₁ + n₂","n₁ + n₂ − 1","n₁ + n₂ − 2","(n₁ − 1)(n₂ − 1)"],"a":2,"x":["df = n₁ + n₂ − 2 for the pooled (equal-variance) two-mean t-test.","DNA: D is the contingency-table df form — a high-quality distractor that looks \"statistical\"; A/B are off by one or two.","Reference: two-mean equal-variance t-test, line 674."]},{"id":"c17q21","ch":17,"n":21,"q":"An operator measures the same five parts before and after equipment calibration and wants to compare the two means. The most appropriate test is the:","o":["Two-mean, unequal-variance t-test","One-way ANOVA","Paired t-test","F-test"],"a":2,"x":["Same items measured twice (before/after) creates dependency paired t-test.","DNA: A is the dominant trap — treating dependent measurements as independent; the chapter explicitly lists calibration before/after as a paired-test scenario.","Reference: \"Paired t-Test,\" lines 753–764, 776–780."]},{"id":"c17q22","ch":17,"n":22,"q":"The F-statistic used in a two-sample variance test is best described as the:","o":["Difference between two sample means divided by the standard error","Ratio of two sample variances","Sum of squared deviations from the mean","Ratio of observed to expected frequencies"],"a":1,"x":["F = ratio of two sample variances.","DNA: A is the t-statistic structure; D evokes the chi-square (O vs. E) — both correct-elsewhere swaps.","Reference: \"F-Test,\" lines 811–812."]},{"id":"c17q23","ch":17,"n":23,"q":"A factory wants to determine whether aging improves consistency (i.e., reduces variation) in product crispness. An F-test is selected. This situation calls for a:","o":["Two-tail test, with α split between both tails","One-tail test, with the α risk in the right tail","Two-tail test, with the entire α in the left tail","Test that cannot be performed with the F-distribution"],"a":1,"x":["Testing for a reduction in variance is directional one-tail with the rejection region in the right tail.","DNA: A/C dilute the α incorrectly; the scenario framing (\"improvement in consistency\") is the Analyze cue.","Reference: pickle-factory F-test, lines 856–858."]},{"id":"c17q24","ch":17,"n":24,"q":"The fundamental purpose of one-way ANOVA is to:","o":["Compare the variances of two populations","Test whether several population means are all equal","Determine whether two categorical variables are independent","Estimate a confidence interval for a single proportion"],"a":1,"x":["One-way ANOVA tests whether several population means are all equal.","DNA: A = F-test (two variances); C = contingency-table chi-square; D = proportion CI — three real procedures in wrong slots.","Reference: \"Single-Factor ANOVA,\" lines 865–867."]},{"id":"c17q25","ch":17,"n":25,"q":"Which of the following is NOT a stated condition for using one-way ANOVA?","o":["The populations of interest are normally distributed","The samples are independent of each other","Each population has the same variance","Each treatment group has the same number of observations"],"a":3,"x":["The three stated conditions are normality, independence, and equal variance; equal sample sizes are not required (ANOVA handles unbalanced designs).","DNA: D is plausible-sounding but unlisted — the negative-stem exception.","Reference: ANOVA conditions a–c, lines 875–877."]},{"id":"c17q26","ch":17,"n":26,"q":"In one-way ANOVA, the F-statistic is calculated as:","o":["Mean square within treatments ÷ mean square between treatments","Mean square between treatments ÷ mean square within treatments","Sum of squares total ÷ degrees of freedom total","Sum of squares between ÷ sum of squares within"],"a":1,"x":["F = MS_between ÷ MS_within.","DNA: A inverts the ratio — the single most common ANOVA error; D omits conversion of SS to mean squares.","Reference: ANOVA table and F definition, lines 891, 909, 974."]},{"id":"c17q27","ch":17,"n":27,"q":"A one-way ANOVA compares k = 3 treatments using a total of N = 12 readings. The numerator and denominator degrees of freedom for the F-statistic are, respectively:","o":["2 and 9","3 and 12","1 and 11","2 and 11"],"a":0,"x":["Numerator df = k − 1 = 2; denominator df = N − k = 9.","DNA: B uses raw k and N; C/D mix in the total df (N − 1 = 11).","Reference: ANOVA df rule, lines 910–911 (worked example, lines 975–979)."]},{"id":"c17q28","ch":17,"n":28,"q":"In an ANOVA, the calculated F = 23.92 and the critical value from the F-table is 4.26. Because ANOVA is a right-tailed test, the correct decision is to:","o":["Fail to reject H₀; the means are equal","Reject H₀; at least one mean differs from the others","Reject H₀; every mean differs from every other mean","Accept H₀ because F exceeds the critical value"],"a":1,"x":["Test statistic (23.92) > critical value (4.26) reject H₀; the alternative says at least one mean differs.","DNA: C overstates (\"every mean differs\") — the alternative never claims that; D misstates the rule and uses \"accept.\"","Reference: ANOVA decision rule, lines 912–914, 981–986, 1010–1011."]},{"id":"c17q29","ch":17,"n":29,"q":"For a chi-square goodness-of-fit test with k categories, the test is always ______ and the critical value is found using ______ degrees of freedom.","o":["Two-tailed; k","Right-tailed; k − 1","Left-tailed; k − 1","Right-tailed; N − k"],"a":1,"x":["Chi-square goodness-of-fit is always right-tailed, with critical value at row k − 1.","DNA: A/C corrupt the tail; D substitutes the ANOVA denominator df (N − k).","Reference: chi-square procedure, lines 1075–1078."]},{"id":"c17q30","ch":17,"n":30,"q":"Which of the following is NOT a stated condition for performing a chi-square goodness-of-fit test?","o":["All expected frequencies are at least 1","At most 20 percent of the expected frequencies are less than 5","The observed frequencies must follow a normal distribution","The test statistic is the sum of (O − E)²/E across all categories"],"a":2,"x":["The stated conditions concern expected frequencies (≥ 1; at most 20% below 5); there is no normality requirement on the counts.","DNA: A/B are the real conditions and D is a true formula statement — only C is false, making it the exception.","Reference: chi-square conditions, lines 1070–1083."]},{"id":"c17q31","ch":17,"n":31,"q":"In a chi-square goodness-of-fit test, one defect category historically occurs 42% of the time, and the current sample contains 208 rejected parts. The expected frequency for that category is approximately:","o":["33.28","58.24","87.36","100"],"a":2,"x":["Expected = 0.42 × 208 = 87.36.","DNA: A (0.16 × 208) and B (0.28 × 208) are the expected values for the other categories in the same example — premium distractors; D (100) is the observed count for this category, baiting the observed/expected confusion.","Reference: expected-frequency calculation, lines 1118–1121."]},{"id":"c17q32","ch":17,"n":32,"q":"A contingency table is a two-dimensional classification of count data. Applying the chi-square procedure to it tests whether:","o":["Two variables are independent or have a significant association","A single population mean equals a target value","Two population variances are equal","A process is in statistical control"],"a":0,"x":["Contingency tables test independence/association between two classifications.","DNA: B = one-sample mean test; C = F-test; D = SPC/control charts (a different chapter) — all real, all misplaced.","Reference: \"Contingency Tables,\" lines 1205–1209."]},{"id":"c17q33","ch":17,"n":33,"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes parametric from nonparametric tests?","o":["Nonparametric tests assume the population is normally distributed","Parametric tests make no assumption about the population distribution","Nonparametric tests make no assumption about a specific population distribution and are called \"distribution-free\"","Parametric tests can only be used on ranked data"],"a":2,"x":["Nonparametric (\"distribution-free\") tests assume no specific population distribution; parametric tests assume one (typically normal).","DNA: A and B reverse the two definitions — the precise close-difference swap; D falsely restricts parametric tests to ranks.","Reference: \"Parametric and Nonparametric Tests,\" lines 1226–1234."]},{"id":"c18q1","ch":18,"n":1,"q":"According to the chapter, what is the primary justification for baselining the current state before making improvements?","o":["To eliminate the need for stakeholder involvement","Without knowing where you started, it is unlikely you can determine where you want to land","To replace the project charter with a control plan","To calculate the risk priority number for the process"],"a":1,"x":["Direct paraphrase of the chapter's anchor line on baselining.","DNA: distractors A/C/D are real Six Sigma artifacts (stakeholders, charter, RPN) inserted to bait phase confusion.","Ref: Gap Analysis, p. 329 (opening paragraph)."]},{"id":"c18q2","ch":18,"n":2,"q":"Which of the following is NOT listed in the chapter as a reason baselining the current state is important?","o":["Identify resources required to accomplish the goals","Make a leadership commitment","Eliminate the need for a control plan in the Control phase","Articulate the improvements made as financial and economic benefits"],"a":2,"x":["The chapter's five listed reasons are: identify stakeholders, identify resources, make a leadership commitment, measure post-improvement progress, articulate financial/economic benefits. \"Eliminate the control plan\" is fabricated.","DNA: classic \"NOT-listed\" trap using true-sounding but absent content.","Ref: Gap Analysis, p. 329 (bulleted list)."]},{"id":"c18q3","ch":18,"n":3,"q":"Priya measures customer perception before launching a new mobile application. According to the chapter, this gap-analysis scenario is best classified as:","o":["Reactive","Corrective","Proactive","Adaptive"],"a":2,"x":["\"Customer perception prior to a product/service launch\" is listed under Proactive.","DNA: \"Adaptive/Corrective\" are plausible quality buzzwords not used by the text.","Ref: p. 329, \"A. Proactive.\""]},{"id":"c18q4","ch":18,"n":4,"q":"A logistics team measures operational efficiency after rolling out an organizational restructuring. Per the chapter, this is an example of which gap-analysis scenario?","o":["Proactive","Predictive","Reactive","Preventive"],"a":2,"x":["\"Measuring operational efficiency after the organizational change\" is listed under Reactive.","DNA: timing word (\"after\") is the discriminator; A baits the proactive/reactive flip.","Ref: p. 330, \"B. Reactive.\""]},{"id":"c18q5","ch":18,"n":5,"q":"Govindarajan wants to understand the user experience following a feature change to a product. According to the chapter, what should he do FIRST?","o":["Launch the feature and wait for customer complaints to accumulate","Identify and measure upfront the predefined metrics that will clearly show the difference resulting from the change","Conduct a fault tree analysis of the existing feature","Calculate the RPN of the proposed feature"],"a":1,"x":["\"Identify the attributes (predefined metrics) … upfront.\"","DNA: scenario stem with named actor (sample-question signature); A is the tempting \"do nothing first\" trap.","Ref: p. 330, \"We need to start with the objective…\" paragraph."]},{"id":"c18q6","ch":18,"n":6,"q":"The chapter states that gap analysis begins with which of the following?","o":["The objective","The control plan","The risk priority number","The fishbone diagram"],"a":0,"x":["\"We need to start with the objective.\"","DNA: B/C/D are correct tools placed at the wrong starting point.","Ref: p. 330."]},{"id":"c18q7","ch":18,"n":7,"q":"The chapter notes some performance gaps are visible at Define while others surface later. When does it say gaps in customer service typically become more apparent?","o":["At the Define stage","By the Analyze stage, as the team \"peels the onion\"","At the Control stage","At project closure"],"a":1,"x":["\"…by the analysis stage, gaps in customer service will be more apparent as we peel the onion.\"","DNA: the \"peel the onion\" phrase is the textual fingerprint; A is the half-right trap (satisfaction level is apparent at Define, but service gaps are not).","Ref: p. 329–330."]},{"id":"c18q8","ch":18,"n":8,"q":"The chapter explicitly names several tools practitioners use to perform gap analysis. Which of the following is one of them?","o":["Poka-yoke","Kanban","Failure Mode Effects Analysis (FMEA)","Single-minute exchange of dies (SMED)"],"a":2,"x":["Listed tools: audits, assessments, value stream analysis, FMEA, hypothesis testing.","DNA: distractors are legitimate Lean tools from other chapters (Improve/Control), exploiting cross-phase confusion.","Ref: p. 330 (final paragraph)."]},{"id":"c18q9","ch":18,"n":9,"q":"Per the chapter, if a team plans to improve a process through Value Stream Mapping, what is the logical step?","o":["Skip baselining and proceed directly to defining the future state","Identify and baseline the current state's predefined metrics","Build a fault tree before mapping the stream","Conduct a 5 Why analysis before identifying any metrics"],"a":1,"x":["\"…identifying and baselining the current state's predefined metrics are logical steps.\"","DNA: A is the \"skip the baseline\" trap that contradicts the chapter's entire thesis.","Ref: p. 330."]},{"id":"c18q10","ch":18,"n":10,"q":"The chapter cites which standard in connection with gap-analysis examples such as energy use and energy loss?","o":["ISO 9001:2015","ISO 14001","ISO 50001:2018","ISO/TS 16949"],"a":2,"x":["\"(Ref: ISO 50001:2018).\"","DNA: near-miss ISO numbers are a recall trap; 50001 is the energy-management standard.","Ref: p. 330."]},{"id":"c18q11","ch":18,"n":11,"q":"Which of the following appears in the chapter's predefined-metrics example for a value-stream gap analysis?","o":["Net Promoter Score","Overall Equipment Effectiveness (OEE)","Customer Effort Score","Days Sales Outstanding"],"a":1,"x":["OEE % is an explicit row in the metrics table.","DNA: distractors are real CX/finance metrics not used in the chapter. Note: tested by name only—corrupted numeric cells were avoided.","Ref: p. 330, predefined-metrics table."]},{"id":"c18q12","ch":18,"n":12,"q":"In the chapter's predefined-metrics example, the \"Ratio of Value add to lead time\" is also identified as which metric?","o":["Overall Equipment Effectiveness","Rolled Throughput Yield","Process Cycle Efficiency","Takt time"],"a":2,"x":["\"Ratio of Value add to lead time (Process Cycle Efficiency) %.\"","DNA: OEE and RTY appear elsewhere in the same table as bait.","Ref: p. 330, table row."]},{"id":"c18q13","ch":18,"n":13,"q":"According to the chapter, what is the only thing a user genuinely needs in order to perform a 5 Why analysis?","o":["A Black Belt certification","Advanced statistical software","The persistence to keep asking \"why\" until reaching the root cause","A completed fault tree"],"a":2,"x":["\"No special training or skill is required. All the user needs is the persistence to ask 'why'…\"","DNA: A/B overstate prerequisites—the inverse-of-truth distractor.","Ref: 5 Why Analysis, p. 331 (opening)."]},{"id":"c18q14","ch":18,"n":14,"q":"A Green Belt insists his team must ask \"why\" exactly five times—no more, no fewer. Based on the chapter, what is the most accurate critique?","o":["The team should instead ask why exactly three times","The number five is a literal, mandatory requirement of the method","Five is merely a guideline; the root cause may be reached at the third, seventh, or ninth why depending on complexity","The team should stop at the first plausible answer regardless of depth"],"a":2,"x":["\"5 Why is merely a guideline and not literal.\"","DNA: B is the literal-five trap the chapter explicitly debunks; D over-corrects into stopping too early.","Ref: p. 331."]},{"id":"c18q15","ch":18,"n":15,"q":"The chapter notes many organizations now use a different name for the 5 Why tool to dispel the \"exactly five\" misunderstanding. That name is:","o":["Root-Root Analysis","Why-Why Analysis","Cause-Cause Analysis","Deep-Dive Analysis"],"a":1,"x":["\"…organizations call this tool 'Why-Why Analysis.'\"","DNA: A/C/D are invented parallel-construction names.","Ref: p. 331."]},{"id":"c18q16","ch":18,"n":16,"q":"Per the chapter, what is the risk of stopping a 5 Why analysis too soon (e.g., at the second or third why)?","o":["The identified cause may still be shallow","The cause becomes too broad to act on","The cause automatically becomes a system-level breakdown","The analysis converts itself into a fault tree"],"a":0,"x":["\"If you stop too soon … the cause may still be shallow.\"","DNA: B is the opposite failure mode (stopping too late)—pairs with Q17 to test discrimination.","Ref: p. 331."]},{"id":"c18q17","ch":18,"n":17,"q":"The chapter warns that going several whys deeper than required tends to produce causes that are:","o":["Too specific and immediately actionable","Too broad—such as \"organizational culture\" or \"market conditions\"—and not actionable","Identical to the original problem statement","Always the true system-level root cause"],"a":1,"x":["\"…the cause can get very broad, like 'organizational culture' or 'market conditions'…not quite actionable.\"","DNA: the verbatim examples are the fingerprint; A inverts the consequence.","Ref: p. 331."]},{"id":"c18q18","ch":18,"n":18,"q":"According to the chapter, what is the ideal point at which to stop asking \"why\"? (Choose the best answer.)","o":["After exactly five iterations","As soon as any plausible cause is named","When the cause is specific and actionable and the organization has no control over the subsequent why","When the cause has broadened to \"market conditions\""],"a":2,"x":["\"The ideal point … is where the root cause is specific and actionable, and the problem solver or organization does not have any control to act on the subsequent 'why.'\"","DNA: \"best answer\" forced choice; A is the literal-five trap, B is the stop-too-soon trap.","Ref: p. 331."]},{"id":"c18q19","ch":18,"n":19,"q":"In the Toyota robot example, the team found the circuit overloaded and blew a fuse. Per the chapter, had the team stopped at this first \"why,\" what would have occurred?","o":["The operator would keep adding lubrication","The organization would treat the fuse as the root cause and stock fuses as maintenance spares","A filter would have been installed on the pump","The oil pump would have been replaced"],"a":1,"x":["\"…the organization would have thought that the fuse is the root cause and kept an inventory of fuses…\"","DNA: A is the second -why consequence (Q20)—tests whether the candidate tracks the chain depth.","Ref: p. 332."]},{"id":"c18q20","ch":18,"n":20,"q":"In the Toyota example, the chapter warns that stopping at the SECOND \"why\" (insufficient lubrication) would have led to which problem?","o":["Unnecessarily stocking fuses","Advising the operator to keep adding lubrication, causing floor spillage and factory accidents","Installing a filter prematurely","Replacing the bearings"],"a":1,"x":["\"…the operator would have been advised to keep pouring more lubrication … spillage on the floor and factory accidents.\"","DNA: A swaps in the first-why consequence; depth tracking is the analyze-level skill.","Ref: p. 332."]},{"id":"c18q21","ch":18,"n":21,"q":"In the Toyota example, what was the stated reason the pump intake was clogged with metal shavings?","o":["The bearings had locked up","There was no filter on the pump","The fuse had blown","The technician failed to lubricate the bearings"],"a":1,"x":["Fifth why: \"Why is the intake clogged with metal shavings? Because there is no filter on the pump.\"","DNA: A/C/D are other links in the same causal chain—sequence-comprehension trap.","Ref: p. 332, steps 4–5."]},{"id":"c18q22","ch":18,"n":22,"q":"The chapter argues that identifying \"no filter on the pump\" was sufficient for this robot but not sufficient overall. Why? (Choose the best answer.)","o":["Because the fuse was actually the true root cause","Because it did not address the process- or system-level breakdown that could allow a missing filter, washer, or safety part elsewhere","Because lubrication remained insufficient","Because five whys must always be extended to seven"],"a":1,"x":["The text states the fifth why was sufficient for this robot but did not address the process/system-level breakdown (missing washer/safety part elsewhere).","DNA: A reverses the already-rejected fuse cause.","Ref: p. 332 (\"Is the fifth 'why'… sufficient?\")."]},{"id":"c18q23","ch":18,"n":23,"q":"When the Toyota analysis continued past the filter, the chapter identifies the deeper system-level cause as:","o":["A defective fuse","Excessive lubrication on the bearings","No error proofing and no process for preventive maintenance","A permanently clogged intake"],"a":2,"x":["\"Why did the maintenance technician forget…? There was no error proofing and a process for preventive maintenance.\"","DNA: C is system-level; A/B/D are shallower chain links.","Ref: p. 332, continuation steps 1–2."]},{"id":"c18q24","ch":18,"n":24,"q":"After reaching the system-level root cause, what corrective measures does the chapter recommend? (Choose the best answer.)","o":["Increase the spare-fuse inventory","Ensure a defined, documented, fully deployed maintenance process AND provide closed-loop feedback to the vendor or retrofit the equipment so it can only be assembled when the filter is present","Increase the lubrication frequency on a fixed schedule","Remove the filter requirement to simplify assembly"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter prescribes a defined/documented/deployed maintenance process and closed-loop vendor feedback or error-proof retrofit (assembly only when filter present).","DNA: \"best answer\" compound option; A/C/D are the shallow fixes the chapter explicitly rejects.","Ref: p. 332 (\"One should stop at this point…\")."]},{"id":"c18q25","ch":18,"n":25,"q":"The chapter describes an effective way to confirm a root cause when it is practical to do so. What is it?","o":["Asking \"why\" exactly five times","Turning the root cause ON and OFF to verify problem prevention and re-creation","Summing the probabilities of all contributing causes","Constructing an AND/OR logic tree"],"a":1,"x":["\"If you have the ability to turn ON and OFF the root cause to verify the problem prevention and problem re-creation, this is an effective way to confirm the root cause.\"","DNA: D imports FTA logic (cross-tool trap).","Ref: p. 332."]},{"id":"c18q26","ch":18,"n":26,"q":"When turning a root cause on and off is impractical or could harm customers, the chapter advises practitioners to:","o":["Skip verification entirely","Be creative in verification—e.g., using software simulation or equivalent methods","Default immediately to fault tree analysis","Halt the investigation at the third why"],"a":1,"x":["\"…practitioners are advised to be creative in verification—for example, using a software simulation or equivalent methods.\"","DNA: A is the negligence trap; C is a cross-tool default.","Ref: p. 332."]},{"id":"c18q27","ch":18,"n":27,"q":"According to the chapter, customer complaints represent which type of approach to verifying whether a root cause and its corrective action were effective?","o":["A proactive approach","A predictive approach","A reactive approach","A preventive approach"],"a":2,"x":["\"Complaints from the customer are a reactive approach to verify if the root cause and corresponding corrective action has been effective.\"","DNA: reuses the proactive/reactive axis from Gap Analysis—tests it in a new context.","Ref: p. 332."]},{"id":"c18q28","ch":18,"n":28,"q":"Fault tree analysis is best described as:","o":["A bottom-up tally of individual component reliability","A top-down failure analysis in which a system failure is examined through a combined series of lower-level events","A brainstorming tool that organizes ideas into natural categories","A scheduling tool for leveling production volume"],"a":1,"x":["\"Fault tree analysis (FTA) is a top-down failure analysis in which a failure of a system is analyzed using a combined series of lower-level events.\"","DNA: A flips the direction; C/D are unrelated tools.","Ref: Fault Tree Analysis, p. 332 (opening)."]},{"id":"c18q29","ch":18,"n":29,"q":"The chapter states fault tree analysis is mainly used in which discipline?","o":["Marketing analytics","Reliability engineering","Financial auditing","Workforce planning"],"a":1,"x":["\"This analysis method is mainly used in reliability engineering…\"","DNA: domain-recall item.","Ref: p. 332."]},{"id":"c18q30","ch":18,"n":30,"q":"What is the KEY distinction the chapter draws between fault tree analysis and other approaches such as the 5 Why?","o":["FTA is bottom-up whereas the 5 Why is top-down","Other approaches assume a single root cause, whereas FTA considers multiple causes for each cause and assigns AND/OR logical relationships with probabilities","FTA requires no special skill, unlike the 5 Why","FTA can only be applied to non-technical programs"],"a":1,"x":["This is the headline close-difference: \"Other analysis approaches assume one root cause … fault tree analysis takes into consideration multiple causes for each cause and assigns a logical relationship between the causes.\"","DNA: A is the killer trap—both methods are top-down, so candidates who \"remember FTA is top-down\" wrongly reject B and pick A. The real differentiator is single vs. multiple causes + AND/OR + probability.","Ref: p. 333 (\"Other analysis approaches assume one root cause…\")."]},{"id":"c18q31","ch":18,"n":31,"q":"In fault tree analysis, the relationship between contributing causes is expressed using:","o":["SIPOC logic","AND/OR logic","PDCA cycles","SMART criteria"],"a":1,"x":["\"Understand the AND/OR logic leading to the failure of a system.\"","DNA: distractors are real frameworks from other chapters.","Ref: p. 333 (FTA uses list)."]},{"id":"c18q32","ch":18,"n":32,"q":"According to the chapter, how are probabilities handled in fault tree analysis?","o":["All causes are assumed equally likely","Probabilities are calculated for causes in series or in parallel, and all probabilities sum up to the top-level cause","Probabilities are ignored because FTA is purely qualitative","Only the single most likely cause receives a probability"],"a":1,"x":["\"The probabilities are thus calculated for causes in series or in parallel. All the probabilities sum up to the top-level cause.\"","DNA: C is the \"qualitative-only\" trap; A/D understate the probabilistic treatment.","Ref: p. 333."]},{"id":"c18q33","ch":18,"n":33,"q":"The chapter notes FTA is not limited to technical uses. When applied to implementing programs, which is the correct FIRST step?","o":["Identify the next-tier objectives","Identify a top-level program objective","Calculate the program's RPN","Develop the AND/OR relationships before any objectives are set"],"a":1,"x":["Program sequence: identify the top-level program objective next-level objectives develop AND/OR relationships identify subsequent next-tier objectives.","DNA: A and D are downstream steps offered as the \"first\" step—sequence trap.","Ref: p. 333 (\"Fault tree analysis is not only applicable to technical applications…\")."]},{"id":"c19q1","ch":19,"n":1,"q":"Maria runs each experimental trial five times in a row, taking five measurements without ever resetting or changing the machine setup between them. Which term best describes this practice?","o":["Replication","Repetition","Randomization","Blocking"],"a":1,"x":["Repetition = running trials under the same machine setup, back-to-back, without resetting. Replication requires re-establishing each trial's conditions, so it captures setup-to-setup variation. The scenario specifies \"without ever resetting the setup,\" which is the textbook definition of repetition.","DNA: Option A is the classic ASQ trap — the right definition of the wrong term. This replication-vs-repetition pair is a known semantic-distinction item.","Reference: Basic Terms section (replication vs. repetition discussion), early chapter (~p. 343–344)."]},{"id":"c19q2","ch":19,"n":2,"q":"In a designed experiment, the input variables that the experimenter deliberately sets and changes in order to observe their effect on the output are called:","o":["Responses","Treatments","Factors","Blocks"],"a":2,"x":["Factors are the deliberately manipulated input variables.","DNA: A (Responses) defines the output — correct definition of the wrong concept. B (Treatments) is a combination of factor levels, not the inputs themselves. D (Blocks) groups nuisance variation. All three distractors are real terms placed in the wrong slot.","Reference: Basic Terms section (factors, levels, responses, treatments)."]},{"id":"c19q3","ch":19,"n":3,"q":"All of the following are fundamental principles of design of experiments EXCEPT:","o":["Randomization","Replication","Blocking","Confounding"],"a":3,"x":["The three foundational DOE principles are randomization, replication, and blocking. Confounding is a consequence/problem (especially in fractional designs), not a design principle.","DNA: Negative-stem (\"EXCEPT\") item where the odd-one-out is a genuine DOE term from a different category — mirrors the sample's \"which is not a step/principle\" style.","Reference: Basic Terms / DOE principles section (randomization, blocking, replication)."]},{"id":"c19q4","ch":19,"n":4,"q":"An engineer plans a full factorial experiment to study 3 factors, each set at 2 levels. How many experimental runs does the full factorial design require?","o":["4","6","8","9"],"a":2,"x":["A two-level, three-factor full factorial = 2³ = 8 runs.","DNA: Apply-level calculation. D (9) lures anyone who computes 3² instead of 2³; A (4) is the half-fraction count; B (6) is 3×2. Each distractor reflects a specific arithmetic slip.","Reference: Full vs. fractional factorial discussion; Table 19.2 (the eight-run 2³ matrix), ~p. 348–349."]},{"id":"c19q5","ch":19,"n":5,"q":"A team decides to study 3 factors using a two-level half-fraction design (2³⁻¹) instead of the full factorial. How many runs will this fractional design require?","o":["2","4","6","8"],"a":1,"x":["2³⁻¹ = 2² = 4 runs (half of the full eight).","DNA: Apply-level. D (8) is the full factorial — the trap for anyone who ignores the \"−1.\" A (2) over-halves.","Reference: Half-fraction discussion, Tables 19.3–19.4, ~p. 349."]},{"id":"c19q6","ch":19,"n":6,"q":"In a fractional factorial design, a main effect and a two-factor interaction share an identical column of plus/minus signs, so their separate influences cannot be distinguished. These two effects are said to be:","o":["Replicated","Randomized","Confounded (aliased)","Blocked"],"a":2,"x":["Confounding (aliasing) is precisely the inability to separate a main effect from an interaction because they occupy the same sign column.","DNA: Replication, randomization, and blocking are all real, tempting DOE terms — the close-difference pattern where only one term fits the described mechanism.","Reference: \"Effects and Confounding\" section, ~p. 349–350."]},{"id":"c19q7","ch":19,"n":7,"q":"When the two lines on an interaction plot are drawn essentially parallel to one another, the most appropriate conclusion is that:","o":["A strong interaction is present between the two factors","No meaningful interaction is present between the two factors","Both main effects are equal to zero","The design is unbalanced and must be repeated"],"a":1,"x":["Parallel lines on an interaction plot mean the effect of one factor does not change across levels of the other no interaction.","DNA: Apply-level plot interpretation (V.A.2). A inverts the rule (non-parallel = interaction); C confuses interaction with main effects; D introduces an irrelevant design concern.","Reference: DOE graphs and plots / interaction effects discussion, Figure 19.1 area, ~p. 351–353."]},{"id":"c19q8","ch":19,"n":8,"q":"A single factor produces an average response of 6.75 at its low setting and 4.25 at its high setting. Calculated as (high-level average − low-level average), what is the main effect of this factor?","o":["+2.50","−2.50","+5.50","−1.25"],"a":1,"x":["Main effect = high average − low average = 4.25 − 6.75 = −2.50 (these are the chapter's actual Speed-factor averages).","DNA: Apply calculation. A (+2.50) is the signature trap — subtracting in the wrong order (low − high). C (5.50) is the midpoint/average of the two values. D (−1.25) halves the effect, confusing the effect with a regression-style coefficient.","Reference: Main effects calculation (Speed factor), ~p. 351–352."]},{"id":"c19q9","ch":19,"n":9,"q":"A team has already completed several full-factorial experiments and determined that the factors do not interact significantly within the ranges being studied. According to the chapter, what does this finding justify?","o":["Abandoning further experimentation on these factors","Adopting a fractional factorial for further study, since there will be no significant confounding","Doubling the number of replications to be safe","Switching to a one-factor-at-a-time investigation"],"a":1,"x":["This restates the chapter's first paragraph on p. 350 almost verbatim: prior full-factorial work showing negligible interactions means no significant confounding, so a cheaper fractional design becomes appropriate.","DNA: \"Best answer\" scenario item; A and D are over-/under-reactions, C is plausible-but-wrong busywork.","Reference: Page 350, first paragraph (\"Suppose the team has completed a number of full-factorial designs…\")."]},{"id":"c19q10","ch":19,"n":10,"q":"An experimental design in which each level of every factor appears the same number of times in combination with each level of every other factor is described as:","o":["Saturated","Confounded","Balanced","Randomized"],"a":2,"x":["This is the chapter's exact definition of a balanced design.","DNA: A (Saturated) and B (Confounded) are real fractional-design terms used as close-difference lures; D (Randomized) is a principle, not a balance property.","Reference: Balanced design definition (near Tables 19.3–19.4), ~p. 349."]},{"id":"c19q11","ch":19,"n":11,"q":"Which statistical method does the chapter identify as the appropriate technique for assessing the significance of the effects produced by a designed experiment?","o":["Process capability analysis","Analysis of variance (ANOVA)","Pareto analysis","Gauge repeatability and reproducibility (GR&R)"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter explicitly names ANOVA as the appropriate method for assessing the significance of experimental effects.","DNA: All distractors are legitimate Six Sigma tools from other phases (capability, Pareto, GR&R) — the \"right tool, wrong job\" trap.","Reference: Two-Level Fractional Factorial Experiment Conclusions section; Table 19.8 discussion, ~p. 358–359."]},{"id":"c19q12","ch":19,"n":12,"q":"When ANOVA is used to evaluate a designed experiment, under which condition is the null hypothesis rejected at the chosen alpha level?","o":["When the calculated F-value is less than the critical value","When the calculated F-value is exactly equal to 1","When the calculated F-value is greater than the critical value","When the calculated F-value is negative"],"a":2,"x":["Reject H₀ when calculated F exceeds the critical (table) value at the chosen alpha.","DNA: A reverses the rule (a frequent trap); B (F = 1) describes the no-difference case; D is nonsensical since F cannot be negative — the kind of \"obviously eliminable\" option ASQ includes to reward quick elimination.","Reference: Experiment Conclusions section (F-value vs. critical value logic), ~p. 358–359."]},{"id":"c19q13","ch":19,"n":13,"q":"The foundational development of the design of experiments methodology is credited to which individual?","o":["Walter Shewhart","W. Edwards Deming","Genichi Taguchi","Ronald Fisher"],"a":3,"x":["The chapter credits Ronald Fisher with first developing DOE.","DNA: Mirrors the sample bank's heavy \"quality-pioneer attribution\" pattern. C (Taguchi) is the strongest distractor — he is associated with the statistical design of experiments and the loss function, so an unprepared candidate may misattribute the founding of DOE to him. Shewhart (control charts) and Deming (PDCA/14 Points) are wrong-domain lures. (Note: ignore the chapter's \"1920s and 1980s\" phrasing — the \"1980s\" is an OCR/text error; Fisher's work was 1920s–30s.)","Reference: Chapter introduction / history of DOE, ~p. 342."]},{"id":"c19q14","ch":19,"n":14,"q":"An engineer observes that raising temperature increases yield when pressure is held high, but the same temperature increase decreases yield when pressure is held low. This dependence of one factor's effect on the level of another factor is best described as:","o":["A main effect","An interaction","Replication","A blocking effect"],"a":1,"x":["When one factor's effect reverses or changes depending on another factor's level, that is an interaction.","DNA: Scenario item. A (Main effect) is the tempting wrong answer because temperature does have an effect — but the dependency on pressure is the defining feature of interaction, not main effect. C and D are unrelated terms.","Reference: Interaction effects section (additive vs. interaction), ~p. 352–354."]},{"id":"c19q15","ch":19,"n":15,"q":"A seven-factor screening experiment produces the following calculated effects: A = 13, B = 0, C = 20, D = 5, E = 0, F = 0, G = 23. Which two factors should the team prioritize as the largest contributors to the response?","o":["A and D","B and E","C and G","D and G"],"a":2,"x":["C (20) and G (23) are the two largest effects, so they are the priority contributors — exactly the conclusion of the seven-factor software example.","DNA: Apply-level data interpretation from a \"complex scenario.\" D (D and G) lures anyone who grabs G correctly but picks the wrong second factor; A and B select small or zero effects, testing whether the candidate can read magnitude.","Reference: Seven-factor screening example, Step 3 analysis and Table 19.8 (\"focus on C and G, the largest contributors\"), ~p. 358."]},{"id":"c19q16","ch":19,"n":16,"q":"A team must run an experiment across two different raw-material lots. They are not interested in the lot effect itself — they only want to prevent differences between lots from contaminating the estimates of their studied factors. Which DOE technique addresses this?","o":["Replication","Randomization","Blocking","Confounding"],"a":2,"x":["Blocking isolates a known, uninteresting nuisance source (here, lot) so its variation doesn't bias the factor estimates.","DNA: Replication and randomization are real principles but address different goals; confounding is a problem, not a control technique — the \"right term, wrong purpose\" trap.","Reference: Basic Terms section (blocking), ~p. 343."]},{"id":"c19q17","ch":19,"n":17,"q":"In a factorial experiment, a specific combination of factor levels that is applied together in a single experimental run is called a:","o":["Block","Treatment","Response","Replicate"],"a":1,"x":["A treatment is one specific combination of factor levels applied in a run.","DNA: D (Replicate) is the strongest lure — a replicate is a repeat of a treatment, not the combination itself. A and C are wrong-slot terms.","Reference: Basic Terms section (treatments), ~p. 343."]},{"id":"c19q18","ch":19,"n":18,"q":"An experimenter studies temperature at settings of 180°F and 220°F. Within the language of DOE, the 180°F and 220°F settings are the factor's:","o":["Responses","Levels","Treatments","Effects"],"a":1,"x":["The specific values a factor is set to (180°F, 220°F) are its levels.","DNA: D (Effects) tempts because temperature produces an effect — but the settings themselves are levels. Correct-definition-of-wrong-concept pattern.","Reference: Basic Terms section (factors and levels), ~p. 343."]},{"id":"c19q19","ch":19,"n":19,"q":"An experiment investigates how feed rate and spindle speed affect the surface finish of a machined part. The measured surface finish is the:","o":["Factor","Level","Response","Block"],"a":2,"x":["The measured output (surface finish) is the response.","DNA: A (Factor) is the lure for anyone who reverses inputs and outputs — the single most common DOE-vocabulary slip.","Reference: Basic Terms section (responses), ~p. 343."]},{"id":"c19q20","ch":19,"n":20,"q":"In a designed experiment, the portion of variation in the response that cannot be attributed to the studied factors or their interactions is referred to as:","o":["An effect","Experimental error","A treatment","A replicate"],"a":1,"x":["Unexplained variation = experimental error.","DNA: A (Effect) and C (Treatment) are real DOE terms placed where they don't fit; D confuses a method (replication) with a quantity (error).","Reference: Basic Terms section (errors), ~p. 343–344."]},{"id":"c19q21","ch":19,"n":21,"q":"Using the sign-multiplication method, the runs assigned \"+\" in an interaction column average 6.25, and the runs assigned \"−\" average 4.75. What is the value of this interaction effect?","o":["0","1.50","5.50","11.00"],"a":1,"x":["Interaction effect = (+ average) − (− average) = 6.25 − 4.75 = 1.50 (the chapter's F×C value).","DNA: Apply calculation. A (0) is the \"no-interaction\" trap; C (5.50) is the midpoint of the two averages; D (11.00) is their sum.","Reference: Interaction effects calculation (F×C), ~p. 352–354."]},{"id":"c19q22","ch":19,"n":22,"q":"On an interaction plot, the two lines cross each other sharply rather than running parallel. This pattern most strongly indicates:","o":["No interaction between the factors","A strong interaction between the factors","A perfectly balanced design","Zero experimental error"],"a":1,"x":["Crossing (non-parallel) lines are the strongest visual signature of an interaction.","DNA: Apply-level plot reading; A inverts the rule (the parallel-lines case from the first set), testing whether the candidate confuses the two patterns.","Reference: DOE graphs and plots / interaction effects, ~p. 351–353."]},{"id":"c19q23","ch":19,"n":23,"q":"A full factorial design investigates 4 factors, each at 2 levels. How many runs does it require?","o":["8","12","16","32"],"a":2,"x":["2⁴ = 16 runs.","DNA: Apply. D (32) is 2⁵ (miscounting factors); A (8) is 2³ (dropping a factor); B (12) is 4×3 arithmetic confusion.","Reference: Full factorial run-count logic (2^k), ~p. 347–348."]},{"id":"c19q24","ch":19,"n":24,"q":"A team studies 7 factors in only 8 runs, leaving no degrees of freedom available to independently estimate experimental error. This design is best described as:","o":["Balanced","Saturated","Replicated","Blocked"],"a":1,"x":["A design that uses up all degrees of freedom on factors, leaving none for error, is saturated.","DNA: A (Balanced) is the close-difference lure — the design is balanced, but that's not what \"no df for error\" describes. Tests precise term selection.","Reference: Seven-factor screening example, ~p. 356–358."]},{"id":"c19q25","ch":19,"n":25,"q":"During a long experiment, tool wear gradually increases as the day progresses. Running the treatment combinations in random order, rather than in a fixed sequence, primarily protects against:","o":["Confounding the factor effects with the time-related tool-wear trend","The need to replicate any runs","Having too few factor levels","Producing a balanced design"],"a":0,"x":["Randomization spreads a time-based trend (tool wear) across treatments so it doesn't systematically align with — and thus confound — any factor.","DNA: Scenario stem; B, C, D attach randomization to unrelated benefits, rewarding candidates who know its specific purpose.","Reference: Basic Terms section (randomization), ~p. 343."]},{"id":"c19q26","ch":19,"n":26,"q":"Beyond strengthening the conclusions, genuine replication of experimental runs allows the experimenter to estimate which of the following?","o":["The number of factors","Experimental error","The grand mean only","The confounding pattern"],"a":1,"x":["Replication (resetting and re-running) yields an independent estimate of experimental (pure) error.","DNA: The contrast with repetition from the first set is implicit; C and D attach replication to the wrong outputs.","Reference: Basic Terms section (replication), ~p. 343–344."]},{"id":"c19q27","ch":19,"n":27,"q":"Compared with a factorial experiment, the principal weakness of changing only one factor at a time (OFAT) is that it:","o":["Requires more replication","Cannot detect interactions between factors","Always confounds the main effects","Cannot estimate the grand mean"],"a":1,"x":["OFAT cannot reveal interactions because it never varies factors together — the core rationale for factorial designs.","DNA: A, C, D are plausible-sounding but wrong; the interaction-blindness point is the chapter's stated motivation for DOE.","Reference: DOE rationale / introduction (advantage over one-factor-at-a-time), ~p. 342."]},{"id":"c19q28","ch":19,"n":28,"q":"An engineer suspects that only a few of ten candidate factors actually influence the output and wants to identify them quickly using as few runs as possible. The most appropriate approach is a:","o":["Full factorial design","Fractional factorial screening design","Single replicated run","One-factor-at-a-time study"],"a":1,"x":["Many factors + few runs = a fractional factorial screening design.","DNA: A (full factorial) is the over-built trap; D (OFAT) is slow and interaction-blind; C is insufficient. Best-answer scenario.","Reference: Fractional factorial / screening discussion, ~p. 356."]},{"id":"c19q29","ch":19,"n":29,"q":"In a four-run, two-level experiment, a factor's two \"+\" runs score 88 and 84, and its two \"−\" runs score 58 and 67. Calculated as the difference between the level averages, what is this factor's main effect?","o":["5.0","11.75","23.5","47.0"],"a":2,"x":["Four-run design 2 runs per level divide by 2: [(88+84) − (58+67)] ÷ 2 = 47 ÷ 2 = 23.5 (the chapter's cooking-method effect).","DNA: Apply. D (47.0) forgets to divide; B (11.75) divides by 4 (using the 8-run rule by mistake — a deliberate cross-example trap); A (5.0) is unrelated.","Reference: Fractional factorial cooking/marinade example, ~p. 356."]},{"id":"c19q30","ch":19,"n":30,"q":"In an eight-run, two-level design, the difference between the sum of a factor's \"+\" responses and the sum of its \"−\" responses is 80. What is the factor's effect?","o":["80","40","20","10"],"a":2,"x":["Eight-run design 4 runs per level divide the difference by 4: 80 ÷ 4 = 20 (factor C).","DNA: Apply. B (40) divides by 2 (wrong design size); A (80) forgets to divide; D (10) divides by 8.","Reference: Seven-factor example, Step 3 (\"divide by 4 to find the effect\"), ~p. 358."]},{"id":"c19q31","ch":19,"n":31,"q":"Which statement about the factors that can be studied in a designed experiment is correct?","o":["Factors must be continuous (quantitative) only","Factors must be categorical (qualitative) only","Factors may be either quantitative or qualitative","Factors must always be measured on a ratio scale"],"a":2,"x":["The chapter explicitly states the studied factors can be variable-quantitative or attribute-qualitative.","DNA: A and B are the over-restrictive traps; D invents a false requirement.","Reference: Seven-factor example, Step 1 (\"can be both variable-quantitative or attribute-qualitative\"), ~p. 357."]},{"id":"c19q32","ch":19,"n":32,"q":"A green belt uses a fractional design in which factor C is aliased with the A×B interaction. Under which circumstance can the green belt still trust the estimate of C's main effect?","o":["When the A×B interaction is known to be negligible","When the design is deliberately unbalanced","When the run order is not randomized","Only after C has been replicated at least ten times"],"a":0,"x":["Confounding only damages the estimate if the aliased interaction is real; if A×B is negligible, the C column reflects C alone. This is the page-350 logic applied.","DNA: Scenario stem; B, C, D attach trust to irrelevant or counterproductive conditions.","Reference: Page 350, first paragraph + \"Effects and Confounding,\" ~p. 349–350."]},{"id":"c19q33","ch":19,"n":33,"q":"In the ANOVA used to evaluate a designed experiment, the F-ratio compares:","o":["The grand mean to zero","The between-groups (treatment) variation to the within-groups (error) variation","The number of factors to the number of levels","The high level to the low level of a single factor"],"a":1,"x":["The F-ratio is between-groups (treatment/signal) variation divided by within-groups (error/noise) variation.","DNA: D is the lure for those who confuse ANOVA with a single main-effect comparison; A and C are nonsensical-but-plausible to the unprepared, rewarding quick elimination.","Reference: ANOVA discussion / Table 19.8 and the MSS_B / MSS_W formulas, ~p. 358–359."]},{"id":"c20q1","ch":20,"n":1,"q":"A Six Sigma team has just developed a solution concept and wants to demonstrate to stakeholders that it is feasible and worth the resources before building anything detailed. Which technique are they using?","o":["Pilot test","Try-storming simulation","Proof of concept","Control plan"],"a":2,"x":["PoC = early-stage feasibility demonstration to elicit stakeholder feedback/commitment before detailed build-out.","DNA mimicry: Pilot (A) and try-storming (B) are correct techniques placed in the wrong slot — the classic ASQ \"right concept, wrong role\" distractor.","Reference: \"Proof of Concept: Ideation Toward Feasibility\" section; opening bullet list definition."]},{"id":"c20q2","ch":20,"n":2,"q":"Which of the following is not listed as something a proof of concept is used to demonstrate?","o":["Suitability of the proposed solution to the problem statement","Foundational justification for acquiring resources and securing commitments","That the solution will perform under full-scale, continuous production load","Potential fulfillment of the needs analysis within business costs and constraints"],"a":2,"x":["The four PoC bullets cover suitability, needs/cost fulfillment, success criteria, and resource/schedule justification. Full-scale continuous-load performance is a pilot/Control -phase concern, not PoC.","DNA mimicry: Negative stem with one out-of-phase distractor among three verbatim-correct ones.","Reference: \"Proof of Concept: Ideation Toward Feasibility\" — four-bullet list."]},{"id":"c20q3","ch":20,"n":3,"q":"Maria runs a brainstorming session, then immediately has the team build a rough prototype and role-play the revised process to refine it. Which technique is Maria applying?","o":["Proof of concept","Try-storming","Pilot test","Poka-yoke"],"a":1,"x":["Try-storming = brainstorming whose feasible ideas are immediately simulated/prototyped/role-played.","DNA mimicry: Poka-yoke (D) is a real lean term inserted as noise.","Reference: \"Try-storming: Rapid Actions from Brainstorming.\""]},{"id":"c20q4","ch":20,"n":4,"q":"According to the chapter, in which stage of the project are pilot tests performed?","o":["The earliest stage, before any concept is validated","The latter stages, prior to broader implementation","Concurrently with the project charter","Only after full deployment is complete"],"a":1,"x":["Chapter explicitly: pilot tests are \"performed in the latter stages of the project... to determine readiness for broader implementation.\"","DNA mimicry: Sequence/timing recall with tempting \"earliest stage\" distractor.","Reference: Opening bullet list, Pilot Tests definition."]},{"id":"c20q5","ch":20,"n":5,"q":"A team tests a process change during off-peak periods with reduced scope and tight control. Which purpose is this least aligned with?","o":["Verifying the effectiveness of the solution","Validating the implementation plan","Eliciting early feasibility feedback to justify acquiring resources","Identifying unintended risks or side effects"],"a":2,"x":["Off-peak, reduced-scope, tight-control testing = pilot. Its purposes are verify/validate/deploy-readiness/confirm results/identify risks. Eliciting early feasibility feedback to justify resources is a PoC purpose, not a pilot purpose.","DNA mimicry: \"least aligned\" framing + a PoC purpose smuggled into a pilot context.","Reference: \"Pilot Tests: Readiness for Deployment\" — five-bullet purpose list."]},{"id":"c20q6","ch":20,"n":6,"q":"In the chapter's integrated \"gates\" model, what role does the try-storming gate serve?","o":["It justifies the resources needed to start the project","It acts as a filter to quickly vet and discard approaches that are not feasible","It confirms readiness for organization-wide deployment","It documents and monitors the controlled process"],"a":1,"x":["Verbatim: try-storming gate \"operates as a filter to quickly vet and discard approaches that are not feasible for further development.\"","DNA mimicry: A = PoC role, C = pilot/deployment role, D = Control-phase role.","Reference: \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches.\""]},{"id":"c20q7","ch":20,"n":7,"q":"A project automates subscription renewals via auto-charging the customer's credit card. It fixes missed payments but removes the personal contact that enabled upselling. According to the chapter, this scenario best illustrates the value of which technique?","o":["Proof of concept, for demonstrating feasibility","Try-storming, for incremental idea-building","Pilot test, for surfacing unintended side effects before full rollout","Benchmarking, for comparing against best-in-class"],"a":2,"x":["The credit-card example sits in the Pilot Tests section illustrating \"identifies any unintended risks or side effects\" — fixing missed payments but introducing a lost-upsell problem.","DNA mimicry: scenario stem with all options as real techniques; correct one matches the section the example lives in.","Reference: \"Pilot Tests: Readiness for Deployment\" — closing example."]},{"id":"c20q8","ch":20,"n":8,"q":"Which sequence correctly orders the three implementation-planning techniques as presented in the chapter's serial model?","o":["Pilot test proof of concept try-storming","Try-storming proof of concept pilot test","Proof of concept try-storming pilot test","Proof of concept pilot test try-storming"],"a":2,"x":["Serial order = PoC try-storming pilot.","DNA mimicry: permutation distractors; D swaps the last two (the most tempting error).","Reference: \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches\" — serial sequence description."]},{"id":"c20q9","ch":20,"n":9,"q":"A green belt observes that weeks and months have elapsed between the proof of concept and the try-storming step. According to the chapter, what does this most likely suggest?","o":["The ideas generated are lacking in substance","Slippage or even abandonment of the project","The pilot test gate was too restrictive","The solution is ready for broader implementation"],"a":1,"x":["Verbatim: long gaps between PoC and try-storming \"suggest a slippage or even abandonment.\" Trap note: \"ideas could be lacking\" (A) is the diagnosis for the opposite problem (rushing to pilot) — see Q10. ASQ frequently pairs the two halves of a sentence as competing distractors.","Reference: \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches\" — final paragraph."]},{"id":"c20q10","ch":20,"n":10,"q":"A team rushes rapidly from concept straight to pilot testing without adequate try-storming. According to the chapter, what does this most likely suggest?","o":["The project is highly efficient and should be replicated","Ideas could be lacking","The proof of concept gate failed","Excessive resources were consumed"],"a":1,"x":["Verbatim: \"rapid progression to pilot tests without adequate try-storming suggests that ideas could be lacking.\" Direct mirror-image of Q9.","Reference: \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches\" — final paragraph."]},{"id":"c20q11","ch":20,"n":11,"q":"Which technique is explicitly described as effective for training Six Sigma participants and for rapidly adjusting internal website user interfaces?","o":["Pilot test","Proof of concept","Try-storming","Spaghetti diagram"],"a":2,"x":["Try-storming is named as \"a very effective tool or technique for training Six Sigma participants\" and is used to rapidly adjust internal website user interfaces.","DNA mimicry: spaghetti diagram (D) as a plausible-sounding lean distractor.","Reference: \"Try-storming: Rapid Actions from Brainstorming.\""]},{"id":"c20q12","ch":20,"n":12,"q":"The chapter states that the pilot test gate is based on control of what?","o":["Cost and schedule","Scope and scale","Severity and detection","Resources and personnel"],"a":1,"x":["Verbatim: \"The gate of pilot tests is based on control of scope and scale.\"","DNA mimicry: C = FMEA terms (severity/detection); D = project-risk terms — both real, both wrong here.","Reference: \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches.\""]},{"id":"c20q13","ch":20,"n":13,"q":"Which of the following is not a stated purpose of a pilot test?","o":["Establishing that participants and stakeholders can deploy the change","Confirming the solution will provide the desired results when properly implemented","Eliciting the foundational rationale for acquiring resources and securing commitments","Validating the implementation plan"],"a":2,"x":["A, B, D are listed pilot purposes. C is a PoC purpose (foundational justification for resources/commitments).","DNA mimicry: negative stem with a cross-technique purpose as the answer.","Reference: Pilot purposes — \"Pilot Tests\" five-bullet list; PoC bullet for the distractor."]},{"id":"c20q14","ch":20,"n":14,"q":"A team needs to \"sample\" a process-change solution before committing to it organization-wide, and wants the ability to roll back changes if substantial risks appear. Which technique fits, and why?","o":["Proof of concept — because nothing is deployed, so risk is zero","Pilot test — because reduced scope keeps rollback measures within bounds","Try-storming — because prototypes are disposable","Control plan — because it monitors the process after the fact"],"a":1,"x":["\"Sample\" the solution, reduced scope, rollback within bounds = pilot. The stem language (\"sample,\" rollback \"within bounds\") is lifted from the pilot section.","DNA mimicry: full-sentence \"technique + rationale\" options where the rationale must also be correct; A's rationale (\"risk is zero\") is a plausible-but-wrong justification.","Reference: \"Pilot Tests\" opening + \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches\" (rollback \"relatively within bounds\")."]},{"id":"c20q15","ch":20,"n":15,"q":"How does the chapter characterize the relationship between proof of concept, try-storming, and pilot tests?","o":["They must always be performed in a strict serial order with no overlap","They are shown serially but can also be concurrent and interdependent","They are interchangeable terms for the same activity","Only one of the three may be used per project"],"a":1,"x":["Verbatim: the timing/sequence is \"shown in a serial manner, but they can also be concurrent and interdependent.\"","DNA mimicry: A is the over-literal trap (the same one I flagged you on earlier — the \"sequential gates\" model is the author's framing, not a rigid rule).","Reference: \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches\" — opening sentence."]},{"id":"c20q16","ch":20,"n":16,"q":"Devon's team has a validated concept and several brainstormed solution ideas, but hasn't yet built anything testable. Which technique comes next in the chapter's serial model?","o":["Pilot test","Proof of concept","Try-storming","Control plan"],"a":2,"x":["Serial order is PoC try-storming pilot; with concept validated and ideas in hand but nothing built, try-storming (prototype/simulate) is next. Distractor A skips a gate; D is Control-phase.","Reference: \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches\" — serial sequence."]},{"id":"c20q17","ch":20,"n":17,"q":"Which technique is described as an \"early-stage design validation technique\"?","o":["Try-storming","Pilot test","Proof of concept","Poka-yoke"],"a":2,"x":["Verbatim: PoC is \"an early-stage design validation technique.\" Try-storming (A) and pilot (B) are the cross-technique distractors; poka-yoke (D) is lean noise.","Reference: Opening bullet list, PoC definition."]},{"id":"c20q18","ch":20,"n":18,"q":"According to the chapter, try-storming extends which traditional quality practice?","o":["Benchmarking","Brainstorming","Value stream mapping","Auditing"],"a":1,"x":["\"Try-storming simulations... extends traditional brainstorming by incorporating a simulated or prototype solution.\"","Reference: Opening bullet list; \"Try-storming\" section."]},{"id":"c20q19","ch":20,"n":19,"q":"A pilot test reveals substantial risks and deficiencies. According to the chapter, why is this an acceptable outcome of the pilot gate?","o":["Because the proof of concept already guaranteed feasibility","Because the measures to control and roll back changes remain relatively within bounds","Because try-storming will fix the deficiencies afterward","Because pilot tests cannot introduce new risks"],"a":1,"x":["\"If the pilot test reveals substantial risks and deficiencies, the measures taken to control and roll back changes are relatively within bounds.\" This is the whole point of controlled scope/scale.","Reference: \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches.\""]},{"id":"c20q20","ch":20,"n":20,"q":"The purpose of try-storming is best described as which of the following?","o":["Confirmatory — proving the final solution works at scale","Exploratory — incrementally building the concept toward completion","Custodial — sustaining gains after deployment","Diagnostic — finding the root cause of a defect"],"a":1,"x":["Try-storming's \"purpose is exploratory in pursuit of incrementally building the concept toward completion.\" Distractor A describes a pilot's confirmatory nature — the classic right-concept/wrong-tool trap.","Reference: Opening bullet list, try-storming definition."]},{"id":"c20q21","ch":20,"n":21,"q":"Which of the following is not a technique covered under BoK V.B implementation planning?","o":["Proof of concept","Try-storming simulation","Pilot test","Gauge R&R study"],"a":3,"x":["V.B lists PoC, try-storming, pilot tests. Gauge R&R is Measure phase (MSA). Negative stem with an out-of-phase but real tool.","Reference: Chapter scope line (BoK V.B); cross-check 2022 BoK §III.E for GR&R placement."]},{"id":"c20q22","ch":20,"n":22,"q":"A company applies information technology changes to how it receives and processes information, rapidly adjusting internal website interfaces to elicit more permanent solutions. Which technique does the chapter associate with this?","o":["Pilot test","Try-storming","Proof of concept","Total productive maintenance"],"a":1,"x":["Try-storming \"is also used when information technology is applied to adjust or modify how a company receives, processes, and manages information. The user interfaces of internal websites can be rapidly adjusted.\"","Reference: \"Try-storming: Rapid Actions from Brainstorming.\""]},{"id":"c20q23","ch":20,"n":23,"q":"What is the stated logic behind conducting a pilot test?","o":["To demonstrate feasibility before committing resources","That prior to completion, there are opportunities to \"sample\" the solution","To discard non-feasible approaches early","To document the process for long-term control"],"a":1,"x":["\"The logic behind pilot tests is that prior to the completion, there are opportunities to 'sample' the solution.\" A = PoC logic, C = try-storming logic, D = Control — all real, all misplaced.","Reference: \"Pilot Tests: Readiness for Deployment.\""]},{"id":"c20q24","ch":20,"n":24,"q":"In the integrated model, the proof of concept ideally represents what?","o":["A filter for discarding infeasible ideas","A project milestone or \"gate\" enabling broader commitments","The final readiness check before rollout","A rollback control mechanism"],"a":1,"x":["\"Ideally, the proof of concept should represent a project milestone or 'gate,' enabling broader commitments.\" A = try-storming's role; C/D = pilot's roles.","Reference: \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches.\""]},{"id":"c20q25","ch":20,"n":25,"q":"Which pair correctly matches a technique to its primary purpose as stated in the chapter?","o":["Proof of concept identifying unintended side effects","Try-storming confirming readiness for broader implementation","Pilot test validating the implementation plan","Proof of concept discarding infeasible approaches"],"a":2,"x":["Only C is correctly matched: validating the implementation plan is a stated pilot purpose. A, B, D each swap a technique with another's purpose — the dominant ASQ distractor mechanic.","Reference: Pilot five-bullet list; try-storming and PoC definitions."]},{"id":"c20q26","ch":20,"n":26,"q":"After a proof of concept establishes its foundation, what does the chapter say happens next?","o":["The solution is immediately deployed organization-wide","The ideation can be manifested into more specific and detailed solutions and completion items","The control plan is finalized","The project charter is rewritten"],"a":1,"x":["\"After determining this foundation, the ideation can be manifested into more specific and detailed solutions and completion items.\" Distractor A is the over-eager \"deploy now\" trap.","Reference: \"Proof of Concept: Ideation Toward Feasibility\" — closing sentence."]},{"id":"c20q27","ch":20,"n":27,"q":"A green belt wants to measure project efficiency during implementation planning. According to the chapter, efficiency can be measured by what?","o":["Defects per million opportunities between steps","Time and resources between implementation steps","The risk priority number at each gate","Customer satisfaction scores per phase"],"a":1,"x":["\"The efficiency of a project can be measured by time and resources between implementation steps.\" A inserts a DPMO red herring.","Reference: \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches\" — final paragraph."]},{"id":"c20q28","ch":20,"n":28,"q":"Which technique is explicitly tied to \"presenting it to participants and stakeholders for the purpose of eliciting feedback and detailed inputs\"?","o":["Pilot test","Try-storming","Proof of concept","Kaizen blitz"],"a":2,"x":["PoC \"presents it to the participants and stakeholders for the purpose of eliciting feedback and detailed inputs.\"","Reference: Opening bullet list, PoC definition."]},{"id":"c20q29","ch":20,"n":29,"q":"Patel's team built a quick prototype straight after brainstorming and let participants add their own incremental creative contributions during a demonstration. This \"incremental creative contribution\" characteristic is the hallmark of which technique?","o":["Proof of concept","Pilot test","Try-storming","Benchmarking"],"a":2,"x":["Try-storming's demonstration \"enables participants to apply their own incremental creative contributions.\" This incremental/creative language is unique to try-storming in the chapter.","Reference: \"Try-storming: Rapid Actions from Brainstorming.\""]},{"id":"c20q30","ch":20,"n":30,"q":"If the pilot test gate does not restrict problematic solutions, what does the chapter warn will happen?","o":["The proof of concept must be repeated","The benefits of the project will be offset by new risks, defects, and maladies from suboptimal implementation","Try-storming will need to restart","The project will automatically be abandoned"],"a":1,"x":["\"If the pilot test gate does not restrict problematic solutions, then the benefits of the Six Sigma project will be offset by the new risks, defects, and maladies introduced from the suboptimal implementation approach.\" D is the over-literal trap — the text says offset, not automatically abandoned.","Reference: \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches.\""]},{"id":"c20q31","ch":20,"n":31,"q":"Which statement about the timing of the three techniques is most accurate per the chapter?","o":["They are strictly serial and can never overlap","They are shown serially but can be concurrent and interdependent","Pilot tests always precede proof of concept","Try-storming is optional and never gated"],"a":1,"x":["\"The timing and sequence... are shown in a serial manner, but they can also be concurrent and interdependent.\" A is the rigid-sequence distractor (recall: the \"sequential gates\" framing is the author's pedagogy, not Six Sigma law).","Reference: \"Implementation Planning with Integrated Approaches\" — opening sentence."]},{"id":"c20q32","ch":20,"n":32,"q":"A pilot test for a process change is recommended to be conducted under which condition to limit disruption?","o":["During peak demand to stress-test the solution","During off-peak periods with reduced scope and tight control","Across all sites simultaneously","Only after the control plan is signed off"],"a":1,"x":["\"If this is a process change, the implementation could be tested during off-peak periods\" with \"reduced scope and tight control.\" A inverts the intent.","Reference: \"Pilot Tests: Readiness for Deployment.\""]},{"id":"c20q33","ch":20,"n":33,"q":"Of proof of concept, try-storming, and pilot tests, which is performed latest in the project lifecycle?","o":["Proof of concept","Try-storming","Pilot test","They are always simultaneous"],"a":2,"x":["Pilot tests are performed \"in the latter stages of the project,\" after PoC (early) and try-storming (middle).","Reference: Opening bullet list, pilot definition; serial-sequence paragraph."]},{"id":"c21q1","ch":21,"n":1,"q":"A production manager schedules output based on a six-month sales forecast and moves completed subassemblies into a staging warehouse regardless of downstream consumption. Which characteristic best identifies this system?","o":["It is a pull system because material moves toward the customer","It is a push system, which tends to generate excess inventory","It is a kanban system, since material is staged for replenishment","It is a takt-driven system aligned to customer demand"],"a":1,"x":["Forecast-driven scheduling with material staged downstream regardless of consumption is the textbook definition of a push system, which accumulates excess inventory. The DNA trap is A and D, which dress push up as \"demand-driven\" or \"moving toward the customer\"; C swaps in the mechanism (kanban) for the system type.","Reference: \"Pull Systems\" section, lines 130–133 (\"Unlike traditional systems where products are stocked in inventory and 'pushed'…\")."]},{"id":"c21q2","ch":21,"n":2,"q":"A stable work cell carries an average of 90 units of WIP and completes 15 units per hour. Using the relationship pull systems rely on, what is the average lead time?","o":["6 hours","1,350 hours","0.17 hours","105 hours"],"a":0,"x":["Little's Law: Lead Time = WIP ÷ Throughput = 90 ÷ 15 = 6 hours. B multiplies (inversion trap, wrong units), C inverts the ratio, D adds — all classic calculation distractors.","Reference: \"Pull Systems,\" lines 135–138 (Little's law relating lead time, throughput, and WIP)."]},{"id":"c21q3","ch":21,"n":3,"q":"Which statement about kanban is most accurate according to the chapter?","o":["Kanban is the overall scheduling philosophy that replaces forecasting","Kanban is a signal that also ensures no defective pieces are passed downstream","Kanban eliminates the need for any work-in-process inventory","Kanban is a measure of customer demand used to pace the line"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter explicitly states kanban is a signal and that it \"also ensures that no defective pieces are allowed to pass or be transferred.\" A defines the pull philosophy; C is an absolutism trap (reduces, not eliminates, WIP); D is the definition of takt time.","Reference: \"Examples of Lean Tools,\" lines 115–118."]},{"id":"c21q4","ch":21,"n":4,"q":"A green belt installs a fixture so a part can physically be loaded in only one orientation, making misoriented stacking impossible. This is an example of:","o":["Standard work","Visual factory","Poka-yoke","Total productive maintenance"],"a":2,"x":["A physical design change that permits only the correct action is poka-yoke (the chapter's exact pallet-fixture example). Distractors A, B, D are adjacent lean tools — the \"correct definition of the wrong concept\" pattern.","Reference: \"Mistake-Proofing (Poka-Yoke),\" lines 157–160."]},{"id":"c21q5","ch":21,"n":5,"q":"Who is the originator of mistake-proofing, commemorated by a prize honoring excellence among lean practitioners?","o":["Eliyahu Goldratt","Taiichi Ohno","Shigeo Shingo","W. Edwards Deming"],"a":2,"x":["Shigeo Shingo originated poka-yoke; the Shingo Prize commemorates him. Goldratt (TOC), Deming (PDCA/14 Points), and Ohno are plausible pioneer-attribution distractors mirroring the sample-question DNA.","Reference: \"Mistake-Proofing,\" lines 177–179."]},{"id":"c21q6","ch":21,"n":6,"q":"During which 5S step would a team develop and implement standardized procedures to make the work environment more orderly?","o":["Sort (seiri)","Straighten (seiton)","Standardize (seiketsu)","Sustain (shitsuke)"],"a":2,"x":["Standardize (seiketsu) develops/implements standardized procedures. Note the trap vs. Sustain (D) — the chapter assigns procedure development to Standardize, while Sustain is repeated/continuous performance. This mirrors the sample bank's 5S Standardize-vs-Sustain split (Sample Q53).","Reference: \"5S,\" lines 197–201."]},{"id":"c21q7","ch":21,"n":7,"q":"An 8-hour shift provides 480 minutes of available work time. Customer demand requires 120 units per day. What is the takt time?","o":["0.25 minutes per unit","4 minutes per unit","40 minutes per unit","600 minutes per unit"],"a":1,"x":["Takt time = Available work time ÷ Customer demand = 480 ÷ 120 = 4 min/unit. C inverts the ratio direction conceptually; A and D are arithmetic-error distractors.","Reference: \"Takt Time Analysis,\" lines 529–540 (formula and the parallel 480-min worked example)."]},{"id":"c21q8","ch":21,"n":8,"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the three charts used to create standard work?","o":["Production capacity chart","Standardized work combination table","Standardized work analysis chart","Standardized control chart"],"a":3,"x":["The three standard-work charts are the production capacity chart, standardized work combination table, and standardized work analysis chart. \"Standardized control chart\" is a fabricated near-term blending standard work with SPC — the plausible-invented-option trap.","Reference: \"Standard Work,\" lines 323–324."]},{"id":"c21q9","ch":21,"n":9,"q":"A team is analyzing a process and identifies a step where an entire production lot waits while the previous lot is being processed, inspected, or moved. According to Shingo's flow elements, this is best classified as:","o":["Process delay","Lot delay","Transportation","Inspection"],"a":0,"x":["Shingo's process delay = an entire lot waits while the previous lot is processed/inspected/moved. The trap is B (lot delay), which is when one piece is processed while the rest of the same lot waits — a precise one-word distinction ASQ exploits.","Reference: \"Flow Analysis Charts,\" lines 467–474."]},{"id":"c21q10","ch":21,"n":10,"q":"Setup time, the key metric for SMED, is most precisely defined as:","o":["The total machine run time required to produce one batch","The time from the last good piece of one product to the first good piece of the next","The time an operator spends performing maintenance between shifts","The cycle time of the slowest process step in the line"],"a":1,"x":["Setup time is defined as last good piece of one product to first good piece of the next. A, C, D substitute run time, maintenance, and cycle time respectively — adjacent-metric traps.","Reference: \"Quick changeover/SMED\" discussion, lines 626–628."]},{"id":"c21q11","ch":21,"n":11,"q":"A registration office finds that its applications desk limits total throughput even when other counters are idle, and it threatens to become a bottleneck under heavier load. This resource is best described as:","o":["A buffer","A capacity-constrained resource (CCR)","A takt-time violation","A lot delay"],"a":1,"x":["A CCR limits throughput and risks becoming a bottleneck; critically, the chapter notes a CCR can affect processes even when not at full capacity (the idle-counter detail). The university applications-desk example is taken directly from the text.","Reference: \"Cycle Time Reduction Through Constraints Management,\" lines 684–692."]},{"id":"c21q12","ch":21,"n":12,"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes kaizen from kaikaku?","o":["Kaizen is revolutionary redesign; kaikaku is incremental improvement","Kaizen is evolutionary and incremental; kaikaku is revolutionary innovation","Both are management-initiated and identical in scope","Kaikaku is performed daily by operators; kaizen is led by executives"],"a":1,"x":["Kaizen = evolutionary/incremental; kaikaku = revolutionary innovation/redesign. A reverses the pair (the primary trap); C and D scramble scope and ownership.","Reference: \"Breakthrough Continuous Improvement (Kaikaku),\" lines 725–729."]},{"id":"c21q13","ch":21,"n":13,"q":"A cross-functional team receives lean training and, within a single week, completes a focused improvement project—collecting data and implementing recommendations immediately. This activity is best described as a:","o":["Gemba walk","Kaizen blitz","Value stream map","Hoshin plan"],"a":1,"x":["A trained cross-functional team completing a continuous-improvement project within a week is a kaizen blitz. Gemba walk, VSM, and Hoshin planning are real but distinct tools — adjacent-concept distractors.","Reference: \"Kaizen Blitz,\" lines 712–723."]},{"id":"c21q14","ch":21,"n":14,"q":"When creating a current-state value stream map, the chapter advises that mapping should begin where?","o":["At the supplier's supplier, working forward","At the shipping end of the value stream, closest to the customer","At the bottleneck identified through takt analysis","At the process step with the highest WIP"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter directs mappers to begin at the shipping end, closest to the customer, to keep customer focus. A inverts the start point (supplier side) — the most tempting trap since value streams are often described supplier-to-customer.","Reference: \"Current State\" (VSM), lines 490–494."]},{"id":"c21q15","ch":21,"n":15,"q":"As a rule of thumb, a new current-state value stream map should be created when approximately what proportion of the countermeasures has been implemented?","o":["20%","50%","80%","100%"],"a":2,"x":["The text's rule of thumb is ~80% of countermeasures implemented before remapping the current state. 20/50/100% are numeric distractors typical of the sample bank's quantitative-recall items.","Reference: \"Establish and Implement a Plan,\" lines 515–518."]},{"id":"c21q16","ch":21,"n":16,"q":"Lean and Six Sigma are described as complementary. Which pairing correctly matches each methodology's primary objective?","o":["Six Sigma improves process flow; lean reduces process variation","Six Sigma reduces process variation; lean improves process flow","Both methodologies primarily reduce process complexity","Six Sigma shifts the process average; lean reduces the process average"],"a":1,"x":["Figure 21.2 pairs Six Sigma with reducing process variation (and shifting the average) and lean with improving flow (and reducing complexity). A reverses the pair (primary trap); D and C scramble the attributes.","Reference: \"Lean Six Sigma objectives,\" lines 50–57 (Figure 21.2)."]},{"id":"c21q17","ch":21,"n":17,"q":"A prerequisite step is recommended before selecting whether to apply lean or Six Sigma techniques to uncover the problems. Which step is this?","o":["Process capability study","Value stream mapping","FMEA","Control plan development"],"a":1,"x":["The text states value stream mapping should be applied \"as a prerequisite\" to uncover problems before generating savings. Capability studies, FMEA, and control plans are real tools but not the named prerequisite — adjacent-tool traps.","Reference: \"Relevance of Lean to Six Sigma,\" lines 23–28."]},{"id":"c21q18","ch":21,"n":18,"q":"A maintenance program shifts a portion of routine equipment-care tasks to operators as part of their ongoing process activities, integrating engineering, operations, and maintenance. This describes:","o":["Standard work","Poka-yoke","Total productive maintenance","Visual factory"],"a":2,"x":["TPM integrates engineering, operations, and maintenance and shifts a portion of tasks to operators. Distractors are co-located lean tools (the \"correct definition of wrong concept\" pattern).","Reference: \"Total Productive Maintenance,\" lines 247–251."]},{"id":"c21q19","ch":21,"n":19,"q":"Which of the following is NOT listed as a value-added step in lean?","o":["The process step transforms the product","The customer recognizes the product's value","The product must be reworked","The job is done properly the first time"],"a":2,"x":["Rework is waste, never value-added — this mirrors Sample Q71 almost exactly. A, B, D are the three genuine value-added criteria, making them tempting in a NOT/except stem.","Reference: \"Examples of Lean Tools\" (value-added criteria) and the rework-as-waste framing throughout; cf. lines 100–103 and value-added discussion lines 430–436."]},{"id":"c21q20","ch":21,"n":20,"q":"A process capacity calculation uses available time per shift in the numerator. Which expression correctly forms the denominator?","o":["Total cycle time per piece only","Tool change time per piece only","Total cycle time per piece + tool change time per piece","Machine time per piece − manual time per piece"],"a":2,"x":["Capacity = Available time per shift ÷ (Total cycle time per piece + Tool change time per piece). A and B drop a denominator term; D fabricates a subtraction — classic formula-component distractors.","Reference: \"Process Capacity,\" lines 416–419."]},{"id":"c21q21","ch":21,"n":21,"q":"Countermeasures are typically identified to eliminate three conditions. Which trio is correct?","o":["Muda, mura, muri","Muda, kaizen, kaikaku","Mura, gemba, jidoka","Muri, heijunka, andon"],"a":0,"x":["Countermeasures target muda (waste), mura (unevenness), and muri (unreasonableness). The distractors mix in unrelated lean terms (kaizen, gemba, jidoka, heijunka, andon) that are valid concepts elsewhere — the adjacent-terminology trap.","Reference: \"Countermeasure Activities,\" lines 569–572."]},{"id":"c21q22","ch":21,"n":22,"q":"When developing a process based on understanding who receives or is affected by the outputs, which tool emphasizes serving internal customers in order to satisfy external customers?","o":["FMEA","SIPOC","Spaghetti diagram","Control chart"],"a":1,"x":["SIPOC highlights serving internal customers to satisfy external ones and identifies who receives process outputs. Spaghetti diagram (flow), FMEA (risk), and control chart (monitoring) are precise wrong-tool distractors.","Reference: \"SIPOC,\" lines 359–369."]},{"id":"c21q23","ch":21,"n":23,"q":"Process capability is best described in the chapter as a comparison of which two elements?","o":["Cycle time versus takt time","Voice of the customer versus voice of the process","WIP versus throughput","Internal setup versus external setup"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter defines process capability as a comparison of VOC with VOP. A is the takt/cycle relationship, C is Little's Law, D is SMED — each a real relationship attached to the wrong term.","Reference: \"Process Capability,\" lines 397–400."]},{"id":"c21q24","ch":21,"n":24,"q":"A facility manager states that long changeovers force large lot sizes to amortize setup costs, following a traditional formula. Which formula is being referenced?","o":["Little's law","Economic order quantity (EOQ)","Takt time","Risk priority number"],"a":1,"x":["EOQ is explicitly named as the traditional formula endorsing large lots to amortize long setups. Little's Law and takt time are quantitative distractors; RPN is from FMEA.","Reference: \"Cycle Time Reduction…\" / SMED discussion, lines 622–625."]},{"id":"c21q25","ch":21,"n":25,"q":"In an ideally level-loaded process, how should sequential process steps be arranged relative to takt time?","o":["Each downstream process should be slightly slower than the one preceding it","Each downstream process should be slightly faster than the one preceding it","All processes should run well above takt time to build buffer","Upstream and downstream times are irrelevant once takt is set"],"a":1,"x":["The ideal level-loaded state has each downstream process slightly faster than the one preceding it. A reverses the direction (primary trap); C contradicts the \"little or no waste\" goal; D dismisses sequencing entirely.","Reference: \"Takt Time Analysis\" (line balancing), lines 561–564."]},{"id":"c21q26","ch":21,"n":26,"q":"A kaizen team works across a full value stream, led by a project manager and sometimes mentored by consultants, addressing process, system, and organizational issues. Which level of kaizen is this?","o":["Point kaizen","Mini kaizen","Flow kaizen","Process kaizen"],"a":2,"x":["Flow kaizen spans the full value stream, is led by a project manager, and addresses process/system/organizational issues. Point (individual workstation), mini (work team area), and process (specific subprocess) kaizen are the scope-distinction distractors.","Reference: \"Organizing for Kaizen,\" lines 814–825."]},{"id":"c21q27","ch":21,"n":27,"q":"Which statement about the relationship between kaizen and kaikaku is accurate?","o":["Kaikaku improvements are self-sustaining and require no kaizen","Without kaizen, kaikaku improvements will deteriorate over time","Kaizen is typically initiated by management due to its broad scope","Kaikaku consists of small, low-risk daily modifications"],"a":1,"x":["The text states that without kaizen, kaikaku improvements deteriorate over time. A inverts that; C wrongly assigns management-initiation to kaizen (it belongs to kaikaku); D describes kaizen, not kaikaku.","Reference: \"Breakthrough Continuous Improvement (Kaikaku),\" lines 732–743."]},{"id":"c21q28","ch":21,"n":28,"q":"The German-origin term for \"pace\" or \"rhythm,\" likened to an orchestra conductor's beat, refers to which measure?","o":["Cycle time","Lead time","Takt time","Setup time"],"a":2,"x":["Takt is the German word for pace/rhythm (orchestra-conductor analogy). Cycle, lead, and setup times are the adjacent-metric traps in your known high-confusion zone.","Reference: \"Takt Time Analysis,\" lines 523–525."]},{"id":"c21q29","ch":21,"n":29,"q":"A green belt wants to identify bottlenecks by determining the capacity of machines and humans in a process. Which standard-work chart is the correct tool?","o":["Production capacity chart","Standardized work combination table","Standardized work analysis chart","Value stream map"],"a":0,"x":["The production capacity chart determines machine/human capacity and identifies bottlenecks. The work combination table analyzes value-added vs. non-value-added times; the work analysis chart aids layout/training — precise wrong-chart distractors.","Reference: \"Standard Work,\" lines 325–327."]},{"id":"c21q30","ch":21,"n":30,"q":"According to the chapter, approximately what percentage of total costs—along with the conditions for quality and delivery—is determined in the design planning stages?","o":["50 percent","65 percent","85 percent","95 percent"],"a":2,"x":["The chapter cites 85 percent of total costs (plus quality/delivery conditions) as determined in design planning, justifying upstream gemba kaizen. The other percentages are numeric-recall distractors.","Reference: \"Gemba kaizen\" discussion, lines 796–798."]},{"id":"c21q31","ch":21,"n":31,"q":"Which sequence correctly orders Shingo's three non-delay flow elements as defined in the flow analysis discussion?","o":["Processing, inspection, transportation","Transportation, processing, inspection","Inspection, transportation, processing","Processing, transportation, delay"],"a":0,"x":["Shingo's three non-delay flow elements are processing, inspection, transportation (then process delay and lot delay). Reordered options test exact sequence recall — a sample-bank pattern.","Reference: \"Flow Analysis Charts,\" lines 460–474."]},{"id":"c21q32","ch":21,"n":32,"q":"A team is reducing changeover time and identifies steps that can be performed while the prior job is still running, such as presetting tooling and gathering documentation. This effort targets which type of setup activity?","o":["Internal setup performed while the machine is stopped","External setup that can be done while the machine is running","Lot delay reduction","Process delay elimination"],"a":1,"x":["Steps performed while the machine still runs are external setup. The trap is A (internal setup, done while stopped) — the precise internal-vs-external SMED distinction. C and D pull in unrelated delay types.","Reference: SMED discussion, lines 643–646."]},{"id":"c21q33","ch":21,"n":33,"q":"A team has implemented a future-state value stream and now creates an action plan of countermeasures to reach the future-state goal. According to the chapter, countermeasures are best defined as:","o":["The metrics used to baseline the current state","Actions taken to reduce or eliminate the root causes of problems preventing goal achievement","The visual signals authorizing replenishment","The standardized work charts created for operator training"],"a":1,"x":["Countermeasures are actions to reduce/eliminate root causes preventing goal achievement. A (baseline metrics), C (kanban), and D (standard-work charts) are real concepts mapped to the wrong term — the signature ASQ distractor pattern.","Reference: \"Countermeasure Activities,\" lines 569–572."]},{"id":"c22q1","ch":22,"n":1,"q":"A plant maintains an elaborate wall of control charts that are updated only when an automotive customer visits and are never used to adjust the process. Which term best describes this practice?","o":["Statistical process control (SPC)","Statistical quality control (SQC)","Statistical process display (SPD)","Rational subgrouping"],"a":2,"x":["Display = statistics for show, never applied to run the process.","DNA: A/B are the legitimate cousins; the trap is conflating SPC/SQC with SPD.","Ref: \"Key Definitions,\" lines 27–38."]},{"id":"c22q2","ch":22,"n":2,"q":"A \"control process,\" as defined in SPC, is best described as:","o":["A documented procedure for charting defects on a continuous-flow line","A feedback loop that measures actual performance, compares it to a standard, and acts on the difference","The application of statistical techniques to reduce variation in a process","The use of 25 subgroups to establish trial control limits"],"a":1,"x":["Control process = measure compare to standard act on difference.","DNA: C is the verbatim SPC definition (correct term, wrong question); D is a setup step.","Ref: lines 25–26."]},{"id":"c22q3","ch":22,"n":3,"q":"Which statement about common cause variation is correct?","o":["It results from unusual events that an alerted operator can usually remove","It is inherent to the process and is generally not controllable by process operators","It is also referred to as assignable variation","Its presence means the process is out of statistical control"],"a":1,"x":["Common = inherent, natural, not operator-controllable, present in an in-control process.","DNA: A and C define special/assignable cause; D reverses the relationship (common-cause-only = in control).","Ref: \"Special vs. Common Causes,\" lines 134–148."]},{"id":"c22q4","ch":22,"n":4,"q":"An operator nudges a machine setting every time a plotted point is not exactly on the centerline, even when no chart rule is violated. The most likely result is:","o":["A reduction in common cause variation","An increase in process variation (overadjustment)","Detection of an assignable cause","A permanently narrower process capability"],"a":1,"x":["Reacting to common-cause noise = overadjustment/overcontrol more variation.","DNA: C and D are the \"improvement\" framings used to lure you toward a positive-sounding answer.","Ref: lines 106–107, 150–152, 1106–1110."]},{"id":"c22q5","ch":22,"n":5,"q":"Which statement is TRUE of a process in a state of statistical control?","o":["All product produced will meet specification limits","Only random (common) causes are present, but products may or may not meet specifications","The process variation reflects the voice of the customer","No further improvement to the process is possible"],"a":1,"x":["In control ≠ conforming. A not-in-control process can still make good parts; an in-control one can make bad parts.","DNA: A is the single most common SPC misconception ASQ exploits; C swaps voice-of-process for voice-of-customer.","Ref: lines 274–276."]},{"id":"c22q6","ch":22,"n":6,"q":"Specification limits and process (control) limits differ in that:","o":["Specification limits reflect the voice of the process; control limits reflect the voice of the customer","Specification limits reflect the voice of the customer; process variation reflects the voice of the process","Both reflect the voice of the customer","Control limits are, by definition, always set inside the specification limits"],"a":1,"x":["Spec limits = voice of the customer; process variation = voice of the process.","DNA: A is the exact reversal — the highest-yield \"close-difference\" trap on this topic.","Ref: lines 128–129."]},{"id":"c22q7","ch":22,"n":7,"q":"All of the following are stated objectives of SPC EXCEPT:","o":["Monitoring processes in real time","Centering the process","Guaranteeing that 100% of output meets specification","Determining when and when not to take action on the process"],"a":2,"x":["SPC never \"guarantees\" conformance (ties to Q5). A, B, D are listed objectives.","DNA: EXCEPT stem; the absolute word \"guaranteeing/100%\" is the tell.","Ref: \"Objectives and Benefits,\" lines 82–95."]},{"id":"c22q8","ch":22,"n":8,"q":"Which of the following is NOT listed as an advantage of control charts?","o":["Data can be collected at the process by the operator","They provide consistency between operators, shifts, or facilities","They help determine whether problems require local or management action","They eliminate the need to establish specification limits"],"a":3,"x":["Control charts do not replace spec limits. A, B, C are the listed advantages.","DNA: negative stem; the false option contradicts the in-control≠spec principle.","Ref: \"Advantages of Control Charts,\" lines 388–393."]},{"id":"c22q9","ch":22,"n":9,"q":"Per the chapter's supplemental definitions, an \"assignable cause\" is best described as a change caused by:","o":["The interplay of multiple minor variables in the process","The behavior of a major variable (or a new major variable) in the process","Variation inherent to the process that the operator cannot remove","The 99.72% of points expected to fall within ±3σ"],"a":1,"x":["Assignable = a major variable's behavior (or a new one).","DNA: A defines the chance/unassignable cause (multiple minor variables) — the deliberate near-twin.","Ref: \"Supplemental Definitions,\" lines 42–45."]},{"id":"c22q10","ch":22,"n":10,"q":"A team counts surface defects (bubbles, scratches, chips) on glass panes; each pane may contain several defects, and the inspected sample size varies between subgroups. The appropriate chart is:","o":["p-chart","np-chart","c-chart","u-chart"],"a":3,"x":["Defects (multiple per unit) + varying sample size = u.","DNA: distractor c-chart is correct only for constant size; classic 2×2 trap.","Ref: \"Control Charts for Attributes,\" lines 877–878; u-chart, 1004–1010."]},{"id":"c22q11","ch":22,"n":11,"q":"Packages of exactly 1,000 light bulbs are tested, and the number of bulbs that fail to light is recorded per package. Each bulb is simply \"lights\" or \"does not light.\" The most appropriate chart is:","o":["p-chart","np-chart","c-chart","u-chart"],"a":1,"x":["Defectives (good/bad units) + constant size (n = 1,000) = np.","DNA: p-chart is the lure (also defectives, but for varying size).","Ref: lines 875–876, 948–954."]},{"id":"c22q12","ch":22,"n":12,"q":"In attributes charting, the key distinction between a \"defective\" and a \"defect\" is that:","o":["A defect makes the whole unit unusable, while a defective is only a minor flaw","\"Defectives\" are counted when each item is classified good or bad; \"defects\" are counted when a single item may have several flaws","Defects are plotted on p- and np-charts; defectives are plotted on c- and u-charts","There is no practical difference between the terms"],"a":1,"x":["Defective = bad unit; defect = a flaw (a unit can hold several).","DNA: C reverses the chart families (p/np = defectives; c/u = defects).","Ref: lines 834–836, 866–878."]},{"id":"c22q13","ch":22,"n":13,"q":"A destructive, expensive test yields only one measurement per batch. The appropriate control chart is:","o":["X̄ and R chart","X̄ and s chart","Individuals and moving range (ImR/XmR) chart","Median chart"],"a":2,"x":["Sample size of one individuals & moving range.","DNA: A/B require subgroups > 1; D is for outliers, not n = 1.","Ref: lines 754–757."]},{"id":"c22q14","ch":22,"n":14,"q":"On an individuals and moving range (ImR) chart, the moving range for each point is calculated as:","o":["The largest value in the subgroup minus the smallest value","The standard deviation of all individual readings to date","The absolute value of the difference between each measurement and the previous one","The average of the two most recent individual readings"],"a":2,"x":["Moving range = |current − previous|.","DNA: A is the subgroup range definition; B is sigma. The chart \"sample size\" is 2 because two consecutive points form each MR.","Ref: lines 759–760, 800–801."]},{"id":"c22q15","ch":22,"n":15,"q":"A median control chart is the preferred variables chart when:","o":["The sample size must be one","Outliers are expected in the samples","Only attribute (pass/fail) data are available","The sample size exceeds 200"],"a":1,"x":["Median charts plot the median (robust to outliers) and connect middle points; range gage checks the range.","DNA: A points to ImR; D is an attributes-size figure.","Ref: \"Median Control Charts,\" lines 810–819."]},{"id":"c22q16","ch":22,"n":16,"q":"How does the X̄ and s chart differ from the X̄ and R chart?","o":["The s chart plots the range; the R chart plots the standard deviation","The s value is calculated from all values in the subgroup, whereas the range uses only the largest and smallest values","The X̄ and s chart can only be used for attribute data","The X̄ and s chart requires a sample size of one"],"a":1,"x":["s uses all values; range uses only max − min (they converge for n ≤ 5).","DNA: A is the exact swap; C/D are category errors.","Ref: lines 695–704."]},{"id":"c22q17","ch":22,"n":17,"q":"The central principle of rational subgrouping is to select subgroups so that:","o":["Variation within a subgroup is large and variation between subgroups is small","Variation within a subgroup is small (homogeneous), allowing meaningful differences to appear between subgroups","Each subgroup contains at least 50 individual readings","Subgroup sizes vary by no more than 20 percent"],"a":1,"x":["Homogeneous within, differentiable between.","DNA: A is the reversal; C/D are attributes-sizing and ±20% recalculation facts dropped in as true-but-irrelevant distractors.","Ref: lines 438–439, 454–456, 504–505."]},{"id":"c22q18","ch":22,"n":18,"q":"In an X̄ and R chart built with rational subgroups, the intended roles are:","o":["The R chart detects process shifts; the X̄ chart captures only common cause variation","The X̄ chart detects process shifts; the R chart captures only common cause variation","Both charts detect only special causes","Both charts capture only common cause variation"],"a":1,"x":["X̄ chart catches shifts; R chart should reflect only common-cause within-subgroup variation.","DNA: A inverts the two charts' jobs.","Ref: lines 469–471."]},{"id":"c22q19","ch":22,"n":19,"q":"Attributes control charts, based on the binomial or Poisson distribution, generally require:","o":["Subgroups of two to five sequential pieces","Exactly 25 individual readings","Larger subgroup sizes, on the order of 50 to 200","A sample size of one"],"a":2,"x":["Attributes charts need large subgroups to detect shifts.","DNA: A (2–5) is the variables -chart figure; D is the ImR case.","Ref: lines 465–466, 488–489, 869–870."]},{"id":"c22q20","ch":22,"n":20,"q":"A team treats a point as a special cause and adjusts the process, but the point was actually produced by ordinary common cause variation. This represents:","o":["A Type I error (reacting to a \"special cause\" that has no effect)","A Type II error (failing to address a real special cause)","Correct application of a run rule","Rational subgrouping"],"a":0,"x":["Acting on a \"special cause\" that isn't real = false alarm = Type I (α) = overadjustment.","DNA: A/B is the canonical α-vs-β trap. Source flag: lines 102–103 (used here) correctly equate this false-alarm/overadjustment situation with Type I; however, line 1284 of the same chapter mislabels the identical situation as \"type II.\" That is an internal contradiction in the OCR'd text. For the ASQ exam, hold to the standard convention used in lines 102–105: Type I = false alarm/overadjustment; Type II = missed signal/underadjustment.","Ref: lines 102–103."]},{"id":"c22q21","ch":22,"n":21,"q":"Which scenario describes a Type II error in control charting?","o":["Adjusting the process because of normal piece-to-piece variability","Special causes actually affecting the process are not detected or addressed","Rejecting product that conforms to specification","Recalculating control limits after removing assignable causes"],"a":1,"x":["Failing to detect/act on a real special cause = missed signal = β = underadjustment.","DNA: C describes a spec decision, not a control-chart error.","Ref: lines 104–105."]},{"id":"c22q22","ch":22,"n":22,"q":"The operating characteristic (OC) curve plots the true value of a process parameter against the probability that a single sample will fall within the control limits. It primarily highlights the risk of:","o":["Type I (α) error","Type II (β) error","Sampling error only","Measurement system bias"],"a":1,"x":["The OC curve = probability of accepting H₀ (no change) it characterizes Type II (β) risk and chart detection power.","DNA: A (α) is the standard lure since \"control chart α\" is over-learned.","Ref: \"OC curve,\" lines 288–293."]},{"id":"c22q23","ch":22,"n":23,"q":"The foundations of statistical process control and process behavior (control) charts are credited to:","o":["W. Edwards Deming, at the Ford Motor Company","Walter Shewhart, at Bell Telephone Laboratories, in the 1920s","Joseph Juran, at Western Electric, in the 1950s","Genichi Taguchi, in the 1960s"],"a":1,"x":["Shewhart, Bell Telephone Laboratories, 1920s.","DNA: C (\"Western Electric\") is engineered bait — Western Electric/WE rules are adjacent to Shewhart's work, so it feels right.","Ref: lines 55–57, 246–247."]},{"id":"c22q24","ch":22,"n":24,"q":"As described in the chapter, pre-control (Rath and Strong, for IBM) operates by:","o":["Calculating ±3σ control limits from 25 subgroups","Dividing the tolerance band into a target zone bounded by two cautionary zones, requiring no calculations or charting","Plotting the standard deviation of each subgroup","Counting defects per unit on a per-sample basis"],"a":1,"x":["Target zone + two cautionary zones; no calculations/charting.","DNA: the structure identifies the answer regardless of the date. (Note: the chapter states \"1953,\" whereas pre-control is more commonly attributed to Rath & Strong ca. 1954 — answer keyed to chapter structure, not the date.)","Ref: lines 68–73."]},{"id":"c22q25","ch":22,"n":25,"q":"According to the chapter, the term \"process behavior chart\" is preferred over \"control chart\" in order to emphasize that:","o":["The chart controls the operator's actions","The focus should be on studying the process, not the people","The chart guarantees conformance to specification","Behavior charts replace the need for a process log"],"a":1,"x":["Renamed to stress studying the process, not blaming people.","DNA: A is a tempting literal-but-wrong reading of \"control.\"","Ref: lines 241–243."]},{"id":"c22q26","ch":22,"n":26,"q":"Control limits are conventionally placed at ±3 standard deviations from the average. For a normal distribution, these limits are expected to enclose approximately what percentage of plotted points?","o":["68.27%","95.45%","99.72%","100%"],"a":2,"x":["±3σ encloses ≈ 99.72% (chapter's figure; conventionally rounded to 99.73%).","DNA: A (±1σ, 68.27%) and B (±2σ, 95.45%) are the empirical-rule decoys; D (\"100%\") is the absolutist trap.","Ref: lines 1096–1099 (also ±3σ at line 285)."]},{"id":"c22q27","ch":22,"n":27,"q":"After a nonrandom pattern reveals a special cause (e.g., a novice operator), the chapter recommends that the points associated with the special cause be:","o":["Left in place so the limits remain conservative","Excluded when recalculating control limits, which should consequently become more restrictive","Doubled in weight to emphasize the disturbance","Converted to attribute data"],"a":1,"x":["Exclude special-cause points and recompute tighter (more restrictive) limits.","DNA: A sounds prudent but is wrong; leaving the points inflates the limits.","Ref: lines 1268–1271."]},{"id":"c22q28","ch":22,"n":28,"q":"When the formula for the lower control limit (LCL) of a p-, np-, c-, or u-chart yields a negative number, the correct action is to:","o":["Set the LCL equal to the UCL","Use no lower control limit","Recompute using ±2σ instead of ±3σ","Switch to a variables chart"],"a":1,"x":["Negative LCL no LCL (stated for p, np, u, and c).","DNA: all distractors are \"fixes\" that violate the rule. This appears four times in the chapter — high-probability item.","Ref: lines 907, 971, 1034, 1055."]},{"id":"c22q29","ch":22,"n":29,"q":"Using the eight rules employed by Minitab to detect special causes, which pattern is a valid out-of-control signal?","o":["Seven points in a row on the same side of the centerline","Nine points in a row on the same side of the centerline","Three points in a row all increasing","Ten points in a row alternating up and down"],"a":1,"x":["Minitab's run rule = 9 on one side.","DNA: A (\"seven\") is the AIAG rule and the chapter's own \"run violation\" (lines 1122, 1157) — the dominant trap because the chapter gives both counts. C should be 6 increasing (line 1149); D should be 14 alternating (line 1150). Source flag: the chapter lists three run thresholds — 7 (its \"Control Chart Interpretation\" list and AIAG) and 9 (Minitab). On the exam, read the stem for which framework is named.","Ref: Minitab rules, lines 1146–1154; AIAG rules + chapter list, lines 1122–1124, 1155–1164."]},{"id":"c22q30","ch":22,"n":30,"q":"A team builds an X̄ and R chart with subgroups of n = 4. The grand average (X̿) is 7.126 and the average range (R̄) is 0.0037. Using A₂ = 0.729, the upper control limit for the averages chart (UCL = X̿ + A₂R̄) is closest to:","o":["7.123","7.126","7.129","7.137"],"a":2,"x":["7.126 + (0.729 × 0.0037) = 7.126 + 0.0027 ≈ 7.129.","DNA: A (7.123) is the LCL (subtract instead of add) — the most common arithmetic-direction trap; D is a distractor magnitude.","Ref: worked example, lines 615–620."]},{"id":"c23q1","ch":23,"n":1,"q":"What is the fundamental purpose of a control plan?","o":["To identify the root causes of process variation","To \"hold the gains\" realized through the prior design, measure, analyze, and improve phases","To establish the project charter and define project scope","To calculate the risk priority number for each failure mode"],"a":1,"x":["The control plan's stated purpose is to \"hold the gains\" from the prior phases. A (root cause) is the Analyze phase; C is the Define phase / charter; D is FMEA. This mimics the ASQ habit of populating distractors with correctly-stated functions of other DMAIC tools.","Reference: Lines 32–33, \"The purpose of a control plan is to 'hold the gains' realized through the prior Six Sigma phases of design, measure, analyze, and improve.\""]},{"id":"c23q2","ch":23,"n":2,"q":"Which of the following is NOT listed as basic minimum information that a control plan should contain?","o":["Identification of any gages or test equipment needed for the operations","Appropriate sample sizes and frequencies of all testing","A detailed cost-benefit analysis justifying the process","Reaction plans that can be easily understood by the operators"],"a":2,"x":["Negative stem. A/B/D are all explicitly listed minimum-content items; \"cost-benefit analysis\" is plausible-sounding (it's a real CoQ tool elsewhere in the BoK) but is not a control-plan content element — the classic \"right concept, wrong place\" trap.","Reference: Lines 13–28 (basic minimum information list). Cost-benefit analysis is absent."]},{"id":"c23q3","ch":23,"n":3,"q":"Which element of a control plan enables the process operator to evaluate current performance relative to predetermined control limits?","o":["Control subject","Performance standard","Unit of measure","Decision/deployment"],"a":1,"x":["\"Performance standard\" is defined as enabling evaluation against predetermined control limits. A (control subject = the feature monitored) and C (unit of measure) are adjacent elements from the same list — close-difference distractors testing whether you can separate the standard from the subject.","Reference: Lines 56–57, \"Performance standard. This enables the process operator to evaluate the current performance relative to the predetermined control limits.\""]},{"id":"c23q4","ch":23,"n":4,"q":"An operator on a single line records some part dimensions in inches and others in centimeters on the same chart. According to the chapter, which control plan element has been compromised, risking skewed control charts?","o":["Measurement rules","Unit(s) of measure","Record","Version"],"a":1,"x":["The text ties the inches-vs-centimeters example directly to the Unit(s) of measure element. A (measurement rules: device, frequency, sample size, manner of recording) is the strongest distractor — it sounds like it should govern units, but the chapter assigns the consistency-of-units issue specifically to \"Unit(s) of measure.\"","Reference: Lines 58–61, \"Confusion in units (for example, inches versus centimeters…) can skew control charts if not properly managed.\""]},{"id":"c23q5","ch":23,"n":5,"q":"Which control plan element exists to communicate whether the plan is the \"latest and greatest\" released and currently in use?","o":["Record","Decision/deployment","Version","Control subject"],"a":2,"x":["\"Version\" carries the \"latest and greatest\" language verbatim. A (Record) and B (Decision/deployment) are real elements from the same list, used as same-list distractors.","Reference: Lines 71–72, \"Version. This is necessary to communicate whether the control plan is the 'latest and greatest' released and in use.\""]},{"id":"c23q6","ch":23,"n":6,"q":"The chapter cites Deming's directive to \"fix the process, not the people,\" noting that studies show the real issue is something in the system approximately what percentage of the time?","o":["50 percent","65 percent","80 percent","95 percent"],"a":2,"x":["The text states 80 percent. The distractors (50/65/95) are the standard numeric-decoy pattern ASQ uses for quantitative recall — only one matches the source.","Reference: Lines 99–101, \"Studies have shown that 80 percent of the time the real issue is something in the system.\""]},{"id":"c23q7","ch":23,"n":7,"q":"The scope of control plans includes all of the following forms EXCEPT:","o":["Dynamic control plans","Quality process sheets","Standard operating procedures","Risk priority matrices"],"a":3,"x":["Negative stem. The chapter names dynamic control plans, quality process sheets, and SOPs as the scope of control plans. \"Risk priority matrices\" is FMEA terminology inserted as an off-scope distractor.","Reference: Lines 611–612, \"the scope of control plans includes dynamic control plans, quality process sheets, and standard operating procedures.\""]},{"id":"c23q8","ch":23,"n":8,"q":"Process controls allow operators to support the decision of whether to do what with a running process?","o":["Sort, set-in-order, shine, or standardize it","Start, continue, pause, or stop running it","Plan, do, check, or act on it","Define, measure, analyze, or improve it"],"a":1,"x":["The text uses \"start, continue, pause, or stop.\" A is 5S, C is PDCA, D is DMAIC — three rhyming four-verb decoys drawn from elsewhere in the BoK. This is a high-value trap because all four \"feel\" like control-phase language.","Reference: Lines 632–633, \"support the decision of whether to start, continue, pause, or stop running the process.\""]},{"id":"c23q9","ch":23,"n":9,"q":"An operator discovers that a recent process change has achieved a new, more desirable performance level. According to the chapter, what is the operator's responsibility regarding the SOP?","o":["Wait until the next scheduled audit before altering any documentation","Update all documents relating to that process and have them reviewed by affected functional areas","Discard the SOP, since improved processes no longer require written procedures","Forward the change only to the Quality Director for permanent archiving"],"a":1,"x":["Apply-level scenario. The SOP is a \"living document\"; on a process change the operator updates all related documents and routes them to affected functional areas for review. A/C/D each capture a fragment of correct practice but violate the \"living document + cross-functional review\" requirement.","Reference: Lines 764–770, \"if something changes in the system, then the operator should ensure that the SOP is updated… reviewed by other affected functional areas and technical experts.\""]},{"id":"c23q10","ch":23,"n":10,"q":"Which of the following is NOT given in the chapter as an alternative term for a standard operating procedure (SOP)?","o":["Work instructions","Level three ISO 9001 documentation","Control chart constant","Job aids"],"a":2,"x":["Negative stem. A/B/D are explicit synonyms in the text. \"Control chart constant\" is a real Six Sigma artifact (the kind of thing you'd tab in your binder) but is unrelated to SOP terminology — off-topic decoy.","Reference: Lines 753–754, \"such as work instructions, level three ISO 9001 documentation, operating guides, job aids, or standard job practices.\""]},{"id":"c23q11","ch":23,"n":11,"q":"What is the primary role of documented information in sustaining improvements?","o":["To replace the need for hands-on operator training","To establish that described activities are actually performed and that affected processes and systems are compliant","To eliminate the need for control charts on the shop floor","To serve as the project charter for the next Six Sigma project"],"a":1,"x":["Understand-level. Documented information establishes that activities are performed and systems are compliant. Distractors overstate document control's reach (replacing training, eliminating charts) — ASQ favors distractors that are directionally related but absolute/overreaching.","Reference: Lines 853–854."]},{"id":"c23q12","ch":23,"n":12,"q":"Documents are controlled by having approved releases as of a particular date, authorized by a:","o":["Line operator on the production floor","Recognized authority such as the Quality Director or Plant Manager","Third-party regulatory auditor","Direct customer representative"],"a":1,"x":["The text names \"recognized authority (e.g., Quality Director, Plant Manager).\" A (operator), C (third-party auditor), D (customer) are all real roles in this chapter, placed to test whether you conflate document release authority with audit or operator roles.","Reference: Lines 864–865."]},{"id":"c23q13","ch":23,"n":13,"q":"According to the chapter, a properly controlled document should include a statement warning that:","o":["Printed versions are not controlled, and to reference the online version for the most current revision","All printed copies must be shredded at the end of each shift","Only the Plant Manager is permitted to view printed copies","Printed versions always supersede electronic versions"],"a":0,"x":["Verbatim minimum-content requirement. Distractors B/C/D invert or exaggerate the real rule (printed = uncontrolled), a common ASQ reversal trap.","Reference: Lines 883–884, \"printed versions are not controlled, and to reference the online version for the most current revision.\""]},{"id":"c23q14","ch":23,"n":14,"q":"The chapter describes documents as serving what dual role in an improvement effort?","o":["Inputs and outputs of the process","Causes and effects of variation","Drivers and metrics of performance","Both tools and artifacts of the improvement"],"a":3,"x":["The chapter states documents are \"both tools and artifacts of the improvement.\" A (inputs/outputs), B (causes/effects), C (drivers/metrics) are all genuine Six Sigma paired concepts used as recognition decoys.","Reference: Line 891, \"Documents serve as both tools and artifacts of the improvement.\""]},{"id":"c23q15","ch":23,"n":15,"q":"Records that demonstrate proper process completion primarily represent which of the following?","o":["The accountability and performance (or evidence of non-performance) of workers and their functions","The financial budget allocated to the project","The voice of the customer requirements","The control chart constants for the process"],"a":0,"x":["Records = accountability and performance (or evidence of non-performance). Distractors pull in budget, VOC, and control constants — each a legitimate Six Sigma concept inserted to test discrimination.","Reference: Lines 899–900."]},{"id":"c23q16","ch":23,"n":16,"q":"Per the chapter, training is distinguished from general education by the expectation that the outcome of successful training will result in:","o":["A completion certificate for each attendee","Specific competencies","A passing examination score","A reduced future training budget"],"a":1,"x":["Training \"specific competencies\" is the defining distinction from education. A/C (certificate, exam score) are the measurement traps the chapter explicitly warns against conflating with the outcome.","Reference: Lines 917–918, \"training is distinguished from general education by the expectation that the outcome of successful training will result in specific competencies.\""]},{"id":"c23q17","ch":23,"n":17,"q":"According to the chapter, the success of training intended to sustain Six Sigma improvements is correctly measured by:","o":["The count of certificates awarded","The total time spent in classrooms and webinars","Sustainable changes to behavior that lead to performance improvements","The number of trainees enrolled in the program"],"a":2,"x":["High-value trap. The chapter explicitly rejects certificate counts (A), time spent (B), and enrollment (D) as success measures; success = behavior/performance change. ASQ loves stems where the appealing administrative metrics are the wrong answers.","Reference: Lines 1068–1071, \"The success of training is not measured by the count of certificates nor the time spent in classrooms… The results have to be manifested in sustainable changes to behavior.\""]},{"id":"c23q18","ch":23,"n":18,"q":"The chapter states that the level of training should extend beyond knowledge and comprehension to:","o":["Memorization","Application","Certification","Documentation"],"a":1,"x":["Training must reach \"application.\" A (memorization) is the sub-comprehension decoy; C/D are administrative decoys. Note the parallel to Bloom's levels — application outranks knowledge/comprehension.","Reference: Lines 948–949, \"the level of training should extend beyond knowledge and comprehension to application.\""]},{"id":"c23q19","ch":23,"n":19,"q":"A training needs analysis is prompted by all of the following conditions EXCEPT:","o":["Organizational restructuring","Changes to functional or departmental priorities and objectives","Adjustments to new technologies or process steps","The successful completion of a third-party certification audit"],"a":3,"x":["Negative stem. A/B/C are the three named triggers for a needs analysis. A \"successful third-party audit\" is plausible (audits appear in this very chapter) but is not a needs-analysis trigger — cross-section decoy.","Reference: Lines 962–966."]},{"id":"c23q20","ch":23,"n":20,"q":"Validation of training should extend beyond attendee satisfaction. Which of the following is NOT named as a method of validating training?","o":["Oral or written examinations","Demonstrations","Verification that completed work meets performance expectations","Tallying the number of webinars that were hosted"],"a":3,"x":["Negative stem mirroring Q17's logic — counting webinars is an activity metric, not a validation method. A/B/C are the named validation techniques.","Reference: Lines 1042–1044, \"Validation of training can be achieved through oral and/or written examinations, demonstrations, ongoing monitoring, and the verification that completed work meets performance expectations.\""]},{"id":"c23q21","ch":23,"n":21,"q":"According to the chapter, an audit is best defined as a quality assessment activity intended to:","o":["Reconcile the description of systems, processes, activities, and objective evidence with their actual outcomes","Calculate the process capability indices of a finished product","Generate brainstormed solutions to an open-ended problem","Replace the control plan once a process is stable"],"a":0,"x":["Remember-level definition. The audit reconciles described systems/processes with actual outcomes. B (capability), C (brainstorming/affinity), D (control plan) are correctly-defined other tools — the signature ASQ distractor style.","Reference: Lines 1076–1078."]},{"id":"c23q22","ch":23,"n":22,"q":"A first-party audit is conducted:","o":["Within an organization, between functional areas","By the direct customer on one of its suppliers","By a regulatory or accreditation body","By a government agency enforcing nuclear regulations"],"a":0,"x":["First-party = internal, between functional areas. B = second-party, C/D = third-party. This three-way audit set is a frequently tested close-difference cluster — the distractors are the other two correct definitions.","Reference: Lines 1112–1113, \"First-party audits are conducted within an organization, between functional areas.\""]},{"id":"c23q23","ch":23,"n":23,"q":"A customer audits one of its suppliers as a condition of continued purchases and preservation of the business relationship. This is an example of which audit type?","o":["First-party audit","Second-party audit","Third-party audit","Document audit"],"a":1,"x":["Apply-style framing of a Remember fact: a customer auditing a supplier as a purchase condition = second-party. The \"condition of continued purchases\" phrase is the tell.","Reference: Lines 1128–1129, \"Second-party audits are conducted by external providers (i.e., the direct customer), and are frequently a condition of continued purchases.\""]},{"id":"c23q24","ch":23,"n":24,"q":"Which audit type is conducted by a representative of a regulatory or accreditation body, where there is no direct customer-supplier relationship, often to overcome a barrier of entry to a market?","o":["First-party audit","Second-party audit","Third-party audit","Process audit"],"a":2,"x":["Third-party = regulatory/accreditation body, no direct customer-supplier link, used to clear a market barrier of entry. A/B are the other two audit types; D (\"process audit\") is a category of audit scope, not a party type — a subtle level-confusion decoy.","Reference: Lines 1146–1149."]},{"id":"c23q25","ch":23,"n":25,"q":"In the PDCA cycle as described in the chapter, the \"Plan\" step consists of:","o":["Implementing the chosen solution","Defining a problem and hypothesizing possible causes and solutions","Evaluating the results of the implementation","Standardizing a satisfactory solution"],"a":1,"x":["Plan = define problem + hypothesize causes/solutions. The distractors are the literal Do (A), Check (C), and Act (D) definitions — a pure sequence-discrimination item, exactly how ASQ tests PDCA.","Reference: Line 1211."]},{"id":"c23q26","ch":23,"n":26,"q":"In the PDCA cycle, the \"Check\" step consists of:","o":["Defining the problem","Implementing a solution","Evaluating the results","Returning to the plan step"],"a":2,"x":["Check = evaluate results. Same sequence-discrimination pattern; each distractor is a correct definition of a different PDCA quadrant.","Reference: Line 1213."]},{"id":"c23q27","ch":23,"n":27,"q":"What distinguishes Deming's PDSA model from the Japanese PDCA model?","o":["The \"study\" step compares the results with the initial predictions as process learning","PDSA omits the planning step entirely","PDSA can only be applied to manufacturing processes","PDSA replaces the \"do\" step with a documentation step"],"a":0,"x":["The PDCA-vs-PDSA distinction is a known high-yield trap. Deming's \"Study\" step compares results against initial predictions as process learning. B/C/D are fabricated differences designed to look reasonable.","Reference: Lines 1231–1232 and 1240, \"The 'study' step of the PDSA compares the results with the initial predictions as process learning.\""]},{"id":"c23q28","ch":23,"n":28,"q":"According to the chapter, the \"Act\" step of the PDCA cycle directs the team to:","o":["Hypothesize the root causes of the problem","Evaluate whether the implementation was effective","Return to the plan step if results are unsatisfactory, or standardize the solution if results are satisfactory","Collect baseline data on the control variables"],"a":2,"x":["Act has the dual branch: loop back if unsatisfactory, standardize if satisfactory. A (Plan), B (Check), D (control-plan step 3) are neighboring-step decoys — note D pulls from the control-plan creation steps, a cross-section trap.","Reference: Lines 1214–1215."]},{"id":"c23q29","ch":23,"n":29,"q":"The chapter contrasts PDSA and SDCA. PDSA should be used when reviewing a process for improvement ideas, whereas SDCA should be used to:","o":["Define the project charter and problem statement","Help ensure the process is stable and maintaining the gains of previous improvement efforts","Calculate the risk priority number of failure modes","Conduct a third-party regulatory audit"],"a":1,"x":["PDSA = improvement review; SDCA = stabilize and maintain gains. Source note: the chapter expands SDCA inconsistently (\"act\" on line 626 vs. \"adjust\" on lines 750/813), so I tested SDCA's purpose (stated consistently) rather than the acronym — I'd advise you tab this as a known ambiguity and not rely on the final-letter expansion under exam pressure.","Reference: Lines 1265–1267, \"PDSA should be used when reviewing a process for possible improvement ideas, and SDCA should be used to help ensure that the process is stable and maintaining the gains.\""]},{"id":"c23q30","ch":23,"n":30,"q":"According to the chapter, the PDCA and PDSA models can also be mapped to which two other methodologies?","o":["SIPOC and the Kano model","The Six Sigma DMAIC methodology and AIAG's 8 Discipline (8D) approach","SWOT analysis and the Taguchi loss function","The theory of constraints and value stream mapping"],"a":1,"x":["PDCA/PDSA map to DMAIC and AIAG's 8D. The distractors bundle real but unrelated tool pairs (SIPOC/Kano, SWOT/Taguchi, TOC/VSM) — testing whether you recall the specific cross-mappings the text asserts.","Reference: Lines 1242–1243, \"The PDCA, PDSA models can also be mapped to the Six Sigma DMAIC methodology and AIAG's 8 Discipline approach.\""]},{"id":"c24q1","ch":24,"n":1,"q":"What is the fundamental purpose of total productive maintenance (TPM)?","o":["To replace all preventive maintenance with operator self-repair","To improve maintenance practices and enable the prediction and/or prevention of anticipated failure","To eliminate the need for a dedicated maintenance department","To schedule equipment replacement at fixed calendar intervals"],"a":1,"x":["The chapter opens by defining TPM as improving maintenance practices and enabling prediction/prevention of anticipated failure. Distractor A overstates operator self-repair; C (\"eliminate the maintenance department\") is a tempting overgeneralization the text contradicts (it frees technical staff for higher-level work); D describes time-based replacement, not TPM.","Reference: Lines 11–15 (chapter intro paragraph)."]},{"id":"c24q2","ch":24,"n":2,"q":"TPM evolved from several earlier maintenance approaches. Which of the following is NOT one of the approaches the chapter identifies as part of that evolution?","o":["Breakdown maintenance","Corrective maintenance","Predictive maintenance","Productive maintenance"],"a":2,"x":["Predictive maintenance","High-value trap. The four-stage evolution listed is breakdown corrective preventive productive maintenance. Predictive maintenance appears in the BoK subtext/header (line 7) and is strongly associated with TPM, making it the seductive wrong answer — but it is not in the chapter's evolution list. Mimics the ASQ pattern of placing a true-but-out-of-list term as the distractor.","Reference: Lines 61–70 (\"TPM evolved from…\")."]},{"id":"c24q3","ch":24,"n":3,"q":"Under a TPM program, which task is NOT typically shifted to equipment operators?","o":["Cleaning and lubricating their equipment","Performing simple calibrations and adjustments","Conducting routine inspections","Performing higher-level repairs requiring specialized technical expertise"],"a":3,"x":["Operators inspect, clean, lubricate, adjust, and perform simple calibrations; this frees the technical workforce for higher-level activities requiring expertise. D inverts that relationship — a classic role-reversal distractor.","Reference: Lines 71–74."]},{"id":"c24q4","ch":24,"n":4,"q":"Which of the following is NOT listed as an element of a TPM system?","o":["Data collection system (overall equipment effectiveness)","Training and education","Design of experiments","Operational involvement"],"a":2,"x":["Design of experiments","Listed elements: maintenance infrastructure/goals/objectives; data collection (OEE); training & education; workflow & controls; operational involvement. DOE is a real Six Sigma (Improve-phase) tool dropped in as a credible distractor — the \"correct concept, wrong list\" trap.","Reference: Lines 84–89."]},{"id":"c24q5","ch":24,"n":5,"q":"What is identified as the key metric for TPM?","o":["Mean time to repair (MTTR)","Overall equipment effectiveness (OEE)","First-pass yield","Takt time"],"a":1,"x":["The text explicitly names OEE as the key metric. MTTR (A) and first-pass yield (C) are components/related measures, not the headline metric; takt time (D) is a demand measure from a different chapter.","Reference: Lines 90–91."]},{"id":"c24q6","ch":24,"n":6,"q":"OEE is calculated as the product of which three factors?","o":["Reliability × Maintainability × Availability","Availability × Performance efficiency × Quality rate","Uptime × MTBF × First-pass yield","Availability × Cycle time × Scrap rate"],"a":1,"x":["OEE = Availability × Performance efficiency × Quality rate. Distractors recombine real reliability terms (MTBF, maintainability) to mimic plausible formula confusion.","Reference: Line 96."]},{"id":"c24q7","ch":24,"n":7,"q":"Within OEE, the availability factor is used to track which type of loss?","o":["Speed losses","Quality (defect and rework) losses","Unscheduled downtime losses","Start-up losses"],"a":2,"x":["Availability tracks unscheduled downtime losses. A (speed) belongs to performance; B (quality) belongs to quality — the items deliberately offer the other two OEE factors as distractors.","Reference: Lines 97–98."]},{"id":"c24q8","ch":24,"n":8,"q":"The performance efficiency factor of OEE primarily tracks which type of loss?","o":["Downtime losses","Speed losses","Quality losses","Planned-downtime losses"],"a":1,"x":["Performance is used to track speed losses. A is availability; C is quality. Same three-factor cross-trap structure.","Reference: Lines 104–105."]},{"id":"c24q9","ch":24,"n":9,"q":"The quality factor of OEE is, in simplest terms, equivalent to which measure?","o":["Defects per million opportunities","Process capability index","First-pass yield","Rolled throughput yield across all stations"],"a":2,"x":["The chapter states quality is, in simplest terms, first-pass yield. DPMO (A), Cp/Cpk (B), and RTY (D) are real metrics from other phases — plausible-sounding, but the text equates the quality factor specifically with first-pass yield.","Reference: Lines 261–263."]},{"id":"c24q10","ch":24,"n":10,"q":"Which statement correctly maps the three OEE losses?","o":["Availability = speed loss; Performance = downtime loss; Quality = quality loss","Availability = downtime loss; Performance = speed loss; Quality = quality loss","Availability = quality loss; Performance = speed loss; Quality = downtime loss","Availability = downtime loss; Performance = quality loss; Quality = speed loss"],"a":1,"x":["Direct mapping: availability = downtime loss; performance = speed loss; quality = quality loss. The other options scramble the pairings — ASQ's signature \"shuffle the correct elements into wrong relationships\" item.","Reference: Lines 109–111."]},{"id":"c24q11","ch":24,"n":11,"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the \"Six Big Losses\" a TPM program targets?","o":["Changeovers","Idling and minor stoppages","Transportation","Start-up losses"],"a":2,"x":["Transportation","Six Big Losses = breakdowns, changeovers, idling & minor stoppages, reduced speed, scrap & rework, start-up losses. Transportation is a Tim Wood waste (line 49), not a big loss — the trap mixes two different lists from the same chapter.","Reference: Lines 111–113 (Six Big Losses) vs. lines 47–55 (Tim Wood wastes)."]},{"id":"c24q12","ch":24,"n":12,"q":"A machine is in a state of readiness but is inactive because raw material has not arrived. Which \"big loss\" category best describes this condition?","o":["Reduced speed","Idling","Breakdown","Start-up loss"],"a":1,"x":["Idling","Idling = readiness but inactivity due to outside forces such as lack of raw material. Reduced speed (A) is intentional slowness; breakdown (C) is mechanical failure stopping production. Scenario format mirrors the sample exam's named situations.","Reference: Lines 142–143."]},{"id":"c24q13","ch":24,"n":13,"q":"According to the chapter, a \"minor stoppage\" is best distinguished from a breakdown because it is a machine fault that:","o":["Requires replacement of a major component","Can be corrected in less than a minute","Permanently halts production","Is caused by lack of raw material"],"a":1,"x":["Minor stoppages are machine faults correctable in less than a minute — the precise textual discriminator. D (\"lack of raw material\") describes idling, the adjacent concept, making it the tempting close-difference distractor.","Reference: Lines 143–144."]},{"id":"c24q14","ch":24,"n":14,"q":"A machine is running after a setup change but is not yet producing salable product. This is classified as which big loss?","o":["Reduced speed","Changeover","Start-up loss","Idling"],"a":2,"x":["Start-up loss = machine running but not yet manufacturing salable product. Changeover (B) is the setup itself; the post-setup ramp before good product is the start-up loss — a fine-grained distinction ASQ favors.","Reference: Lines 150–151."]},{"id":"c24q15","ch":24,"n":15,"q":"For the purpose of calculating OEE, \"changeover\" time is best defined as the span between:","o":["The first defect and the last defect in a run","The end of the initial production run and the start of the subsequent production run","Two consecutive preventive maintenance events","Operator shift change and the next break"],"a":1,"x":["For OEE, changeover spans end of the initial run to the start of the subsequent run (alternately: last good product of the predecessor to first good product after changeover). D (\"shift change\") is a different example of changeover context but not the OEE timing definition.","Reference: Lines 134–141."]},{"id":"c24q16","ch":24,"n":16,"q":"Mean time to repair (MTTR) is best interpreted as a measure of:","o":["Overall equipment reliability","The maintenance process's effectiveness in response and correction","The frequency of failures over a calendar year","The percentage of good product produced"],"a":1,"x":["MTTR = average repair time, interpreted as maintenance-process effectiveness (response/correction). A (reliability) is MTBF — the deliberate cross-pairing with the very next concept.","Reference: Lines 116–119."]},{"id":"c24q17","ch":24,"n":17,"q":"Mean time between failures (MTBF) reflects which of the following?","o":["The average duration of each repair","The overall reliability of repairable equipment","The total planned downtime per shift","The speed loss of an operating machine"],"a":1,"x":["MTBF = average time between successive failures of repairable equipment, reflecting reliability. A (repair duration) is MTTR.","Reference: Lines 122–125."]},{"id":"c24q18","ch":24,"n":18,"q":"Which statement correctly distinguishes MTBF from MTTR?","o":["MTBF measures repair duration; MTTR measures reliability","MTBF reflects reliability of repairable equipment; MTTR reflects average repair (response/correction) time","Both measure quality yield, differing only in time units","MTBF applies only to non-repairable items; MTTR applies only to repairable items"],"a":1,"x":["The intended pairing: MTBF reliability of repairable equipment; MTTR average repair/response time. A swaps them; D incorrectly assigns MTBF to non-repairable items (the chapter's MTBF is explicitly for repairable equipment). This is the high-yield \"two confusable acronyms in one stem\" item.","Reference: Lines 116–125."]},{"id":"c24q19","ch":24,"n":19,"q":"In the OEE time hierarchy, planned available time is calculated as:","o":["Potential available time − planned downtime","Potential available time − unplanned downtime","Actual operating time − performance losses","Net operating time × quality rate"],"a":0,"x":["Planned available time = Potential available time − Planned downtime (a stated equation). B substitutes unplanned downtime (which instead yields actual operating time) — a subtle term-swap trap.","Reference: Lines 221–225."]},{"id":"c24q20","ch":24,"n":20,"q":"Which of the following would be classified as planned downtime (and therefore removed from the OEE calculation) rather than an availability loss?","o":["An unexpected equipment failure","Running out of parts mid-run","Scheduled preventive maintenance and lunch breaks","An unplanned changeover delay"],"a":2,"x":["Planned downtime examples include facility closed, scheduled maintenance, lunch breaks, and shutdown when demand is met. A, B, and D are all unplanned downtime losses (availability losses), making them the natural traps.","Reference: Lines 215–220 (planned) vs. 232–236 (unplanned)."]},{"id":"c24q21","ch":24,"n":21,"q":"A press has 1,500 available hours, with 250 hours lost to equipment deficiencies and delays. What is its availability?","o":["16.67%","83.33%","80.00%","98.00%"],"a":1,"x":["(1500 − 250) / 1500 = 83.33%. A inverts the ratio (250/1500 ≈ 16.67%); C and D are the chapter's performance (0.80) and quality (98%) figures planted as distractors — testing whether you read the right factor.","Reference: Lines 271–273."]},{"id":"c24q22","ch":24,"n":22,"q":"Given an availability of 83.33%, a performance efficiency of 0.80, and a quality rate of 98%, what is the approximate overall OEE?","o":["87.1%","80.0%","65.3%","98.0%"],"a":2,"x":["0.8333 × 0.80 × 0.98 ≈ 0.653 = 65.3%. The standout trap is A (87.1%), which would result if you averaged the three factors instead of multiplying them — a known OEE misconception. B and D are individual factors, not the product.","Reference: Lines 271–297 (factors) combined per line 96 (formula)."]},{"id":"c24q23","ch":24,"n":23,"q":"When OEE is adapted to monitor labor-intensive functions such as manual assembly or administrative duties, it is referred to as:","o":["Overall labor effectiveness (OLE)","Overall process yield (OPY)","Labor utilization index (LUI)","Administrative cycle efficiency (ACE)"],"a":0,"x":["OEE adapted to labor-intensive/service work is called overall labor effectiveness (OLE). The other acronyms are fabricated but plausible — ASQ commonly fabricates official-sounding distractor acronyms.","Reference: Lines 298–304."]},{"id":"c24q24","ch":24,"n":24,"q":"What is the primary intent of the visual factory ?","o":["To eliminate the need for written work instructions entirely","To make problems visible, notify employees of current operating conditions, and communicate process goals","To automate all material handling on the production floor","To replace control charts with dashboards"],"a":1,"x":["Visual factory makes problems visible, notifies employees of current conditions, and communicates goals. A overstates (\"eliminate written instructions entirely\") — the text in fact stresses clear work instructions; D is too narrow.","Reference: Lines 313–318."]},{"id":"c24q25","ch":24,"n":25,"q":"A single-point lesson is best described as a visual aid that:","o":["Provides a detailed multi-page written procedure","Relies heavily on visual displays with very little writing to show how a task is performed or inspected","Is a financial dashboard reporting cost of quality","Is a control chart posted at the workstation"],"a":1,"x":["A single-point lesson relies heavily on visual displays with little/no writing, showing how to perform or inspect a task. A directly contradicts the \"very little writing\" defining trait.","Reference: Lines 335–341."]},{"id":"c24q26","ch":24,"n":26,"q":"According to the chapter, a visual workplace is defined as a work environment that is:","o":["Fully automated and operator-free","Self-ordering, self-explaining, self-regulating, and self-improving","Controlled exclusively by the maintenance department","Based solely on spreadsheets and numerical reports"],"a":1,"x":["Verbatim four-part definition: self-ordering, self-explaining, self-regulating, self-improving. D (\"solely spreadsheets/numbers\") is the antithesis the chapter explicitly argues against.","Reference: Lines 373–384."]},{"id":"c24q27","ch":24,"n":27,"q":"The Toyota andon board functions primarily to:","o":["Calculate OEE automatically in real time","Notify a leader, supervisor, or maintenance with a light that pinpoints the process step (sometimes with an audible alarm) when production is interrupted","Trigger reorder of raw materials in the warehouse","Document lessons learned for project closure"],"a":1,"x":["The andon board signals a leader/supervisor/maintenance with a light pinpointing the process step (often with an alarm) when a process stops. A and C describe OEE calculation and kanban respectively — adjacent visual-factory concepts used as traps.","Reference: Lines 521–528."]},{"id":"c24q28","ch":24,"n":28,"q":"On a machine-mounted stack light, what does a yellow light indicate?","o":["Normal manufacturing with no interruption","The operator needs help","Manufacturing has stopped completely","Scheduled maintenance is in progress"],"a":1,"x":["Stack light convention: green = normal, yellow = operator needs help, red = manufacturing stopped. C (\"stopped completely\") is the red-light meaning, the most common confusion the item exploits.","Reference: Lines 532–535."]},{"id":"c24q29","ch":24,"n":29,"q":"Within a pull system, a kanban card serves chiefly as:","o":["A control chart for monitoring process variation","A visual indicator that triggers production of parts/components when they are depleted from inventory","A safety lockout device for high-risk equipment","A document-control watermark"],"a":1,"x":["A kanban card is a visual indicator triggering production/replenishment when inventory is depleted. C describes LOTO; D describes document-control watermarks — both are other visual devices in the same section.","Reference: Lines 545–549."]},{"id":"c24q30","ch":24,"n":30,"q":"Jidoka is best characterized by which of the following?","o":["Fully manual inspection performed only by skilled experts","\"Automation with a human touch\" (autonomation), enabling automated stoppages, in-station quality control, and detection of special cause variation","A scheduling technique that levels production volume (heijunka)","A method for calculating mean time between failures"],"a":1,"x":["Jidoka = \"automation with a human touch\"/autonomation; characteristics include poka-yoke, automatic stops, in-station quality control, and detection of special cause variation. C describes heijunka (level scheduling), a deliberately mismatched lean term.","Reference: Lines 603–618."]}]};
